window.GIQ={"meta":{"fold":1,"tier":1,"uw":"Chapter","lede":"","top":51,"fac":{"n":51,"nsrc":5}},"lectures":[{"id":"PHYS-C2","t":"GI hormones and other messengers"},{"id":"PHYS-C3","t":"Motility"},{"id":"PHYS-C4","t":"Saliva"},{"id":"PHYS-C5","t":"Gastric secretion"},{"id":"PHYS-C6","t":"Pancreatic and biliary secretion"},{"id":"PHYS-C7","t":"Digestion and absorption of carbohydrates and proteins"},{"id":"PHYS-C8","t":"Digestion and absorption of lipids"},{"id":"PHYS-C9","t":"Water, electrolytes, regional absorption, diarrhea and the liver"}],"groups":[{"l":"PHYS-C2","u":0,"label":"Gastrin grows the gastric mucosa","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G040-9.jpg","cap":"Gastrin actions: acid secretion, gastric lining growth, gastric motility","where":"2026 lecture: L51A Hormones, slide 9"},{"src":"../sl/G066-82.jpg","cap":"ZES triad: excess gastrin thickens mucosa, inactivates lipase, causes steatorrhea","where":"2024 TA review, slide 82"}],"q":[{"id":"D022-7-1","s":"A 51 yo male complains about an oily anal leakage and his family adds that he has especially foul-smelling feces. Upon examination the patient exhibits hypertrophy of the gastric mucosa. Genetic analysis shows a point mutation in a GI peptide that confers a 10-fold hyperactivity. Which peptide has the mutation?","o":["a. CCK","b. Gastrin","c. GIP","d. Histamine","e. Secretin"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Gastrin","a":"b","why":"<b>B. Gastrin.</b> Too much gastrin causes <b>gastric mucosal hypertrophy</b> (its trophic effect) and too much acid. Acid in the duodenum <b>inactivates pancreatic lipase</b>, so fat is not digested: oily, foul stools (steatorrhea).<br>A. Excess cholecystokinin (CCK) would help fat digestion and does not grow the stomach.<br>C. Glucose-dependent insulinotropic peptide (GIP): raises insulin and lowers acid.<br>D. Histamine: raises acid but has no trophic effect.<br>E. Secretin: lowers acid and blocks gastrin's growth effect.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L51A slide 9 (Gastrin)","L53 slide 46 (Zollinger Ellison Syndrome)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown discussion","n":"Week 14a discussion session (L49-51)","sl":18,"f":"G070"},{"t":"2026 Middletown quiz","n":"Week 14a Canvas quiz Fall 2026 (GI hormones)","sl":3,"f":"G103"},{"t":"2025 Middletown clicker","n":"2025 clickers (image slides)","sl":7,"f":"D022"},{"t":"2024 Middletown clicker","n":"Physio 2 E1 clickers merged","sl":97,"f":"D004"},{"t":"2022 Middletown discussion","n":"week 2 discussion MT","sl":12,"f":"D063"},{"t":"2025 clicker","n":"Fall 2025 week 14 clickers L49-51 (image PDF)","sl":46,"f":"D019"},{"t":"2023 quiz","n":"Physio II exam 1 Canvas quizzes (image slides)","sl":20,"f":"D016"}],"tier":1,"n":"1"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C2","u":1,"label":"CCK releases pancreatic enzymes","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G040-11.jpg","cap":"CCK actions include pancreatic enzyme release: lipase, amylase, trypsin","where":"2026 lecture: L51A Hormones, slide 11"},{"src":"../sl/G044-24.jpg","cap":"Pancreas: CCK acts at acini (enzymes); secretin acts at ducts (bicarbonate)","where":"2026 lecture: L54 Pancreatic & Biliary, slide 24"}],"q":[{"id":"D004-134-1","s":"Which of the following is induced by CCK;","o":["A. Inhibition of bicarbonate secretion rich pancreatic Juice","B. Increased rate of gastric emptying","C. Potentiate secretin action to increase pancreatic enzymes","D. Potentiate secretin action to increase pancreatic secretion"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Potentiate secretin action to increase pancreatic secretion","a":"D","why":"<b>D.</b> Cholecystokinin (CCK) <b>potentiates secretin</b> on pancreatic duct cells, so more bicarbonate-rich pancreatic fluid is secreted.<br>A. CCK increases, not inhibits, bicarbonate-rich juice.<br>B. CCK slows gastric emptying.<br>C. Enzyme release is CCK's own direct action on acinar cells; secretin acts on duct cells for bicarbonate, so 'potentiate secretin to increase enzymes' is wrong.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L51A slide 11 (Actions of CCK)","L54 slide 24 (Regulation of Pancreas)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown discussion","n":"Week 14a discussion session (L49-51)","sl":56,"f":"G070"},{"t":"2024 Middletown clicker","n":"Physio 2 E1 clickers merged","sl":134,"f":"D004"},{"t":"2022 Middletown discussion","n":"week 2 discussion MT","sl":45,"f":"D063"}],"tier":1,"n":"2"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C2","u":1,"label":"CCK is the main brake on gastric emptying","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G040-11.jpg","cap":"CCK actions end with 'Inhibits Gastric Emptying'","where":"2026 lecture: L51A Hormones, slide 11"},{"src":"../sl/G039-28.jpg","cap":"Duodenal fat, acid, stretch and osmolarity slow gastric emptying","where":"2026 lecture: L50 Motility, slide 28"},{"src":"../sl/D012-17.jpg","cap":"CCK summary: stimulated by fat, slows gastric emptying, releases enzymes","where":"2022 Middletown TA weekly, slide 17"}],"q":[{"id":"D037-41-1","s":"Which of the following hormones are released by the presence of protein and fat in the small intestine and has a major effect to decrease gastric emptying?","o":["A. Cholecystokinin (CCK)","B. Glucose dependent insulinotropic peptide (GLIP)","C. Gastrin","D. Motilin","E. Secretin"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Cholecystokinin (CCK)","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Cholecystokinin (CCK).</b> Fatty acids, monoglycerides, small peptides and amino acids in the small intestine release CCK from I cells. CCK is the <b>strongest inhibitor of gastric emptying</b>, giving time for fat digestion.<br>B. Glucose-dependent insulinotropic peptide (GIP): released by all nutrients; its main job is insulin.<br>C. Gastrin: released in the stomach; it increases gastric motility.<br>D. Motilin: released during fasting; it drives the migrating myoelectric complex.<br>E. Secretin: released by acid; its main job is bicarbonate.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L51A slide 10 (Cholecystokinin)","L51A slide 11 (Actions of CCK)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown discussion","n":"Week 14a discussion session (L49-51)","sl":13,"f":"G070"},{"t":"2025 TA weekly","n":"Physio GI weeks 1 & 2","sl":41,"f":"D037"},{"t":"2025 Middletown clicker","n":"2025 clickers (image slides)","sl":5,"f":"D022"},{"t":"2024 Middletown clicker","n":"Physio 2 E1 clickers merged","sl":92,"f":"D004"}],"tier":1,"n":"3"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C2","u":2,"label":"CCK contracts gallbladder, opens Oddi","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G040-11.jpg","cap":"CCK: gallbladder contraction and sphincter of Oddi relaxation","where":"2026 lecture: L51A Hormones, slide 11"},{"src":"../sl/D040-126.jpg","cap":"Bile: fat releases CCK from I cells; gallbladder contracts, Oddi relaxes","where":"2023 TA review, slide 126"}],"q":[{"id":"D022-8-1","s":"A 40-year-old obese pregnant woman presents to an ED physician with excruciating right upper quadrant pain, nausea, vomiting, perspiration, and uneasiness for the last 2 hours. The patient states that she had a whole pizza with extra cheese ~ 3-hours ago. Radioimaging and ultrasound show gallstones in the common bile duct. Diagnosis of acute cholecystitis is made. Which of the following stimuli will exacerbate the pain?","o":["a. CCK","b. Gastrin","c. Glucose-dependent insulinotropic peptide (GIP)","d. Secretin","e. Somatostatin"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"CCK","a":"a","why":"<b>A. Cholecystokinin (CCK).</b> The fatty pizza releases CCK, which <b>contracts the gallbladder</b> against the stone in the common bile duct and worsens the pain.<br>B. Gastrin: acts on the stomach.<br>C. Glucose-dependent insulinotropic peptide (GIP): raises insulin.<br>D. Secretin: raises biliary bicarbonate and water but does not contract the gallbladder.<br>E. Somatostatin: inhibits gallbladder contraction, so it would ease the pain.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L51A slide 11 (Actions of CCK)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown discussion","n":"Week 14a discussion session (L49-51)","sl":25,"f":"G070"},{"t":"2026 Middletown quiz","n":"Week 14a Canvas quiz Fall 2026 (GI hormones)","sl":3,"f":"G103"},{"t":"2025 Middletown clicker","n":"2025 clickers (image slides)","sl":8,"f":"D022"},{"t":"2024 Middletown clicker","n":"Physio 2 E1 clickers merged","sl":101,"f":"D004"},{"t":"2022 Middletown discussion","n":"week 2 discussion MT","sl":16,"f":"D063"},{"t":"2023 quiz","n":"Physio II exam 1 Canvas quizzes (image slides)","sl":22,"f":"D016"}],"tier":1,"n":"4"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C2","u":3,"label":"GIP responds to glucose, amino and fatty acids","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G040-14.jpg","cap":"GIP stimuli: carbohydrates, amino acids and fatty acids ('Food!')","where":"2026 lecture: L51A Hormones, slide 14"},{"src":"../sl/D061-42.jpg","cap":"Costanzo table: GIP released by fatty acids, amino acids and oral glucose","where":"2023 TA review, slide 42"}],"q":[{"id":"D022-6-1","s":"A 10 yo girl consumes a cheeseburger, fries and a chocolate shake. The meal stimulates the release of several GI hormones. The presence of fat, carbohydrate OR protein in the duodenum stimulates the release of which hormone from the duodenal mucosa in this case?","o":["a. CCK","b. Gastrin","c. GIP","d. Motilin","e. Secretin"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"GIP","a":"c","why":"<b>C. Glucose-dependent insulinotropic peptide (GIP)</b>, from K cells of the duodenum and jejunum, is the <b>only gut hormone released by all three nutrients</b>: carbohydrate, protein (amino acids) and fat.<br>A. Cholecystokinin (CCK): fat and protein only.<br>B. Gastrin: protein and stretch in the stomach, not the duodenum.<br>D. Motilin: released during fasting and inhibited by food.<br>E. Secretin: acid and fatty acids.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L51A slide 14 (Glucose Dependent Insulinotropic Peptide (GIP))"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown discussion","n":"Week 14a discussion session (L49-51)","sl":15,"f":"G070"},{"t":"2026 Middletown quiz","n":"Week 14a Canvas quiz Fall 2026 (GI hormones)","sl":2,"f":"G103"},{"t":"2025 Middletown clicker","n":"2025 clickers (image slides)","sl":6,"f":"D022"},{"t":"2024 Middletown clicker","n":"Physio 2 E1 clickers merged","sl":94,"f":"D004"},{"t":"2022 Middletown discussion","n":"week 2 discussion MT","sl":9,"f":"D063"},{"t":"2023 quiz","n":"Physio II exam 1 Canvas quizzes (image slides)","sl":16,"f":"D016"}],"tier":1,"n":"5"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C2","u":3,"label":"Oral glucose releases GIP, more insulin","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G029-14.jpg","cap":"Graph: oral glucose raises insulin more than IV glucose; GIP and GLP-1 incretins","where":"2026 lecture: L33 Insulin & Glucagon, slide 14"},{"src":"../sl/G041-3.jpg","cap":"GIP: K-cell incretin that increases insulin release from the pancreas","where":"2026 lecture: L51B Hormones, slide 3"},{"src":"../sl/G040-15.jpg","cap":"GIP actions: increases insulin release, inhibits gastric motility and acid","where":"2026 lecture: L51A Hormones, slide 15"}],"q":[{"id":"D004-122-1","s":"In an experiment, one group of subjects is given 50 grams of glucose intravenously and another is given 50 grams of glucose orally. Which factor can explain why the oral glucose load is cleared from the blood at a faster rate compared to the IV glucose load?","o":["A. CCK-induced insulin release","B. CCK-induced vasoactive intestinal peptide release","C. GIP-induced glucagon release","D. GIP-induced insulin release","E. VIP-induced GIP release"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"GIP-induced insulin release","a":"D","why":"<b>D.</b> Oral glucose releases <b>glucose-dependent insulinotropic peptide (GIP)</b> from K cells; GIP <b>adds extra insulin</b>, so oral glucose is cleared faster. Intravenous (IV) glucose acts only directly on beta cells.<br>A. Cholecystokinin (CCK) raises insulin only slightly, and glucose barely releases CCK.<br>B. CCK does not release vasoactive intestinal peptide (VIP).<br>C. GIP raises insulin, not glucagon.<br>E. VIP does not release GIP.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L51B slide 3 (Glucose Dependent Insulinotropic Peptide (GIP))","L51A slide 15 (Glucose Dependent Insulinotropic Peptide (GIP))"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown discussion","n":"Week 14a discussion session (L49-51)","sl":46,"f":"G070"},{"t":"2026 Middletown quiz","n":"Week 14a Canvas quiz Fall 2026 (GI hormones)","sl":2,"f":"G103"},{"t":"2024 Middletown clicker","n":"Physio 2 E1 clickers merged","sl":122,"f":"D004"},{"t":"2022 Middletown discussion","n":"week 2 discussion MT","sl":34,"f":"D063"},{"t":"2025 Middletown clicker","n":"2025 clickers (image slides)","sl":14,"f":"D022"},{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Physio GI exam review 2025","sl":60,"f":"G067"},{"t":"2023 quiz","n":"Physio II exam 1 Canvas quizzes (image slides)","sl":18,"f":"D016"}],"tier":1,"n":"6"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C2","u":4,"label":"Acid, sugar, fat, stretch: secretin, GIP, CCK, gastrin","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G040-12.jpg","cap":"Secretin is released by acid in the duodenum","where":"2026 lecture: L51A Hormones, slide 12"},{"src":"../sl/G040-14.jpg","cap":"GIP is released by carbohydrates, amino acids and fatty acids","where":"2026 lecture: L51A Hormones, slide 14"},{"src":"../sl/D061-42.jpg","cap":"Costanzo table: stimuli for gastrin, CCK, secretin and GIP side by side","where":"2023 TA review, slide 42"}],"q":[{"id":"D022-4-1","s":"Acid, sugar, fat and distension are the most potent stimuli, respectively, for the release of which hormones?","o":["a. Gastrin, CCK, Motilin, Secretin","b. Gastrin, GIP, CCK, Secretin","c. Histamine, GIP, Motilin, CCK","d. Secretin, GIP, CCK, Gastrin","e. Secretin, CCK, GIP, Gastrin"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Secretin, GIP, CCK, Gastrin","a":"d","why":"<b>D.</b> <b>Acid → secretin</b> (S cells); <b>sugar → glucose-dependent insulinotropic peptide (GIP)</b> (K cells); <b>fat → cholecystokinin (CCK)</b> (I cells); <b>distention → gastrin</b> (G cells).<br>A. Puts gastrin first, but acid inhibits gastrin.<br>B. Same error: gastrin for acid, and secretin for distention.<br>C. Histamine is not a hormone, and motilin does not respond to fat.<br>E. Swaps GIP and CCK: sugar releases GIP and fat releases CCK.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L51B slide 27 (Q5. Which of the following is stimulated most by acid?)","L51B slide 31 (Q7. Which of the following is stimulated most by fat?)","L51B slide 35 (Q9. Which of the following is the major stimulant to the release of gastrin?)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown discussion","n":"Week 14a discussion session (L49-51)","sl":4,"f":"G070"},{"t":"2026 Middletown quiz","n":"Week 14a Canvas quiz Fall 2026 (GI hormones)","sl":1,"f":"G103"},{"t":"2025 Middletown clicker","n":"2025 clickers (image slides)","sl":4,"f":"D022"},{"t":"2024 Middletown clicker","n":"Physio 2 E1 clickers merged","sl":86,"f":"D004"},{"t":"2022 Middletown discussion","n":"week 2 discussion MT","sl":4,"f":"D063"},{"t":"2023 quiz","n":"Physio II exam 1 Canvas quizzes (image slides)","sl":14,"f":"D016"}],"tier":1,"n":"7"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C3","u":5,"label":"Nerves change force, not slow-wave frequency","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G039-27.jpg","cap":"Vagal stimulation increases force, not frequency; gastric rhythm 3 per minute","where":"2026 lecture: L50 Motility, slide 27"},{"src":"../sl/G048-13.jpg","cap":"Slow-wave frequency not affected by nerves, but contraction strength is","where":"2025 discussion, slide 13"}],"q":[{"id":"D022-9-1","s":"Which of the following is true of the basic electrical rhythm (slow waves) in the GI tract?","o":["a. It is faster (i.e., higher frequency) in the stomach than the duodenum","b. It is increased in frequency by ACh","c. It is inhibited by GIP in the ileum","d. It is inhibited in the small intestines by CCK","e. None of the above"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"None of the above","a":"e","why":"<b>E. None of the above.</b> The <b>basic electrical rhythm</b> (slow waves) is set by the ICC (interstitial cells of Cajal). Nerves and hormones change the <b>force</b> of contraction, <b>not the slow-wave frequency</b>.<br>A. False: the stomach (three per minute) is slower than the duodenum (twelve per minute).<br>B. False: ACh (acetylcholine) increases force, not frequency.<br>C. False: GIP (glucose-dependent insulinotropic peptide) does not inhibit the rhythm.<br>D. False: CCK (cholecystokinin) does not inhibit the rhythm.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L50 slide 6 (Slow Waves)","L50 slide 27 (Gastric Motility)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown discussion","n":"Week 14a discussion session (L49-51)","sl":29,"f":"G070"},{"t":"2025 Middletown clicker","n":"2025 clickers (image slides)","sl":9,"f":"D022"},{"t":"2025 Middletown TA weekly","n":"MT TA review week 2 (GI)","sl":23,"f":"D033"},{"t":"2024 Middletown clicker","n":"Physio 2 E1 clickers merged","sl":105,"f":"D004"},{"t":"2022 Middletown discussion","n":"week 2 discussion MT","sl":20,"f":"D063"}],"tier":1,"n":"8"},{"id":"D022-10-1","s":"What will happen to slow waves frequency and smooth muscle contractions in duodenum if norepinephrine is added to the vicinity of interstitial cells of Cajal.","o":["a. Slow wave frequency will increase, and smooth muscle contractions will be same","b. Slow wave frequency will decrease, and smooth muscle contractions will be decreased as well","c. Both are not affected by norepinephrine release","d. Slow wave frequency will not change, and smooth muscle contractions will be decreased"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Slow wave frequency will not change, and smooth muscle contractions will be decreased","a":"d","why":"<b>D. Slow-wave frequency unchanged; contractions decreased.</b> The slow-wave rate is fixed by the ICC (interstitial cells of Cajal). <b>NE (norepinephrine)</b> hyperpolarizes the smooth muscle, so fewer action potentials ride on each wave and <b>contractions weaken</b>.<br>A. Nerves and transmitters do not raise slow-wave frequency.<br>B. Frequency does not fall; only the contractions do.<br>C. Contractions are affected: sympathetic input slows GI (gastrointestinal) motility.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L50 slide 6 (Slow Waves)","L49 slide 26 (Sympathetic)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown discussion","n":"Week 14a discussion session (L49-51)","sl":31,"f":"G070"},{"t":"2025 Middletown clicker","n":"2025 clickers (image slides)","sl":10,"f":"D022"},{"t":"2024 Middletown clicker","n":"Physio 2 E1 clickers merged","sl":107,"f":"D004"}],"tier":1,"n":"9"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C3","u":6,"label":"Primary peristalsis vagal; secondary wave enteric","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G039-10.jpg","cap":"Primary peristalsis follows the swallow; secondary wave is automatic, enteric","where":"2026 lecture: L50 Motility, slide 10"},{"src":"../sl/D033-28.jpg","cap":"Primary wave, then secondary wave starting at the point of distention","where":"2025 Middletown TA weekly, slide 28"}],"q":[{"id":"D022-22-1","s":"After a complete vagus nerve removal, which of the following functions will be fully abolished in the gastrointestinal tract?","o":["a. Gastrin release","b. Histamine release","c. Slow waves","d. Primary peristalsis","e. Swallowing"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Primary peristalsis","a":"d","why":"<b>D. Primary peristalsis.</b> The primary wave follows a swallow and is run by the medullary swallowing center <b>through the vagus</b>, so cutting the vagus abolishes it. The <b>secondary wave</b> is run by the enteric nervous system and can still occur.<br>A. Gastrin: G cells also respond to amino acids and peptides in the stomach.<br>B. Histamine: ECL (enterochromaffin-like) cells still respond to gastrin.<br>C. Slow waves: made by ICC (interstitial cells of Cajal), with no nerves needed.<br>E. Swallowing: it starts voluntarily (tongue, hypoglossal nerve), so it is impaired, not fully lost.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L50 slide 10 (Esophageal Motility)","L50 slide 6 (Slow Waves)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown discussion","n":"Week 14b discussion session (L52-54)","sl":26,"f":"G071"},{"t":"2025 Middletown clicker","n":"2025 clickers (image slides)","sl":22,"f":"D022"}],"tier":1,"n":"10"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C3","u":7,"label":"GERD = LES too loose","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G039-12.jpg","cap":"GERD: sphincter too loose, acid rises causing heartburn; PPIs, pH monitor","where":"2026 lecture: L50 Motility, slide 12"},{"src":"../sl/G039-13.jpg","cap":"Nissen fundoplication wraps the stomach around the esophagus to tighten it","where":"2026 lecture: L50 Motility, slide 13"}],"q":[{"id":"D022-11-1","s":"A 62 yo man who weighs 250 lbs was referred to a gastroenterologist by his primary care doctor after complaining of choking in his sleep, recurrent burning chest pain, and chest discomfort for the last 3 months. His pain was worse after have a large meal, alcohol, spicy food, and coffee and is suppressed by acid inhibitors. His EKG and spirometry findings appear normal. Which of the following is the most likely cause?","o":["a. Decrease in LES tone","b. Failure of the LES to relax","c. Increase in UES tone","d. Inhibition of gastric emptying","e. Inhibition of primary esophageal peristalsis"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Decrease in LES tone","a":"a","why":"<b>A. Decrease in LES tone.</b> In GERD (gastroesophageal reflux disease) the LES (lower esophageal sphincter) is <b>too loose</b>. Acid rises into the esophagus and causes burning chest pain, worse after big meals, alcohol and coffee, and relieved by acid blockers.<br>B. LES failing to relax: achalasia, with dysphagia.<br>C. Higher UES (upper esophageal sphincter) tone: would not cause reflux.<br>D. Slow gastric emptying: can add to reflux, but the main defect is the loose LES.<br>E. Loss of primary peristalsis: causes dysphagia, not acid burning.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L50 slide 12 (Gastroesophageal Reflux)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown discussion","n":"Week 14a discussion session (L49-51)","sl":36,"f":"G070"},{"t":"2025 Middletown clicker","n":"2025 clickers (image slides)","sl":11,"f":"D022"},{"t":"2024 Middletown clicker","n":"Physio 2 E1 clickers merged","sl":112,"f":"D004"},{"t":"2022 Middletown discussion","n":"week 2 discussion MT","sl":26,"f":"D063"}],"tier":1,"n":"11"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C3","u":7,"label":"Achalasia = LES too tight, fails to relax","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G039-14.jpg","cap":"Achalasia: tight sphincter; barium shows dilated esophagus with narrow tip","where":"2026 lecture: L50 Motility, slide 14"},{"src":"../sl/G039-15.jpg","cap":"Dilated esophagus above a tight LES, opened by balloon dilation","where":"2026 lecture: L50 Motility, slide 15"},{"src":"../sl/D004-115.jpg","cap":"Normal manometry: sequential esophageal waves; LES pressure drops after swallow","where":"2024 Middletown clicker, slide 115"}],"q":[{"id":"D004-132-1","s":"A 35-year old man presents with dysphagia (difficulty in swallowing) for solid foods and liquids. Imaging studies exhibit dilation of the proximal esophagus, and a tapering of the distal esophagus. In this scenario, you expect all of the following conditions except;","o":["A. Increased parasympathetic stimulation at LES","B. Persitaltic movement in esophagus is uncoordinated","C. Low-pressure increase in esophagus during swallowing","D. Gastric emptying is slowed"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Increased parasympathetic stimulation at LES","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Increased parasympathetic stimulation is NOT expected.</b> Proximal dilation with a narrow 'bird's beak' tip is <b>achalasia</b>. Inhibitory neurons (VIP, vasoactive intestinal peptide; NO, nitric oxide) are lost, so the LES (lower esophageal sphincter) stays tight. Its nerve input is reduced, not increased.<br>B. Expected: peristalsis is lost or uncoordinated.<br>C. Expected: swallows give weak, simultaneous, low-pressure waves.<br>D. Expected: food held above the LES reaches and clears the stomach late.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L50 slide 14 (Achalasia)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown discussion","n":"Week 14a discussion session (L49-51)","sl":73,"f":"G070"},{"t":"2024 Middletown clicker","n":"Physio 2 E1 clickers merged","sl":132,"f":"D004"},{"t":"2022 Middletown discussion","n":"week 2 discussion MT","sl":47,"f":"D063"}],"tier":1,"n":"12"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C3","u":8,"label":"Stomach stretch releases gastrin","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G039-26.jpg","cap":"Receptive relaxation; stomach needs a sense of stretch to release gastrin","where":"2026 lecture: L50 Motility, slide 26"},{"src":"../sl/G040-8.jpg","cap":"Gastrin stimuli: stomach stretch, protein fragments, vagal ACh and GRP","where":"2026 lecture: L51A Hormones, slide 8"}],"q":[{"id":"D022-15-1","s":"As you eat Thanksgiving dinner, which of the following is most likely occurring?","o":["a. Chemoreceptors are sensing the stretch of the stomach and signaling the brain through the efferent vagus nerve resulting in increased gastrin levels","b. Filling of the stomach results in release of NE into the myenteric plexus increasing muscle contraction","c. PSNS stimulation gradually increases the frequency of contraction of the stomach","d. The muscularis mucosa is primarily responsible for the motility","e. VIP, ACh and gastrin are released"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"VIP, ACh and gastrin are released","a":"e","why":"<b>E. VIP, ACh and gastrin are released.</b> Vagal <b>VIP (vasoactive intestinal peptide)</b> relaxes the stomach to receive the meal. Vagal <b>ACh (acetylcholine)</b> increases contraction force and acid. Stretch and protein release <b>gastrin</b>.<br>A. Stretch is sensed by mechanoreceptors, not chemoreceptors, and travels up afferent, not efferent, vagal fibers.<br>B. NE (norepinephrine) inhibits motility.<br>C. Parasympathetic input raises force, not frequency.<br>D. The muscularis externa moves food; the muscularis mucosae only moves the mucosa.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L50 slide 26 (Gastric Motility)","L50 slide 10 (Esophageal Motility)","L51A slide 8 (Gastrin)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown discussion","n":"Week 14a discussion session (L49-51)","sl":48,"f":"G070"},{"t":"2025 Middletown clicker","n":"2025 clickers (image slides)","sl":15,"f":"D022"},{"t":"2024 Middletown clicker","n":"Physio 2 E1 clickers merged","sl":124,"f":"D004"}],"tier":1,"n":"13"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C3","u":9,"label":"Antral contractions against pylorus = retropulsion","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G039-21.jpg","cap":"Antral wall is thicker than the body; plexuses control the churning","where":"2026 lecture: L50 Motility, slide 21"},{"src":"../sl/G039-27.jpg","cap":"Gastric mixing: only 1 mm particles, 2-3 ml leave per contraction","where":"2026 lecture: L50 Motility, slide 27"},{"src":"../sl/D023-37.jpg","cap":"Retropulsion: antral contractions push chyme backward to slow emptying","where":"2024 Middletown tutor review, slide 37"}],"q":[{"id":"D004-128-1","s":"A 50-year-old man was experiencing problems with his pyloric sphincter and needed a surgery. Which of the following will be affected the most until he heals from the surgery;","o":["A. Receptive relaxation","B. Segmentation","C. Haustrations","D. Retropulsion"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Retropulsion","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Retropulsion.</b> It depends on antral contractions against a <b>closed pylorus</b>, which throw chyme back into the stomach. A damaged pyloric sphincter disturbs it most.<br>A. Receptive relaxation: in the orad stomach, far from the pylorus.<br>B. Segmentation: mixing in the small intestine.<br>C. Haustrations: mixing in the colon's pouches.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L50 slide 27 (Gastric Motility)","L50 slide 21 (Antral wall is thicker than the body)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown discussion","n":"Week 14a discussion session (L49-51)","sl":52,"f":"G070"},{"t":"2024 Middletown clicker","n":"Physio 2 E1 clickers merged","sl":128,"f":"D004"},{"t":"2022 Middletown discussion","n":"week 2 discussion MT","sl":41,"f":"D063"}],"tier":1,"n":"14"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C3","u":10,"label":"Isotonic calorie-free liquid empties fastest","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G039-28.jpg","cap":"Gastric emptying: fat and hypertonic slow; isotonic, no-calorie fluid fastest","where":"2026 lecture: L50 Motility, slide 28"}],"q":[{"id":"D004-130-1","s":"Which of the following order is the best order and right sequence of food contents that will be emptying from stomach;","o":["A. Coke, brownie and Pizza","B. Pizza, brownie and coke","C. Brownie, coke and pizza","D. None of the above"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Coke, brownie and Pizza","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Coke, brownie, pizza.</b> <b>Liquids empty before solids</b>, and <b>fat empties slowest</b>. Coke leaves first, the mostly carbohydrate brownie is next, and the fatty, protein-rich pizza is last.<br>B. Reverse order: puts the fattiest solid first.<br>C. Puts a solid before the liquid.<br>D. None of the above: A follows the rules.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L50 slide 28 (Gastric Emptying)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown discussion","n":"Week 14a discussion session (L49-51)","sl":54,"f":"G070"},{"t":"2024 Middletown clicker","n":"Physio 2 E1 clickers merged","sl":130,"f":"D004"},{"t":"2022 Middletown discussion","n":"week 2 discussion MT","sl":43,"f":"D063"}],"tier":1,"n":"15"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C3","u":11,"label":"Motilin drives the migrating myoelectric complex","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G039-32.jpg","cap":"Migrating myoelectric complex is based on the hormone motilin","where":"2026 lecture: L50 Motility, slide 32"},{"src":"../sl/D007-74.jpg","cap":"MMC mainly in small bowel, controlled by the motilin hormone","where":"2022 Harlem TA review, slide 74"}],"q":[{"id":"D022-12-1","s":"Migrating myoelectric complexes (MMCs) occur about every 90 min between meals and are thought to be mediated by;","o":["a. Duodenal motility","b. Gastric emptying","c. Mass movements","d. Gastrin","e. Motilin"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Motilin","a":"e","why":"<b>E. Motilin.</b> MMCs (migrating myoelectric complexes) are <b>based on motilin</b>, which is released between meals.<br>A. Duodenal motility: part of what the MMC does, not its trigger.<br>B. Gastric emptying: the MMC clears the stomach; emptying is not its cause.<br>C. Mass movements: colon events after meals.<br>D. Gastrin: released after meals.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L50 slide 32 (Migrating Myoelectric Complex)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown discussion","n":"Week 14a discussion session (L49-51)","sl":40,"f":"G070"},{"t":"2025 Middletown clicker","n":"2025 clickers (image slides)","sl":12,"f":"D022"},{"t":"2024 Middletown clicker","n":"Physio 2 E1 clickers merged","sl":116,"f":"D004"},{"t":"2022 Middletown discussion","n":"week 2 discussion MT","sl":24,"f":"D063"}],"tier":1,"n":"16"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C3","u":12,"label":"Colon: frequent haustrations, rare mass movements","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G039-34.jpg","cap":"Colon: segmentation (mixing) and peristaltic contractions, plus mass movements","where":"2026 lecture: L50 Motility, slide 34"}],"q":[{"id":"D022-16-1","s":"Which is the most frequent type of movement in descending colon?","o":["a. Mass movements","b. Retropulsion","c. Haustrations","d. Peristalsis"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Haustrations","a":"c","why":"<b>C. Haustrations.</b> These slow <b>segmenting</b> (mixing) contractions of the colon's pouches occur about every <b>thirty minutes</b>, so they are the most frequent colon movement.<br>A. Mass movements: strong but rare, about one to four times a day.<br>B. Retropulsion: a movement of the stomach's antrum.<br>D. Peristalsis: weak and infrequent in the colon compared with haustral mixing.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L50 slide 34 (Large Bowel)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown discussion","n":"Week 14a discussion session (L49-51)","sl":50,"f":"G070"},{"t":"2025 Middletown clicker","n":"2025 clickers (image slides)","sl":16,"f":"D022"},{"t":"2024 Middletown clicker","n":"Physio 2 E1 clickers merged","sl":126,"f":"D004"},{"t":"2022 Middletown discussion","n":"week 2 discussion MT","sl":39,"f":"D063"}],"tier":1,"n":"17"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C3","u":12,"label":"Gastrocolic reflex: eat, then urge to defecate","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G039-34.jpg","cap":"Large bowel: mass movements; gastrocolic reflex, eat and poop","where":"2026 lecture: L50 Motility, slide 34"},{"src":"../sl/D012-38.jpg","cap":"Gastrocolic reflex: stomach distension increases colon motility and mass movements","where":"2022 Middletown TA weekly, slide 38"}],"q":[{"id":"D022-13-1","s":"A 54-year-old woman eats a healthy meal. Approximately 20 min later the woman feels the urge to defecate. Which of the following reflexes results in the urge to defecate when the stomach is stretched?","o":["a. Duodenocolic reflex","b. Gastrocolic reflex","c. Intestino-intestinal reflex","d. Rectosphincteric"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Gastrocolic reflex","a":"b","why":"<b>B. Gastrocolic reflex.</b> Food stretching the stomach raises colon motility and the frequency of <b>mass movements</b>. These fill the rectum and cause the urge to defecate.<br>A. Duodenocolic reflex: starts in the duodenum, not the stomach.<br>C. Intestinointestinal reflex: overdistention of one segment relaxes the rest.<br>D. Rectosphincteric reflex: rectal filling relaxes the internal sphincter, after stool arrives.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L50 slide 34 (Large Bowel)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown discussion","n":"Week 14a discussion session (L49-51)","sl":44,"f":"G070"},{"t":"2025 Middletown clicker","n":"2025 clickers (image slides)","sl":13,"f":"D022"},{"t":"2024 Middletown clicker","n":"Physio 2 E1 clickers merged","sl":120,"f":"D004"},{"t":"2022 Middletown discussion","n":"week 2 discussion MT","sl":32,"f":"D063"}],"tier":1,"n":"18"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C4","u":13,"label":"Ducts remove NaCl, add potassium and bicarbonate","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G042-12.jpg","cap":"Ductal cells remove sodium and chloride, put bicarbonate and potassium into saliva.","where":"2026 lecture: L52 Saliva, slide 12"},{"src":"../sl/G042-22.jpg","cap":"Saliva vs serum: sodium 15 vs 140, chloride 15 vs 110 mEq/L.","where":"2026 lecture: L52 Saliva, slide 22"},{"src":"../sl/D007-88.jpg","cap":"Costanzo Figures 8.12–8.13: duct ion transport and saliva versus plasma ion levels.","where":"2022 Harlem TA review, slide 88"}],"q":[{"id":"D022-17-1","s":"In the process of making saliva, what is the function of ductal cells in salivary glands:","o":["a. These cells contract to eject saliva in the mouth","b. These cells produce a saliva which has highest relative concentration of potassium as compared to plasma","c. These cells produce initial saliva with relative osmolarity as compared to plasma","d. They add proteolytic enzymes to saliva"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"These cells produce a saliva which has highest relative concentration of potassium as compared to plasma","a":"b","why":"<b>B.</b> <b>Ductal cells</b> secrete potassium, so final saliva has <b>potassium far above plasma</b>; potassium is the ion most concentrated relative to plasma.<br>A. Contracting to eject saliva: myoepithelial cells.<br>C. Making the initial plasma-like (isotonic) saliva: acinar cells.<br>D. Saliva has no protein-digesting enzymes; acinar cells add amylase and lipase.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L52 slide 12 (Ductal Cells/Intercalated Duct:)","L52 slide 11 (Structure of Gland)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown discussion","n":"Week 14b discussion session (L52-54)","sl":5,"f":"G071"},{"t":"2026 Middletown quiz","n":"Week 14b Canvas quiz Fall 2026 (secretions)","sl":1,"f":"G104"},{"t":"2025 Middletown clicker","n":"2025 clickers (image slides)","sl":17,"f":"D022"},{"t":"2024 Middletown clicker","n":"Physio 2 E1 clickers merged","sl":152,"f":"D004"},{"t":"2023 discussion","n":"week 3 discussion","sl":5,"f":"D002"},{"t":"2022 Middletown discussion","n":"week 3 discussion MT","sl":5,"f":"D064"},{"t":"2023 quiz","n":"Physio II exam 1 Canvas quizzes (image slides)","sl":25,"f":"D016"}],"tier":1,"n":"19"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C4","u":14,"label":"Parasympathetic VII/IX, acetylcholine, muscarinic drive saliva","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G042-24.jpg","cap":"Parasympathetic main control: facial and ninth cranial nerves, muscarinic receptors, IP3 raises calcium.","where":"2026 lecture: L52 Saliva, slide 24"},{"src":"../sl/D036-97.jpg","cap":"Costanzo Figure 8.14: cranial nerves seven, nine → acetylcholine → muscarinic receptor → calcium.","where":"2024 TA review, slide 97"}],"q":[{"id":"D022-18-1","s":"Which of the following is correct for autonomic nervous system regulation of salivary secretory rate?","o":["a. Salivary secretory rate is decreased in response to parasympathetic and sympathetic stimulation.","b. Salivary secretory rate is increased in response to parasympathetic input via cranial nerves VII and IX.","c. Salivary secretory rate is significantly increased in response to sympathetic stimulation as compared to parasympathetic stimulation.","d. Salivary secretory rate is increased in response to parasympathetic input via cranial nerve X."],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Salivary secretory rate is increased in response to parasympathetic input via cranial nerves VII and IX.","a":"b","why":"<b>B.</b> Saliva rises with <b>parasympathetic</b> input carried by <b>cranial nerves seven (facial) and nine (glossopharyngeal)</b>; this is the main control.<br>A. Both systems increase, not decrease, saliva.<br>C. Sympathetic input gives a smaller, thicker volume; parasympathetic is dominant.<br>D. Cranial nerve ten (vagus) supplies the gut tube, not the salivary glands.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L52 slide 24 (Neural Control of Salivary Secretion)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown discussion","n":"Week 14b discussion session (L52-54)","sl":10,"f":"G071"},{"t":"2025 Middletown clicker","n":"2025 clickers (image slides)","sl":18,"f":"D022"},{"t":"2024 Middletown clicker","n":"Physio 2 E1 clickers merged","sl":154,"f":"D004"},{"t":"2023 discussion","n":"week 3 discussion","sl":7,"f":"D002"},{"t":"2022 Middletown discussion","n":"week 3 discussion MT","sl":7,"f":"D064"}],"tier":1,"n":"20"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C4","u":14,"label":"Muscarinic blockade (atropine) dries the mouth","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G042-24.jpg","cap":"Parasympathetic acetylcholine is main control; high volume blocked by atropine.","where":"2026 lecture: L52 Saliva, slide 24"},{"src":"../sl/D036-97.jpg","cap":"Costanzo Figure 8.14: atropine blocks the muscarinic receptor on acinar and duct cells.","where":"2024 TA review, slide 97"}],"q":[{"id":"D004-187-1","s":"Which of the following decreases salivary secretion?","o":["A. Acetylcholine","B. Atropine","C. Bradykinin","D. Citric acid","E. Facial nerve (7th cranial) stimulation"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Atropine","a":"B","why":"<b>B.</b> <b>Atropine blocks muscarinic acetylcholine receptors</b>, cutting off the main (parasympathetic) drive, so saliva drops.<br>A. Acetylcholine: the main stimulus of saliva.<br>C. Bradykinin: dilates the gland's blood vessels, which supports secretion.<br>D. Citric acid: the number-one stimulant of saliva.<br>E. Facial nerve (cranial nerve seven) stimulation: parasympathetic, so it raises saliva.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L52 slide 24 (Neural Control of Salivary Secretion)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown discussion","n":"Week 14b discussion session (L52-54)","sl":50,"f":"G071"},{"t":"2024 Middletown clicker","n":"Physio 2 E1 clickers merged","sl":187,"f":"D004"},{"t":"2023 discussion","n":"week 3 discussion","sl":40,"f":"D002"},{"t":"2022 Middletown discussion","n":"week 3 discussion MT","sl":37,"f":"D064"}],"tier":1,"n":"21"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C5","u":15,"label":"Hormone-cell pairs: G, I, S, K, D, ECL","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G041-3.jpg","cap":"GIP is produced by K cells of the duodenum and jejunum","where":"2026 lecture: L51B Hormones, slide 3"},{"src":"../sl/D061-42.jpg","cap":"Costanzo table: gastrin from G cells, CCK from I cells, secretin from S cells","where":"2023 TA review, slide 42"},{"src":"../sl/G043-11.jpg","cap":"Gland table: ECL cells make histamine, D cells somatostatin, G cells gastrin","where":"2026 lecture: L53 Gastric Secretion, slide 11"}],"q":[{"id":"D002-11-1","s":"Which cells type is properly matched with its secretion?","o":["A. Mucous cells – Trypsinogen","B. Chief cell - Intrinsic factor","C. Parietal cell – Gastrin","D. G-cell – Pepsinogen","E. S-cells- Secretin"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"S-cells- Secretin","a":"E","why":"<b>E. S cells → secretin</b> (S cells of the duodenum).<br>A. Mucous cells secrete mucus and bicarbonate; trypsinogen comes from pancreatic acinar cells.<br>B. Chief cells secrete pepsinogen; intrinsic factor comes from parietal cells.<br>C. Parietal cells secrete hydrochloric acid and intrinsic factor; gastrin comes from G cells.<br>D. G cells secrete gastrin; pepsinogen comes from chief cells.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L51A slide 12 (Secretin)","L53 slide 5 (Gastric Mucosa: Structure & Cell types)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown discussion","n":"Week 14b discussion session (L52-54)","sl":14,"f":"G071"},{"t":"2026 Middletown quiz","n":"Week 14b Canvas quiz Fall 2026 (secretions)","sl":2,"f":"G104"},{"t":"2023 discussion","n":"week 3 discussion","sl":11,"f":"D002"},{"t":"2022 Middletown discussion","n":"week 3 discussion MT","sl":11,"f":"D064"},{"t":"2023 quiz","n":"Physio II exam 1 Canvas quizzes (image slides)","sl":27,"f":"D016"}],"tier":1,"n":"22"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C5","u":16,"label":"Alkaline tide: bicarbonate into gastric venous blood","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G043-14.jpg","cap":"Parietal cell: acid to lumen, bicarbonate to blood = alkaline tide","where":"2026 lecture: L53 Gastric Secretion, slide 14"},{"src":"../sl/G043-9.jpg","cap":"Mucosa diagram: bicarbonate made and sent to blood, labeled alkaline tide","where":"2026 lecture: L53 Gastric Secretion, slide 9"},{"src":"../sl/G044-22.jpg","cap":"Pancreatic duct cell: bicarbonate to lumen, H+ out to blood","where":"2026 lecture: L54 Pancreatic & Biliary, slide 22"}],"q":[{"id":"D022-23-1","s":"Which blood sample would likely have the highest pH?","o":["a. Cerebral artery","b. Gastric vein","c. Pancreatic veins","d. Portal vein","e. Renal artery"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Gastric vein","a":"b","why":"<b>B. Gastric vein.</b> Parietal cells send acid (H+) into the lumen and <b>bicarbonate (HCO3-) into the gastric venous blood</b>, the <b>alkaline tide</b>.<br>A. Cerebral artery: ordinary arterial pH, about seven point four.<br>C. Pancreatic veins: duct cells put bicarbonate in the duct and H+ in the blood, so this blood is acidic.<br>D. Portal vein: mixes alkaline gastric blood with acidic pancreatic blood and gut blood, so it is less alkaline.<br>E. Renal artery: ordinary arterial pH.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L53 slide 14 (Carbonic Anhydrase)","L53 slide 9 (Acid activates pepsinogen)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown discussion","n":"Week 14b discussion session (L52-54)","sl":32,"f":"G071"},{"t":"2025 Middletown clicker","n":"2025 clickers (image slides)","sl":23,"f":"D022"},{"t":"2024 Middletown clicker","n":"Physio 2 E1 clickers merged","sl":171,"f":"D004"},{"t":"2023 discussion","n":"week 3 discussion","sl":24,"f":"D002"},{"t":"2022 Middletown discussion","n":"week 3 discussion MT","sl":22,"f":"D064"}],"tier":1,"n":"23"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C5","u":17,"label":"Acid stimulants: ACh, gastrin, histamine","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G043-12.jpg","cap":"Parietal cell with M3, H2, CCKB receptors; stimulators and inhibitors listed","where":"2026 lecture: L53 Gastric Secretion, slide 12"},{"src":"../sl/D061-77.jpg","cap":"Costanzo figure annotated: ACh, gastrin, histamine stimulate; somatostatin, prostaglandins inhibit","where":"2023 TA review, slide 77"}],"q":[{"id":"D022-28-1","s":"Which of the following agents is expected to enhance HCl secretion by acting on parietal cells during a period of stomach distention (allowing enough time for physiological responses to distention to occur)?","o":["a. Acetylcholine","b. Gastrin","c. Histamine","d. A, B and C are correct","e. B and C are correct (but not A)"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"A, B and C are correct","a":"d","why":"<b>D. A, B and C.</b> Stretch triggers vagal reflexes that release <b>acetylcholine (ACh)</b> onto parietal cells. It raises <b>gastrin</b> from antral G cells, and gastrin makes enterochromaffin-like cells release <b>histamine</b>. All three bind parietal-cell receptors: M3 (muscarinic), CCK-B (cholecystokinin B) and H2 (histamine type two).<br>A, B, C alone: each is true but incomplete.<br>E. Leaves out ACh, which distension releases through vagovagal reflexes.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L53 slide 12 (Acid (HCl) Secretion)","L53 slide 18 (H2 Blockers)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown discussion","n":"Week 14b discussion session (L52-54)","sl":48,"f":"G071"},{"t":"2026 Middletown quiz","n":"Week 14b Canvas quiz Fall 2026 (secretions)","sl":3,"f":"G104"},{"t":"2025 Middletown clicker","n":"2025 clickers (image slides)","sl":28,"f":"D022"},{"t":"2024 Middletown clicker","n":"Physio 2 E1 clickers merged","sl":185,"f":"D004"},{"t":"2023 discussion","n":"week 3 discussion","sl":38,"f":"D002"},{"t":"2022 Middletown discussion","n":"week 3 discussion MT","sl":35,"f":"D064"},{"t":"2023 quiz","n":"Physio II exam 1 Canvas quizzes (image slides)","sl":33,"f":"D016"}],"tier":1,"n":"24"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C5","u":18,"label":"NSAIDs remove prostaglandin-driven mucus barrier","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G043-43.jpg","cap":"NSAIDs remove the mucus layer, then acid cooks the lining","where":"2026 lecture: L53 Gastric Secretion, slide 43"},{"src":"../sl/G043-42.jpg","cap":"NSAIDs block COX-1 prostaglandins that protect the gastric mucosa","where":"2026 lecture: L53 Gastric Secretion, slide 42"},{"src":"../sl/D036-113.jpg","cap":"Protective factors (mucus, bicarbonate, prostaglandins, blood flow) versus NSAIDs","where":"2024 TA review, slide 113"}],"q":[{"id":"D022-27-1","s":"An electrophysiologist develops low back pain exacerbated by leaning over microscopes and sudden stretching movements to reach all of the electronic dials and switches. She regularly takes medications for the back pain and develops abdominal pain. What is the mechanism?","o":["a. Decreased mucus production","b. Excess acid secretion","c. Increased acetylcholine","d. Increased prostaglandins","e. Increased ammonia production"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Decreased mucus production","a":"a","why":"<b>A.</b> Her back-pain drug is a <b>nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drug (NSAID)</b>. NSAIDs block cyclooxygenase (COX), so the stomach loses <b>prostaglandin E2</b>, which drives <b>mucus and bicarbonate</b>. Without that barrier, acid 'cooks' the lining.<br>B. Excess acid is not the main problem; normal acid harms once the mucus is gone.<br>C. NSAIDs do not change acetylcholine.<br>D. Backwards: prostaglandins fall.<br>E. Ammonia is Helicobacter pylori's survival trick, not an NSAID effect.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L53 slide 41 (NSAID induced Ulcers)","L53 slide 43 (NSAIDs remove this)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown discussion","n":"Week 14b discussion session (L52-54)","sl":46,"f":"G071"},{"t":"2025 Middletown clicker","n":"2025 clickers (image slides)","sl":27,"f":"D022"},{"t":"2024 Middletown clicker","n":"Physio 2 E1 clickers merged","sl":169,"f":"D004"},{"t":"2023 discussion","n":"week 3 discussion","sl":22,"f":"D002"},{"t":"2022 Middletown discussion","n":"week 3 discussion MT","sl":20,"f":"D064"},{"t":"? Harlem clicker","n":"Harlem GI physio clickers (image PDF)","sl":35,"f":"D014"}],"tier":1,"n":"25"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C5","u":19,"label":"Urease makes NH3, which neutralizes acid","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G043-37.jpg","cap":"Urease splits urea; NH3 grabs acid, forming safe NH4+","where":"2026 lecture: L53 Gastric Secretion, slide 37"},{"src":"../sl/G043-36.jpg","cap":"Panel two: urease turns urea into CO2 and NH3, neutralizing acid","where":"2026 lecture: L53 Gastric Secretion, slide 36"}],"q":[{"id":"D004-189-1","s":"This organism is found in a stomach biopsy. What is the mechanisms that allows it to exist in the acidic environment of the stomach?","o":["A. Ammonia neutralizing H+","B. Increasing prostaglandins","C. Increasing urea levels","D. Inhibition of proton pumps","E. Inhibition of urease"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Ammonia neutralizing H+","a":"A","why":"<b>A.</b> The curved, flagellated rod is <b>Helicobacter pylori</b>. Its enzyme <b>urease</b> splits urea into <b>ammonia (NH3)</b>, which binds acid (H+) and makes a protective alkaline cloud.<br>B. Prostaglandins protect the stomach lining; the bacterium does not use them.<br>C. It uses up urea; it does not raise urea levels.<br>D. Proton pump inhibitors are drugs used to treat it, not its survival trick.<br>E. It needs urease working, not inhibited.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/D004-189.jpg","in":"Illustrations of a curved, multi-flagellated gram-negative rod (Helicobacter pylori) beside the options; the organism must be recognized as H. pylori.","tg":["L53 slide 37 (Acid Attack!)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown discussion","n":"Week 14b discussion session (L52-54)","sl":52,"f":"G071"},{"t":"2024 Middletown clicker","n":"Physio 2 E1 clickers merged","sl":189,"f":"D004"},{"t":"2023 discussion","n":"week 3 discussion","sl":42,"f":"D002"},{"t":"2022 Middletown discussion","n":"week 3 discussion MT","sl":39,"f":"D064"},{"t":"? Harlem clicker","n":"Harlem GI physio clickers (image PDF)","sl":39,"f":"D014"}],"tier":1,"n":"26"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C5","u":20,"label":"Chronic antral gastritis: think H. pylori","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G043-35.jpg","cap":"H. pylori: destroys mucus, damages epithelium; biopsy, stool antigen, urea breath test","where":"2026 lecture: L53 Gastric Secretion, slide 35"},{"src":"../sl/G043-36.jpg","cap":"H. pylori burrows into mucus, neutralizes acid with urease, damages mucosa","where":"2026 lecture: L53 Gastric Secretion, slide 36"}],"q":[{"id":"D022-21-1","s":"A 45-year-old man has had vague abdominal pain and nausea for the past 3 years. This pain is unrelieved by antacid medications. On physical examination there are no abnormal findings. Upper GI endoscopy reveals antral mucosal erythema, but no ulcerations or masses. Biopsies are taken, and microscopically there is a chronic non-specific gastritis. Which of the following conditions is most likely to be present in this man?","o":["a. Zollinger-Ellison syndrome","b. Pernicious anemia","c. Helicobacter pylori infection","d. None of the above"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Helicobacter pylori infection","a":"c","why":"<b>C. Helicobacter pylori.</b> It colonizes the <b>antrum</b> and causes <b>chronic gastritis</b> with redness, even without an ulcer. Diagnose by biopsy, stool antigen or carbon-13 urea breath test.<br>A. Zollinger-Ellison syndrome: gastrinoma with high acid and large, multiple ulcers.<br>B. Pernicious anemia: autoimmune gastritis of the body and fundus with vitamin B12 deficiency, not the antrum.<br>D. H. pylori fits, so 'none' is wrong.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L53 slide 35 (Helicobacter pylori)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown discussion","n":"Week 14a discussion session (L49-51)","sl":58,"f":"G070"},{"t":"2026 Middletown discussion","n":"Week 14b discussion session (L52-54)","sl":23,"f":"G071"},{"t":"2025 Middletown clicker","n":"2025 clickers (image slides)","sl":21,"f":"D022"},{"t":"2024 Middletown clicker","n":"Physio 2 E1 clickers merged","sl":167,"f":"D004"},{"t":"2024-25 faculty review","n":"Spring exam 1 professors review with answers","sl":57,"f":"G061"},{"t":"2023 discussion","n":"week 3 discussion","sl":20,"f":"D002"}],"tier":1,"n":"27"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C5","u":21,"label":"ZES: gastrin escapes acid feedback","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G043-44.jpg","cap":"ZES: acid feedback fails, so gastrin stays high despite high acid","where":"2026 lecture: L53 Gastric Secretion, slide 44"},{"src":"../sl/G043-19.jpg","cap":"Normal loop: acid inhibits G cells; acid blockers lower acid, raising gastrin","where":"2026 lecture: L53 Gastric Secretion, slide 19"}],"q":[{"id":"D022-20-1","s":"You and your study partner are discussing the relationship between acid secretion and gastrin levels at 2am on a Sunday morning. Your partner is sleep deprived and says several things that are incorrect. However, in a moment of clarity he says one thing that is correct and insightful. Which of the following is the correct statement?","o":["a. H2 blockers are the preferred treatment for metastatic gastrinoma","b. H2 blockers routinely depress gastrin levels","c. Increased acid production and elevated gastrin levels indicate Zollinger-Ellison syndrome","d. PPIs have no effect on gastrin levels"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Increased acid production and elevated gastrin levels indicate Zollinger-Ellison syndrome","a":"c","why":"<b>C.</b> Normally acid shuts off gastrin. <b>High acid AND high gastrin together</b> means the gastrin source ignores that feedback: <b>Zollinger-Ellison syndrome</b> (gastrinoma).<br>A. Metastatic gastrinoma gets a lifelong proton pump inhibitor (PPI), not histamine type 2 (H2) blockers.<br>B. H2 blockers lower acid, which <b>raises</b> gastrin.<br>D. PPIs also lower acid, so they <b>raise</b> gastrin.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L53 slide 47 (ZES)","L53 slide 44 (Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome)","L53 slide 18 (H2 Blockers)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown discussion","n":"Week 14b discussion session (L52-54)","sl":19,"f":"G071"},{"t":"2025 Middletown clicker","n":"2025 clickers (image slides)","sl":20,"f":"D022"},{"t":"2024 Middletown clicker","n":"Physio 2 E1 clickers merged","sl":163,"f":"D004"},{"t":"2023 discussion","n":"week 3 discussion","sl":16,"f":"D002"},{"t":"2022 Middletown discussion","n":"week 3 discussion MT","sl":16,"f":"D064"}],"tier":1,"n":"28"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C6","u":22,"label":"Pancreatic proteases secreted as inactive zymogens","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G044-21.jpg","cap":"Acini make active amylase and lipase but inactive proteases","where":"2026 lecture: L54 Pancreatic & Biliary, slide 21"},{"src":"../sl/G044-9.jpg","cap":"Trypsinogen, chymotrypsinogen, procarboxypeptidase, proelastase: activated only in duodenum","where":"2026 lecture: L54 Pancreatic & Biliary, slide 9"},{"src":"../sl/G045-42.jpg","cap":"Enterokinase activates trypsinogen; trypsin then activates the other proteases","where":"2026 lecture: L55 Carb & Protein Absorption, slide 42"}],"q":[{"id":"D004-196-1","s":"Which of the following is produced with a negative regulatory domain?","o":["A. Chymotrypsinogen","B. Lingual amylase","C. Lingual lipase","D. Pancreatic amylase","E. Pancreatic lipase"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Chymotrypsinogen","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Chymotrypsinogen</b>. The ‘-ogen’ ending marks a <b>zymogen</b>: an inactive protease whose extra regulatory piece blocks it until <b>trypsin</b> cuts it off in the duodenum. That keeps the protein-made pancreas from digesting itself.<br>B. Lingual amylase: a salivary starch enzyme, secreted active.<br>C. Lingual lipase: secreted active in saliva.<br>D. Pancreatic amylase: secreted active; the pancreas is not made of starch.<br>E. Pancreatic lipase: secreted active; the pancreas is not made of fat.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L54 slide 9 (Why Proteases are made in INACTIVE form)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown discussion","n":"Week 14b discussion session (L52-54)","sl":59,"f":"G071"},{"t":"2024 Middletown clicker","n":"Physio 2 E1 clickers merged","sl":196,"f":"D004"},{"t":"2023 discussion","n":"week 3 discussion","sl":49,"f":"D002"},{"t":"2022 Middletown discussion","n":"week 3 discussion MT","sl":45,"f":"D064"}],"tier":1,"n":"29"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C6","u":23,"label":"Secretin stimulation test diagnoses chronic pancreatitis","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G045-56.jpg","cap":"IV secretin test: normal massive bicarbonate; chronic pancreatitis decreased","where":"2026 lecture: L55 Carb & Protein Absorption, slide 56"},{"src":"../sl/G045-55.jpg","cap":"Chronic pancreatitis: burnt-out pancreas, no enzymes, low bicarbonate to secretin","where":"2026 lecture: L55 Carb & Protein Absorption, slide 55"}],"q":[{"id":"D022-24-1","s":"A 43 yo woman presents with oily, floating stool. She has alcohol overuse issues. She has lost 25 lbs unintentionally. The tests for celiac disease (autoimmune disease resulting in inflammation and loss of villi and absorptive surface area) are equivocal. Which of the following is the most specific test of whether or not this is chronic pancreatitis?","o":["a. Biopsy of pancreas","b. Blood levels of Vitamin A, D, E and K","c. CCK level in the blood","d. Secretin stimulation test"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Secretin stimulation test","a":"d","why":"<b>d. Secretin stimulation test.</b> IV (intravenous) secretin normally causes a <b>massive bicarbonate</b> release; in chronic pancreatitis bicarbonate output is <b>markedly decreased</b>. It tests pancreatic function directly.<br>a. Pancreas biopsy: risky, and not a test of function.<br>b. Vitamin A, D, E, K levels: low in any fat malabsorption, celiac disease included.<br>c. CCK (cholecystokinin) level: not a diagnostic test for chronic pancreatitis.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L55 slide 56 (IV Secretin Stimulation Test)","L55 slide 55 (Chronic Pancreatitis)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown discussion","n":"Week 14b discussion session (L52-54)","sl":35,"f":"G071"},{"t":"2025 Middletown clicker","n":"2025 clickers (image slides)","sl":24,"f":"D022"},{"t":"2024 Middletown clicker","n":"Physio 2 E1 clickers merged","sl":174,"f":"D004"},{"t":"2023 discussion","n":"week 3 discussion","sl":27,"f":"D002"},{"t":"2022 Middletown discussion","n":"week 3 discussion MT","sl":25,"f":"D064"},{"t":"? Harlem clicker","n":"Harlem GI physio clickers (image PDF)","sl":33,"f":"D014"}],"tier":1,"n":"30"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C6","u":23,"label":"Cystic fibrosis: CFTR chloride defect, opposite of cholera","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G045-57.jpg","cap":"CF: clogged pancreatic ducts, enzymes not released, 'opposite of cholera'","where":"2026 lecture: L55 Carb & Protein Absorption, slide 57"},{"src":"../sl/D007-148.jpg","cap":"TA summary: CFTR defect lowers chloride secretion and clogs pancreatic ducts","where":"2022 Harlem TA review, slide 148"}],"q":[{"id":"D004-228-1","s":"What links cholera and cystic fibrosis in the intestinal and pancreatic duct epithelium?","o":["A. Both act on the CFTR chloride channel: cholera toxin holds it open (secretory diarrhea), while in cystic fibrosis it is defective (too little fluid secretion)","B. Both act on the basolateral Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase: cholera toxin blocks it, while in cystic fibrosis it is overactive","C. Both act on the Na⁺-glucose cotransporter (SGLT1): cholera toxin blocks it, while in cystic fibrosis it is missing","D. Both raise cGMP in enterocytes and open the ENaC sodium channel","E. Both block the Cl⁻/HCO₃⁻ exchanger, so chloride builds up inside the cells"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Both act on the CFTR chloride channel: cholera toxin holds it open (secretory diarrhea), while in cystic fibrosis it is defective (too little fluid secretion)","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Both act on CFTR.</b> CFTR (cystic fibrosis transmembrane conductance regulator) is the apical chloride channel of crypt cells and pancreatic duct cells. Cholera toxin keeps Gs switched on, raises cAMP (cyclic adenosine monophosphate) and holds CFTR open, so chloride, sodium and water pour out: secretory diarrhea. In cystic fibrosis CFTR is defective, so too little fluid is secreted and thick secretions plug the pancreatic ducts: the opposite of cholera.<br>B. Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase: it powers the gradients but is not the shared defect, and cholera does not block it.<br>C. SGLT1: it keeps working in cholera, which is why oral rehydration with glucose and salt works.<br>D. cGMP and ENaC: heat-stable E. coli toxin raises cGMP; cholera raises cAMP, and neither disease is an ENaC problem.<br>E. Cl⁻/HCO₃⁻ exchanger: not the target of either disease; the shared protein is the apical CFTR channel.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L55 slide 57 (Cystic Fibrosis)","L57 slide 11 (Intestinal Secretion)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown discussion","n":"Week 15 discussion session (L55-57)","sl":22,"f":"G072"},{"t":"2024 Middletown clicker","n":"Physio 2 E1 clickers merged","sl":228,"f":"D004"},{"t":"2022 Middletown discussion","n":"week 4 discussion MT","sl":28,"f":"D065"}],"tier":1,"n":"31"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C6","u":24,"label":"Bile salts emulsify fat","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G046-22.jpg","cap":"Bile salts break a large fat blob into small droplets","where":"2026 lecture: L56 Lipid Digestion, slide 22"},{"src":"../sl/G044-26.jpg","cap":"Emulsification: large fat blob to small micelle","where":"2026 lecture: L54 Pancreatic & Biliary, slide 26"}],"q":[{"id":"D004-191-1","s":"Bile is produced by the liver continuously. (Which of the following is true?)","o":["A. bile functions to catalyze digestive processes in a manner similar to enzymes","B. bile salts emulsify large droplets of triglycerides","C. bile salts reduce the surface area of droplets of triglycerides","D. 95% of bile salts are lost in the feces"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"bile salts emulsify large droplets of triglycerides","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Bile salts emulsify large triglyceride droplets</b> into small ones, which <b>increases surface area</b> for pancreatic lipase.<br>A. Bile is a detergent, not a catalyst; it has no enzyme action.<br>C. Emulsification raises surface area; it does not reduce it.<br>D. About 95 percent of bile salts are reabsorbed in the terminal ileum and recycled; only about 600 milligrams a day are lost in stool.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L54 slide 26 (Bile ACIDS made from cholesterol)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown discussion","n":"Week 14b discussion session (L52-54)","sl":54,"f":"G071"},{"t":"2024 Middletown clicker","n":"Physio 2 E1 clickers merged","sl":191,"f":"D004"},{"t":"2023 discussion","n":"week 3 discussion","sl":44,"f":"D002"},{"t":"2022 Middletown discussion","n":"week 3 discussion MT","sl":41,"f":"D064"}],"tier":1,"n":"32"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C6","u":25,"label":"Sodium–bile salt cotransporter in ileum","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G046-33.jpg","cap":"Terminal ileum returns bile to the liver through the hepatic portal vein","where":"2026 lecture: L56 Lipid Digestion, slide 33"},{"src":"../sl/G066-180.jpg","cap":"TA slide: sodium–bile salt cotransporters move ileal bile salts into portal blood","where":"2024 TA review, slide 180"}],"q":[{"id":"D022-26-1","s":"Enterohepatic circulation of bile salts will involve which one of the following steps","o":["a. Bile salts are transported from duodenum into liver","b. Portal blood transports bile salts from bile duct to liver","c. Bile salts are transported from intestinal lumen to blood by Na+-bile salt cotransporters","d. All of the above"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Bile salts are transported from intestinal lumen to blood by Na+-bile salt cotransporters","a":"c","why":"<b>C</b>. In the terminal ileum, bile salts move from the lumen into blood on a <b>sodium–bile salt cotransporter</b>; portal blood then carries them back to the liver.<br>A. They are reabsorbed in the terminal ileum, not the duodenum.<br>B. Portal blood brings them from the ileum, not from the bile duct.<br>D. Only C is correct.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L54 slide 29 (Enterohepatic Circulation)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown discussion","n":"Week 14b discussion session (L52-54)","sl":43,"f":"G071"},{"t":"2026 Middletown quiz","n":"Week 14b Canvas quiz Fall 2026 (secretions)","sl":3,"f":"G104"},{"t":"2025 Middletown clicker","n":"2025 clickers (image slides)","sl":26,"f":"D022"},{"t":"2024 Middletown clicker","n":"Physio 2 E1 clickers merged","sl":182,"f":"D004"},{"t":"2023 discussion","n":"week 3 discussion","sl":35,"f":"D002"},{"t":"2022 Middletown discussion","n":"week 3 discussion MT","sl":32,"f":"D064"},{"t":"2023 quiz","n":"Physio II exam 1 Canvas quizzes (image slides)","sl":31,"f":"D016"}],"tier":1,"n":"33"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C7","u":26,"label":"Enterocytes replaced every three to six days","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G016-13.jpg","cap":"Crypt regenerative stem cells constantly replace villus enterocytes","where":"2026 lecture: H15B Digestive Tract, slide 13"},{"src":"../sl/G045-13.jpg","cap":"Villi, enterocytes and their microvilli (brush border) magnified","where":"2026 lecture: L55 Carb & Protein Absorption, slide 13"}],"q":[{"id":"D004-193-1","s":"Why are enterocytes (epithelial cells) in the duodenum replaced every 3-5 days?","o":["A. Acid damages them","B. Proteolytic enzymes damage them","C. There is physical or mechanical damage","D. A, B and C are correct","E. B and C are correct (but not A)"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"A, B and C are correct","a":"D","why":"<b>D.</b> Duodenal enterocytes face <b>acid</b> from the stomach, <b>proteolytic enzymes</b> (shed cells are even digested as dietary protein) and <b>mechanical</b> wear from chyme. So crypt stem cells replace them every few days.<br>A, B, C. Each is true, but each is only part of the reason.<br>E. Leaves out acid, yet acidic chyme enters the duodenum before bicarbonate neutralizes it.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L55 slide 8 (Structure of Intestinal Mucosa)","L55 slide 45 (Autodigestion)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown discussion","n":"Week 14b discussion session (L52-54)","sl":56,"f":"G071"},{"t":"2024 Middletown clicker","n":"Physio 2 E1 clickers merged","sl":193,"f":"D004"},{"t":"2023 discussion","n":"week 3 discussion","sl":46,"f":"D002"},{"t":"2022 Middletown discussion","n":"week 3 discussion MT","sl":43,"f":"D064"}],"tier":1,"n":"34"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C7","u":27,"label":"Glucose: SGLT-1 in, GLUT-2 out","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G045-20.jpg","cap":"SGLT1 brings glucose/galactose in, GLUT5 fructose; GLUT2 exits to blood","where":"2026 lecture: L55 Carb & Protein Absorption, slide 20"},{"src":"../sl/G045-7.jpg","cap":"SGLT1 at lumen, GLUT2 at blood side, Na⁺/K⁺ pump basolateral","where":"2026 lecture: L55 Carb & Protein Absorption, slide 7"}],"q":[{"id":"G055-4-1","s":"GLUT2 and SGLT1 are both found in brush border cells. Why are both required for glucose absorption?","o":["a. GLUT2 transports fructose into the cell and SGLT1 transports glucose into the cell","b. GLUT2 drives glucose against the concentration from the lumen into the cell and SGLT1 transports glucose out of the cell with the concentration gradient","c. GLUT2 transports glucose with the concentration gradient from the lumen into the cell and SGLT1 drives glucose out of the cell against the concentration gradient","d. SGLT1 drives glucose from the lumen into the cell against the concentration gradient and GLUT2 transports glucose out of the cell into the blood with the concentration gradient"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"SGLT1 drives glucose from the lumen into the cell against the concentration gradient and GLUT2 transports glucose out of the cell into the blood with the concentration gradient","a":"d","why":"<b>d.</b> <b>SGLT-1</b> (sodium-glucose cotransporter 1) on the lumen side pulls glucose <b>into</b> the cell against its gradient, riding on sodium. <b>GLUT-2</b> (glucose transporter 2) on the blood side lets it <b>out</b>, down its gradient. (GLUT-2 is basolateral, not in the brush border.)<br>a. Fructose enters by GLUT-5; GLUT-2 is the exit for all three sugars.<br>b. Reversed: GLUT-2 is passive and on the blood side.<br>c. Reversed: SGLT-1 brings glucose in; it doesn't push it out.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L55 slide 19 (Absorption of Sugars)","L55 slide 27 (Glucose)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown discussion","n":"Week 15 discussion session (L55-57)","sl":4,"f":"G072"},{"t":"2025 discussion","n":"Physiology II week 4 discussion (file partly corrupt)","sl":4,"f":"G055"},{"t":"2024 Middletown clicker","n":"Physio 2 E1 clickers merged","sl":201,"f":"D004"},{"t":"2022 Harlem TA review","n":"Harlem TA review 2022","sl":153,"f":"D007"},{"t":"2022 Middletown discussion","n":"week 4 discussion MT","sl":4,"f":"D065"},{"t":"2021 Harlem TA review","n":"acid-base & GI TA review: GI part only","sl":193,"f":"F1212"},{"t":"2023 quiz","n":"Physio II exam 1 Canvas quizzes (image slides)","sl":36,"f":"D016"}],"tier":1,"n":"35"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C7","u":28,"label":"Lactose intolerance causes osmotic diarrhea","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G045-32.jpg","cap":"Unabsorbed sugar: extra osmotic draw in bowel, bacterial overgrowth","where":"2026 lecture: L55 Carb & Protein Absorption, slide 32"},{"src":"../sl/G045-30.jpg","cap":"Lactase deficiency causes osmotic diarrhea; stop milk products","where":"2026 lecture: L55 Carb & Protein Absorption, slide 30"}],"q":[{"id":"G055-24-1","s":"A 25-year-old male is suffering from lactose intolerance; which of the following does not happen in lactose intolerance;","o":["A. Non-persistence of enzyme lactase at the jejunal brush border","B. Undigested lactose being absorbed by the ileal cells","C. Abdominal symptoms e.g. bloating, and pain","D. Gut Bacteria degrading lactose into osmotically active solutes"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Undigested lactose being absorbed by the ileal cells","a":"B","why":"<b>B does not happen.</b> Lactose is a disaccharide and only monosaccharides are absorbed, so <b>undigested lactose is never absorbed</b>. It stays in the lumen and causes osmotic diarrhea.<br>A. Happens: lactase 'non-persistence' (its normal fall after childhood) is the usual cause.<br>C. Happens: bloating and pain from gas and retained water.<br>D. Happens: colon bacteria ferment lactose into small, osmotically active molecules and gas.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L55 slide 30 (Lactase Deficiency)","L55 slide 32 (Can’t absorb sugars?)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown discussion","n":"Week 15 discussion session (L55-57)","sl":29,"f":"G072"},{"t":"2025 discussion","n":"Physiology II week 4 discussion (file partly corrupt)","sl":24,"f":"G055"},{"t":"2024 Middletown clicker","n":"Physio 2 E1 clickers merged","sl":220,"f":"D004"},{"t":"2023 quiz","n":"Physio II exam 1 Canvas quizzes (image slides)","sl":44,"f":"D016"},{"t":"2022 Middletown discussion","n":"week 4 discussion MT","sl":22,"f":"D065"}],"tier":1,"n":"36"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C7","u":29,"label":"Exopeptidase (carboxypeptidase) vs endopeptidases","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G045-44.jpg","cap":"Exopeptidase clips C-terminal amino acids (carboxypeptidase); endopeptidases cut the middle","where":"2026 lecture: L55 Carb & Protein Absorption, slide 44"},{"src":"../sl/D040-151.jpg","cap":"Trypsin activates its own trypsinogen and every other proenzyme","where":"2023 TA review, slide 151"}],"q":[{"id":"D004-238-1","s":"Which of the following is an exopeptidase?","o":["A. Enterokinase (Enteropeptidase)","B. Trypsin","C. Elastase","D. Carboxypeptidase b","E. Chymotrypsin"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Carboxypeptidase b","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Carboxypeptidase B</b> is an <b>exopeptidase</b>: it removes one amino acid at a time from the carboxyl (C-terminal) end. Carboxypeptidase A works the same way.<br>A. Enterokinase: activates trypsinogen with one specific cut; not a digestive exopeptidase.<br>B. Trypsin: endopeptidase (cuts inside the chain).<br>C. Elastase: endopeptidase.<br>E. Chymotrypsin: endopeptidase.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L55 slide 44 (Protein Digestion- Many Proteases)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown discussion","n":"Week 15 discussion session (L55-57)","sl":44,"f":"G072"},{"t":"2024 Middletown clicker","n":"Physio 2 E1 clickers merged","sl":238,"f":"D004"},{"t":"2022 Middletown discussion","n":"week 4 discussion MT","sl":38,"f":"D065"},{"t":"? Montana TA review","n":"Montana physio 2 exam 1 (GI)","sl":188,"f":"D015"}],"tier":1,"n":"37"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C8","u":30,"label":"Which pancreatic lipid enzymes are secreted active","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G046-6.jpg","cap":"Pancreatic lipase is secreted in active form","where":"2026 lecture: L56 Lipid Digestion, slide 6"},{"src":"../sl/G046-12.jpg","cap":"Cholesterol ester hydrolase pulls fatty acid off cholesterol; secreted active","where":"2026 lecture: L56 Lipid Digestion, slide 12"},{"src":"../sl/G046-13.jpg","cap":"Phospholipase A2 makes lysolecithin; secreted inactive and activated by trypsin","where":"2026 lecture: L56 Lipid Digestion, slide 13"}],"q":[{"id":"D004-240-1","s":"Is PLA2 produced as a zymogen?","o":["A. Yes, and it is activated by enterokinase","B. Yes, and it is activated by trypsin","C. No","D. No, but it is not active until the environment pH is basic"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Yes, and it is activated by trypsin","a":"B","why":"<b>B.</b> Yes. <b>Phospholipase A2</b> is secreted <b>inactive</b> (a zymogen, meaning an inactive precursor), and <b>trypsin</b> activates it. It then splits phospholipids into lysolecithin and a fatty acid.<br>A. Enterokinase: activates trypsinogen; trypsin then activates phospholipase A2.<br>C. No: lipase and cholesterol ester hydrolase are secreted active, but phospholipase A2 is not.<br>D. Active only at basic pH: it needs cutting by trypsin, not just a pH change.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L56 slide 13 (Phospholipase A2)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown discussion","n":"Week 15 discussion session (L55-57)","sl":46,"f":"G072"},{"t":"2024 Middletown clicker","n":"Physio 2 E1 clickers merged","sl":240,"f":"D004"},{"t":"2022 Middletown discussion","n":"week 4 discussion MT","sl":40,"f":"D065"}],"tier":1,"n":"38"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C8","u":31,"label":"No apoprotein B, no chylomicrons","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G046-29.jpg","cap":"No apoprotein B, no absorption; apoprotein B is made in intestinal cells","where":"2026 lecture: L56 Lipid Digestion, slide 29"},{"src":"../sl/G046-26.jpg","cap":"Chylomicron surface studded with apoproteins, including several apoprotein B molecules","where":"2026 lecture: L56 Lipid Digestion, slide 26"},{"src":"../sl/D007-162.jpg","cap":"TA summary: apoprotein B-48 from gut cells; abetalipoproteinemia blocks dietary lipid absorption","where":"2022 Harlem TA review, slide 162"}],"q":[{"id":"G055-13-1","s":"Abetalipoproteinemia is a rare inherited disorder in which;","o":["a. There is a normal absorption of fat and fat-soluble vitamins from food","b. The intestinal epithelial cells are unable to package chylomicrons","c. There is overexpression of apoproteins, leading to the absorption of chylomicrons","d. Dietary lipids are enclosed by protein Apo B"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"The intestinal epithelial cells are unable to package chylomicrons","a":"b","why":"<b>B.</b> In <b>abetalipoproteinemia</b>, intestinal cells can't make <b>apoprotein B</b>, so they <b>can't package chylomicrons</b>; fat stays trapped in the cells.<br>A. Normal fat absorption: absorption of fat and vitamins A, D, E and K falls.<br>C. Overexpression of apoproteins: the problem is a lack of apoprotein B.<br>D. Lipids enclosed by apoprotein B: that describes normal chylomicron packaging, the very step that fails.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L56 slide 29 (Chylomicron Transport)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown discussion","n":"Week 15 discussion session (L55-57)","sl":16,"f":"G072"},{"t":"2025 discussion","n":"Physiology II week 4 discussion (file partly corrupt)","sl":13,"f":"G055"},{"t":"2024 Middletown clicker","n":"Physio 2 E1 clickers merged","sl":211,"f":"D004"},{"t":"2022 Middletown discussion","n":"week 4 discussion MT","sl":13,"f":"D065"}],"tier":1,"n":"39"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C8","u":32,"label":"Terminal ileum loss causes steatorrhea","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G046-33.jpg","cap":"No bile, minimal fat absorbed; terminal ileum returns bile salts to the liver","where":"2026 lecture: L56 Lipid Digestion, slide 33"},{"src":"../sl/G046-34.jpg","cap":"Enterohepatic circulation: over ninety-five percent of bile salts return by the portal vein","where":"2026 lecture: L56 Lipid Digestion, slide 34"}],"q":[{"id":"D022-25-1","s":"A 39 yo woman is diagnosed with intestinal cancer and has a significant portion of her distal ileum removed. She is given regular B12 injections to avoid pernicious anemia. Which of the following conditions is she also very likely to experience?","o":["a. Duodenal ulcer","b. Malabsorption of protein","c. Pancreatic insufficiency","d. Secretory diarrhea","e. Steatorrhea"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Steatorrhea","a":"e","why":"<b>E. Steatorrhea.</b> The <b>terminal ileum reabsorbs bile salts</b>. Remove it and bile salts are lost in stool, the liver can't keep up, the bile salt pool shrinks, <b>micelles fail</b>, and fat stays in the stool.<br>A. Duodenal ulcer: caused by acid or Helicobacter pylori, not ileal loss.<br>B. Protein malabsorption: protein is absorbed higher up.<br>C. Pancreatic insufficiency: the pancreas is untouched.<br>D. Secretory diarrhea: bile acids reaching the colon can draw some water, but the classic result is steatorrhea.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L56 slide 33 (Malabsorption: Bile problems)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown discussion","n":"Week 14a discussion session (L49-51)","sl":67,"f":"G070"},{"t":"2026 Middletown discussion","n":"Week 14b discussion session (L52-54)","sl":41,"f":"G071"},{"t":"2026 Middletown quiz","n":"Week 14b Canvas quiz Fall 2026 (secretions)","sl":2,"f":"G104"},{"t":"2025 Middletown clicker","n":"2025 clickers (image slides)","sl":25,"f":"D022"},{"t":"2024 Middletown clicker","n":"Physio 2 E1 clickers merged","sl":180,"f":"D004"},{"t":"2024-25 faculty review","n":"Spring exam 1 professors review with answers","sl":47,"f":"G061"},{"t":"2023 discussion","n":"week 3 discussion","sl":33,"f":"D002"},{"t":"2022 Middletown discussion","n":"week 3 discussion MT","sl":30,"f":"D064"},{"t":"2024 Middletown tutor review","n":"GI tutor review (Helen)","sl":58,"f":"D023"},{"t":"2023 faculty review","n":"Spring exam 1 review with answers","sl":45,"f":"D003"},{"t":"2023 quiz","n":"Physio II exam 1 Canvas quizzes (image slides)","sl":29,"f":"D016"}],"tier":1,"n":"40"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C8","u":33,"label":"Celiac disease: stop gluten","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G046-37.jpg","cap":"Celiac: gluten antibodies flatten villi; biopsy most accurate; stop gluten","where":"2026 lecture: L56 Lipid Digestion, slide 37"},{"src":"../sl/G046-39.jpg","cap":"Normal tall villi versus flattened villi in celiac disease","where":"2026 lecture: L56 Lipid Digestion, slide 39"}],"q":[{"id":"D004-223-1","s":"A 23-year-old man complains of a low volume but chronic, foul-smelling oily diarrhea for the past year. On physical examination there is no abdominal pain or mass and bowel sounds are present. His stool is negative for occult blood. Imaging and microscopic examination show absence of villi, increased surface intraepithelial lymphocytes, and hyperplastic appearing crypts. Which of the following therapies is most likely to be useful for this man?","o":["A. Antibiotics","B. Gluten-free diet","C. Selective vagotomy","D. Corticosteroids","E. Segmental duodenal resection"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Gluten-free diet","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Gluten-free diet.</b> Flat villi, extra intraepithelial lymphocytes and deep crypts mean <b>celiac disease</b>: antibodies against gluten (wheat, rye, barley) strip the villi. <b>Stopping gluten</b> heals it in weeks.<br>A. Antibiotics: treat tropical sprue, an infection that needs a travel history.<br>C. Selective vagotomy: an old ulcer operation.<br>D. Corticosteroids: not the standard treatment; removing gluten is.<br>E. Duodenal resection: celiac affects the whole small bowel; surgery doesn't help.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L56 slide 37 (Celiac Disease)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown discussion","n":"Week 15 discussion session (L55-57)","sl":27,"f":"G072"},{"t":"2024 Middletown clicker","n":"Physio 2 E1 clickers merged","sl":223,"f":"D004"},{"t":"2024-25 faculty review","n":"Spring exam 1 professors review with answers","sl":55,"f":"G061"},{"t":"2025 discussion","n":"Physiology II week 4 discussion (file partly corrupt)","sl":26,"f":"G055"},{"t":"2023 faculty review","n":"Spring exam 1 review with answers","sl":53,"f":"D003"}],"tier":1,"n":"41"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C8","u":33,"label":"No fat absorption, no fat-soluble vitamins","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G046-41.jpg","cap":"No fat absorption, no vitamin A, D, E, K absorption; no lipase, none either","where":"2026 lecture: L56 Lipid Digestion, slide 41"},{"src":"../sl/D004-207.jpg","cap":"Vitamin D drives calbindin-mediated calcium absorption in intestinal cells","where":"2024 Middletown clicker, slide 207"},{"src":"../sl/G047-3.jpg","cap":"Pancreatic proteases must remove R-protein before vitamin B12 can bind intrinsic factor","where":"2026 lecture: L57 Regional Absorption & Liver, slide 3"}],"q":[{"id":"G055-35-1","s":"Tropical sprue infection is often associated with low serum calcium. What is the mechanism?","o":["a. Tropical sprue infectious agents require calcium as a cofactor, reducing calcium available for dietary intake","b. Tropical sprue infectious agents produce a calcium ATPase toxin preventing dietary calcium absorption","c. Tropical sprue impairs absorption of lipids and lipid soluble vitamins","d. Tropical sprue is associated with increased incidence of bowel lymphoma that decreases serum calcium levels"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Tropical sprue impairs absorption of lipids and lipid soluble vitamins","a":"c","why":"<b>C.</b> Tropical sprue flattens villi, so <b>fat and fat-soluble vitamins</b> are poorly absorbed. Low <b>vitamin D</b> means less calbindin and <b>less calcium absorption</b>, so serum calcium falls.<br>A. Organisms use up calcium: no such mechanism.<br>B. Calcium ATPase (calcium pump) toxin: no such toxin.<br>D. Bowel lymphoma: linked to celiac disease, and it doesn't lower calcium.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L56 slide 40 (Tropical Sprue)","L56 slide 41 (Vitamins ADEK)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown discussion","n":"Week 15 discussion session (L55-57)","sl":38,"f":"G072"},{"t":"2025 discussion","n":"Physiology II week 4 discussion (file partly corrupt)","sl":35,"f":"G055"},{"t":"2024 Middletown clicker","n":"Physio 2 E1 clickers merged","sl":232,"f":"D004"},{"t":"2022 Middletown discussion","n":"week 4 discussion MT","sl":32,"f":"D065"}],"tier":1,"n":"42"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C8","u":34,"label":"Vitamins A, D, E, K fat-soluble; B water-soluble","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G046-41.jpg","cap":"Vitamins A, D, E and K are fat-soluble","where":"2026 lecture: L56 Lipid Digestion, slide 41"},{"src":"../sl/G047-3.jpg","cap":"Water-soluble vitamins use sodium-dependent cotransport; B12 needs intrinsic factor and terminal ileum","where":"2026 lecture: L57 Regional Absorption & Liver, slide 3"}],"q":[{"id":"D004-242-1","s":"Which of the following is NOT considered a fat-soluble vitamin?","o":["A. vitamin B12","B. vitamin K","C. vitamin E","D. vitamin A","E. vitamin D"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"vitamin B12","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Vitamin B12.</b> It is <b>water-soluble</b> and is absorbed in the terminal ileum bound to intrinsic factor. The <b>fat-soluble</b> vitamins are <b>A, D, E and K</b>.<br>B. Vitamin K: fat-soluble.<br>C. Vitamin E: fat-soluble.<br>D. Vitamin A: fat-soluble.<br>E. Vitamin D: fat-soluble.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L56 slide 41 (Vitamins ADEK)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown discussion","n":"Week 15 discussion session (L55-57)","sl":48,"f":"G072"},{"t":"2024 Middletown clicker","n":"Physio 2 E1 clickers merged","sl":242,"f":"D004"},{"t":"2022 Middletown discussion","n":"week 4 discussion MT","sl":42,"f":"D065"}],"tier":1,"n":"43"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C9","u":35,"label":"Crypt cells: basolateral Na-K-2Cl, like loop of Henle","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G047-11.jpg","cap":"2026 lecture: Na/K/2Cl transporter like the loop of Henle","where":"2026 lecture: L57 Regional Absorption & Liver, slide 11"},{"src":"../sl/D007-181.jpg","cap":"Crypt cell: basolateral Na+/K+/2Cl- cotransporter loads Cl-; apical Cl- channel","where":"2022 Harlem TA review, slide 181"}],"q":[{"id":"G055-16-1","s":"Where in the intestine would you find Cl- entering into the cells by way of secondary active transport?","o":["A. Apical membrane of epithelial cells of jejunum","B. Apical membrane of pancreatic acinar cells","C. Apical membrane of epithelial cells of colon","D. Basolateral membrane of epithelial cells of crypts"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Basolateral membrane of epithelial cells of crypts","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Basolateral membrane of crypt epithelial cells.</b> Crypt cells load chloride (Cl-) from blood through the <b>sodium-potassium-2-chloride cotransporter</b> on the basolateral side. It is secondary active: the sodium gradient made by the sodium-potassium pump drives Cl- in. Cl- then exits to the lumen through apical Cl- channels.<br>A. Jejunal apical membrane: absorbs sodium with sugars and amino acids; no Cl- cotransporter.<br>B. Pancreatic acinar apical membrane: Cl- leaves there; it does not enter by cotransport.<br>C. Colon apical membrane: sodium and potassium channels, not Cl- cotransport.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L57 slide 11 (Intestinal Secretion)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown discussion","n":"Week 15 discussion session (L55-57)","sl":19,"f":"G072"},{"t":"2025 discussion","n":"Physiology II week 4 discussion (file partly corrupt)","sl":16,"f":"G055"},{"t":"2024 Middletown clicker","n":"Physio 2 E1 clickers merged","sl":214,"f":"D004"},{"t":"2022 Middletown discussion","n":"week 4 discussion MT","sl":16,"f":"D065"},{"t":"2023 quiz","n":"Physio II exam 1 Canvas quizzes (image slides)","sl":42,"f":"D016"}],"tier":1,"n":"44"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C9","u":36,"label":"Diarrhea → normal-gap hyperchloremic acidosis","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G047-12.jpg","cap":"2026 lecture: diarrhea gives normal anion gap, high chloride, low bicarbonate","where":"2026 lecture: L57 Regional Absorption & Liver, slide 12"},{"src":"../sl/D013-22.jpg","cap":"Diarrheal fluid rich in K+ and HCO3-: hyperchloremic normal-gap acidosis","where":"2022 Middletown TA weekly, slide 22"}],"q":[{"id":"G055-37-1","s":"Blood chemistry results from a 17-year-old female with a history of laxative abuse show acidosis and Elevated chloride levels. What is the mechanism?","o":["a. Diarrhea similar in magnitude and mechanism to cholera","b. Increased H+ cotransport with peptides","c. Increased excretion of bicarbonate and potassium","d. Increased compensatory acid production to replace gastric fluid"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Increased excretion of bicarbonate and potassium","a":"c","why":"<b>C. Increased excretion of bicarbonate and potassium.</b> Laxative diarrhea washes out intestinal fluid that is high in <b>bicarbonate (HCO3-) and potassium (K+)</b>. Losing HCO3- causes acidosis; chloride rises in its place (normal-gap, hyperchloremic acidosis), and K+ falls.<br>A. Not a cholera-like process: the acidosis comes from HCO3- loss, not from toxin-driven chloride secretion.<br>B. Hydrogen ion-peptide cotransport absorbs peptides; it does not cause acidosis.<br>D. Losing gastric fluid (vomiting) causes alkalosis, not acidosis.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L57 slide 10 (Colon)","L57 slide 12 (Diarrhea)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown discussion","n":"Week 15 discussion session (L55-57)","sl":40,"f":"G072"},{"t":"2025 discussion","n":"Physiology II week 4 discussion (file partly corrupt)","sl":37,"f":"G055"},{"t":"2024 Middletown clicker","n":"Physio 2 E1 clickers merged","sl":234,"f":"D004"},{"t":"2022 Middletown discussion","n":"week 4 discussion MT","sl":34,"f":"D065"},{"t":"2023 quiz","n":"Physio II exam 1 Canvas quizzes (image slides)","sl":38,"f":"D016"}],"tier":1,"n":"45"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C9","u":37,"label":"Cholera toxin activates Gs, adenylyl cyclase, cAMP","slides":[{"src":"../sl/D015-211.jpg","cap":"Costanzo crypt cell: toxin acts on Gs alpha, cAMP opens Cl- channel","where":"? Montana TA review, slide 211"},{"src":"../sl/G066-253.jpg","cap":"Steps: Gs alpha ADP-ribosylated, GTP stuck, adenylyl cyclase on, cAMP up","where":"2024 TA review, slide 253"},{"src":"../sl/G047-11.jpg","cap":"2026 lecture: cholera increases chloride channels into the lumen","where":"2026 lecture: L57 Regional Absorption & Liver, slide 11"}],"q":[{"id":"G055-28-1","s":"A patient develops abdominal cramps and watery diarrhea by excessive secretion of fluid caused by Vibrio Cholerae. The causative agent is most likely to induce","o":["a. ATP formation","b. Adenylyl cyclase inhibition","c. Adenylyl cyclase activation","d. Closure of chloride channels"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Adenylyl cyclase activation","a":"c","why":"<b>C. Adenylyl cyclase activation.</b> Cholera toxin locks the stimulatory G protein (Gs) on, so <b>adenylyl cyclase keeps making cyclic adenosine monophosphate (cAMP)</b>. cAMP opens crypt chloride channels, and chloride, sodium and water pour out.<br>A. ATP formation: adenosine triphosphate is used up to make cAMP, not formed.<br>B. Adenylyl cyclase is activated, not inhibited.<br>D. Chloride channels open, not close.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L57 slide 11 (Intestinal Secretion)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown discussion","n":"Week 15 discussion session (L55-57)","sl":31,"f":"G072"},{"t":"2025 discussion","n":"Physiology II week 4 discussion (file partly corrupt)","sl":28,"f":"G055"},{"t":"2024 Middletown clicker","n":"Physio 2 E1 clickers merged","sl":225,"f":"D004"},{"t":"2022 Middletown discussion","n":"week 4 discussion MT","sl":25,"f":"D065"}],"tier":1,"n":"46"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C9","u":37,"label":"Cholera → secretory (not osmotic) diarrhea","slides":[{"src":"../sl/D007-181.jpg","cap":"Crypt secretion; cholera keeps adenylate cyclase on, giving rice-water stools","where":"2022 Harlem TA review, slide 181"},{"src":"../sl/G066-248.jpg","cap":"Contrast: osmotic diarrhea comes from unabsorbed solute such as lactose","where":"2024 TA review, slide 248"}],"q":[{"id":"D004-247-1","s":"The binding of cholera toxin irreversibly activates adenylate cyclase in the plasma membranes of crypt cells of the small intestine. This is most likely to cause:","o":["A. jaundice","B. secretory diarrhea","C. gallstones","D. osmotic diuresis"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"secretory diarrhea","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Secretory diarrhea.</b> Permanently active adenylyl cyclase in crypt cells keeps cyclic adenosine monophosphate (cAMP) high, which opens chloride channels; <b>chloride, sodium and water are secreted</b> into the lumen.<br>A. Jaundice: yellow skin from excess bilirubin; unrelated.<br>C. Gallstones: form in the gallbladder; unrelated.<br>D. Osmotic diuresis: extra urine from filtered solute (glucose, mannitol) in the kidney, not the gut.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L57 slide 11 (Intestinal Secretion)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown discussion","n":"Week 15 discussion session (L55-57)","sl":53,"f":"G072"},{"t":"2024 Middletown clicker","n":"Physio 2 E1 clickers merged","sl":247,"f":"D004"},{"t":"2022 Middletown discussion","n":"week 4 discussion MT","sl":47,"f":"D065"}],"tier":1,"n":"47"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C9","u":38,"label":"B12 deficiency → pernicious (megaloblastic) anemia","slides":[{"src":"../sl/D004-210.jpg","cap":"Autoimmune loss of parietal-cell intrinsic factor blocks ileal B12 uptake","where":"2024 Middletown clicker, slide 210"},{"src":"../sl/D007-176.jpg","cap":"Without intrinsic factor, B12 is lost: megaloblastic (pernicious) anemia","where":"2022 Harlem TA review, slide 176"}],"q":[{"id":"D004-245-1","s":"What is Vitamin B12 essential for?","o":["A. WBC formation","B. Vitamin B6 absorption","C. The maturation of RBCs","D. Folic acid absorption"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"The maturation of RBCs","a":"C","why":"<b>C. The maturation of red blood cells.</b> B12 is a cofactor for DNA synthesis, so marrow cells cannot divide normally without it; red cells stay <b>large and immature (megaloblastic anemia)</b>. B12 also keeps nerve myelin healthy.<br>A. White blood cell formation: can be disturbed too, but the tested role is red cell maturation.<br>B. Vitamin B6 absorption: B12 does not control it.<br>D. Folic acid absorption: B12 does not control it; B12 lack traps folate inside cells instead.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 50 (Deficiency of Vitamin B12)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 60 (Review of Objective 4)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown discussion","n":"Week 15 discussion session (L55-57)","sl":51,"f":"G072"},{"t":"2024 Middletown clicker","n":"Physio 2 E1 clickers merged","sl":245,"f":"D004"},{"t":"2022 Middletown discussion","n":"week 4 discussion MT","sl":45,"f":"D065"}],"tier":1,"n":"48"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C9","u":38,"label":"Gastrectomy → no intrinsic factor → B12 loss","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G047-4.jpg","cap":"2026 lecture: gastrectomy leaves no parietal cells; B12 must be injected","where":"2026 lecture: L57 Regional Absorption & Liver, slide 4"},{"src":"../sl/D007-176.jpg","cap":"Gastrectomy pearl: no intrinsic factor, oral B12 fails, inject B12","where":"2022 Harlem TA review, slide 176"}],"q":[{"id":"F1212-197-1","s":"The stomach of a 60-year-old Japanese male had to be totally removed due to cancer. Which of the following disorders is he likely to develop?","o":["A. Steatorrhea","B. Acute pancreatitis","C. Pernicious anemia","D. Excess fecal carbohydrate loss","E. Occasional episodes of paralysis"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Pernicious anemia","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Pernicious anemia.</b> Total gastrectomy removes all <b>parietal cells</b>, so there is <b>no intrinsic factor</b>; B12 cannot be absorbed in the terminal ileum and must be <b>injected</b>.<br>A. Steatorrhea: fat digestion relies on pancreatic lipase and bile, which remain.<br>B. Acute pancreatitis: not caused by removing the stomach.<br>D. Excess fecal carbohydrate loss: carbohydrates are digested and absorbed in the small intestine, which remains.<br>E. Occasional episodes of paralysis: not a result of gastrectomy.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L57 slide 4 (Gastrectomy)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown discussion","n":"Week 15 discussion session (L55-57)","sl":10,"f":"G072"},{"t":"2021 Harlem TA review","n":"acid-base & GI TA review: GI part only","sl":197,"f":"F1212"},{"t":"2025 discussion","n":"Physiology II week 4 discussion (file partly corrupt)","sl":10,"f":"G055"},{"t":"2024 Middletown clicker","n":"Physio 2 E1 clickers merged","sl":208,"f":"D004"},{"t":"2022 Middletown discussion","n":"week 4 discussion MT","sl":10,"f":"D065"},{"t":"2023 quiz","n":"Physio II exam 1 Canvas quizzes (image slides)","sl":40,"f":"D016"}],"tier":1,"n":"49"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C9","u":39,"label":"Liver disease → less vitamin D → hypocalcemia","slides":[{"src":"../sl/D004-207.jpg","cap":"Liver performs 25-hydroxylation; calcium absorption depends on vitamin D and calbindin","where":"2024 Middletown clicker, slide 207"},{"src":"../sl/G047-5.jpg","cap":"2026 lecture: calcium absorption needs 1,25-dihydroxy vitamin D and calbindin","where":"2026 lecture: L57 Regional Absorption & Liver, slide 5"}],"q":[{"id":"G055-7-1","s":"A 10-year-old boy is taken to a Quick Care clinic with yellow eye and skin. After a careful evaluation including liver function tests a diagnosis of hepatitis A is made. Which of the following is also likely to be observed in his profile?","o":["a. Hyperkalemia","b. Hypocalcemia","c. Decreased absorption of protein digestion products","d. None of the above"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Hypocalcemia","a":"b","why":"<b>B. Hypocalcemia.</b> The liver performs vitamin D's first activation step (<b>25-hydroxylation</b>) and makes the bile needed to absorb this fat-soluble vitamin. Inflamed liver, less active vitamin D, less <b>calbindin</b>, less calcium absorbed: low blood calcium.<br>A. Hyperkalemia: hepatitis has no link to high potassium.<br>C. Protein absorption: needs pancreatic and brush-border enzymes, not the liver or bile.<br>D. None of the above: the liver-vitamin D link makes B correct.","ex":"","fl":["Source itself marks the key with '??'. Intended logic: liver 25-hydroxylates vitamin D and cholestasis cuts fat-soluble vitamin absorption → less 1,25-(OH)2D → less Ca2+ absorption (G047:5). Acute hepatitis A rarely causes clinical hypocalcemia, so D is arguable; keep B as the course's intended answer.","Keep B as the intended answer (liver needed for 25-hydroxylation of vitamin D and bile for its absorption, so Ca2+ absorption falls), but the source itself marks it '??': acute hepatitis A rarely causes measurable hypocalcemia, so 'None of the above' is arguable."],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L57 slide 5 (Calcium and Iron Absorption)","L57 slide 13 (Liver Physiology)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown discussion","n":"Week 15 discussion session (L55-57)","sl":7,"f":"G072"},{"t":"2025 discussion","n":"Physiology II week 4 discussion (file partly corrupt)","sl":7,"f":"G055"},{"t":"2024 Middletown clicker","n":"Physio 2 E1 clickers merged","sl":205,"f":"D004"},{"t":"2022 Middletown discussion","n":"week 4 discussion MT","sl":7,"f":"D065"}],"tier":1,"n":"50"}]},{"l":"PHYS-C9","u":40,"label":"Bile-duct obstruction → conjugated bilirubin","slides":[{"src":"../sl/D015-214.jpg","cap":"Bilirubin pathway: hemolysis gives unconjugated, duct obstruction gives conjugated jaundice","where":"? Montana TA review, slide 214"},{"src":"../sl/D004-231.jpg","cap":"Bilirubin pathway beside bile flow; red X marks the blocked duct","where":"2024 Middletown clicker, slide 231"}],"q":[{"id":"G055-32-1","s":"Jaundice caused by conjugated bilirubin is likely to be triggered by;","o":["a. Hemolytic Jaundice","b. Common bile duct obstruction","c. Physiological jaundice","d. All of the above"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Common bile duct obstruction","a":"b","why":"<b>B. Common bile duct obstruction.</b> Bilirubin is conjugated (joined to glucuronic acid) in the liver; if the duct is blocked, the <b>conjugated</b> form backs up into blood.<br>A. Hemolytic jaundice: excess bilirubin production overwhelms conjugation, so unconjugated rises.<br>C. Physiological (newborn) jaundice: immature uridine diphosphate (UDP)-glucuronyl transferase, so unconjugated rises.<br>D. All of the above: only B gives conjugated jaundice.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L57 slide 13 (Liver Physiology)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown discussion","n":"Week 15 discussion session (L55-57)","sl":35,"f":"G072"},{"t":"2025 discussion","n":"Physiology II week 4 discussion (file partly corrupt)","sl":32,"f":"G055"},{"t":"2024 Middletown clicker","n":"Physio 2 E1 clickers merged","sl":229,"f":"D004"},{"t":"2024 Middletown tutor review","n":"GI tutor review (Helen)","sl":60,"f":"D023"},{"t":"2024-25 faculty review","n":"Spring exam 1 professors review with answers","sl":41,"f":"G061"},{"t":"2022 Middletown discussion","n":"week 4 discussion MT","sl":29,"f":"D065"},{"t":"2023 faculty review","n":"Spring exam 1 review with answers","sl":39,"f":"D003"}],"tier":1,"n":"51"}]}],"sum":{},"units":[{"l":"PHYS-C2","h":"Feedback inhibition and gastrin's signatures","p":"<h4>Feedback inhibition</h4>\n<p>Gastrin is the GI hormone with true <b>feedback inhibition</b>: its own product shuts it off. While food sits in the stomach it buffers the acid, so antral pH stays fairly high and gastrin keeps flowing. As the stomach empties, the acid is no longer buffered and the pH falls. Low pH stops the G cells, partly by releasing somatostatin from D cells. Gastrin's motility effect closes a second loop: by emptying the stomach it removes the distention and peptides that released it. CCK and secretin have no such direct brake; they fade once their stimulus is digested, absorbed or neutralized.</p>\n<p>The loop explains three clinical patterns. A <b>PPI (proton pump inhibitor)</b> such as omeprazole removes acid, so the brake lifts and gastrin rises. Removing the antrum removes the G cells, so acid falls and the gastric mucosa atrophies. A gastrin-secreting tumor (<b>gastrinoma</b>, Zollinger–Ellison syndrome) ignores the brake, so gastrin and acid are both high (Chapter 5).</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-96e2d91275.png\" alt=\"Gastrin&#x27;s two loops. Gastrin drives acid directly and through ECL histamine, and the low antral pH that results turns the G cells off. Gastrin also speeds the stomach, which empties it and removes the stretch and peptides. Dashed lines ending in a bar mean &quot;switches off&quot;.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,524px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">Gastrin's two loops. Gastrin drives acid directly and through ECL histamine, and the low antral pH that results turns the G cells off. Gastrin also speeds the stomach, which empties it and removes the stretch and peptides. Dashed lines ending in a bar mean \"switches off\". <small>· Flowchart from GI Physiology, Explained</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Worked examples: gastrin's signatures</h4>\n<p><b>Only feedback, only nerves</b>: of the four official hormones, gastrin alone is switched off by its own product (acid), and gastrin alone is released by a nerve (the vagus, via GRP).</p>\n<p><b>Only one that speeds the stomach</b>: gastrin is the only official hormone that ↑ gastric motility; CCK, secretin and GIP all slow emptying. Motilin is the tempting choice. It does stir the stomach, but only between meals, and it is only a candidate hormone.</p>\n<p><b>Major stimulus for gastrin</b>: <b>distention</b>. Fatty acids release CCK and secretin instead, somatostatin and secretin inhibit gastrin, and bicarbonate does nothing.</p>\n<p><b>Gastrin up after omeprazole</b>: a woman with a duodenal ulcer starts omeprazole, and two days later her gastrin is high. The cause is <b>loss of feedback inhibition</b>: less acid means no antral brake. It is not Zollinger–Ellison syndrome, drug failure or <i>H. pylori</i> (<i>Helicobacter pylori</i>, a stomach bacterium). If gastrin and acid are <i>both</i> high, as in ulcers that recur after <i>H. pylori</i> is cleared, suspect a gastrinoma: its gastrin <b>rises after IV (intravenous) secretin</b> (Chapter 5), which a urea breath test or a drug trial cannot show.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-efe6b8893b.jpg\" alt=\"(a) Typical sites of duodenal and gastric ulcers; the inset shows an ulcer eating through the mucosa toward the muscle. (b) A perforated gastric ulcer: the dark hole goes through the whole wall. A PPI lets ulcers heal by removing acid, and the same loss of acid lets gastrin rise.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,684px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:684px\">(a) Typical sites of duodenal and gastric ulcers; the inset shows an ulcer eating through the mucosa toward the muscle. (b) A perforated gastric ulcer: the dark hole goes through the whole wall. A PPI lets ulcers heal by removing acid, and the same loss of acid lets gastrin rise. <small>· GI hormones lecture, part 1, slide 19</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p><b>Thick stomach, oily stools</b> from a hyperactive peptide: <b>gastrin</b>, whose trophic effect thickens the mucosa while its acid inactivates lipase (worked through in Chapter 5).</p>","s":"Gastrin","sn":2,"pn":"2.2"},{"l":"PHYS-C2","h":"What releases CCK and its five actions","p":"<p><b>CCK</b>: I cells, duodenum and jejunum; gastrin–CCK family. Stimuli: fat digestion products, <b>fatty acids and monoglycerides</b> (not intact triglycerides), and small peptides and amino acids. Fat is the strongest. The name says what it does: <i>cholecysto</i> = gallbladder, <i>kinin</i> = movement. Every action helps digest a fatty, protein-rich meal.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-95517cbc66.jpg\" alt=\"CCK&#x27;s two main targets. It contracts the gallbladder and relaxes the sphincter of Oddi at the common bile duct opening, so bile enters the duodenum; it also makes the pancreas send enzymes down the pancreatic duct to the same opening.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,456px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">CCK's two main targets. It contracts the gallbladder and relaxes the sphincter of Oddi at the common bile duct opening, so bile enters the duodenum; it also makes the pancreas send enzymes down the pancreatic duct to the same opening. <small>· Pancreatic &amp; biliary secretion lecture, slide 7</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p>First, CCK <b>contracts the gallbladder</b> and relaxes the sphincter of Oddi, so bile enters the duodenum to emulsify fat. Second, it drives <b>pancreatic enzyme</b> secretion: lipase, amylase and proteases such as trypsin. Third, it adds a little pancreatic HCO₃⁻ (bicarbonate) itself but mainly <b>potentiates</b> secretin's HCO₃⁻ effect (Chapter 6). Fourth, it is trophic to the exocrine pancreas and gallbladder. Fifth, it <b>inhibits gastric emptying</b>, which buys time for slow fat digestion; CCK is the main hormone that slows the stomach (Chapter 3).</p>","s":"CCK and secretin","sn":3,"pn":"3.1"},{"l":"PHYS-C2","h":"CCK's loop and gallstones after pizza","p":"<p>As the fat and protein are digested and absorbed, and less new chyme arrives, the stimulus fades and CCK falls. Minor actions: a small rise in insulin; release of somatostatin from D cells, which damps acid; lower LES (lower esophageal sphincter) tone; and more intestinal motility, including a share in the gastrocolic reflex through CCK<sub>A</sub> receptors.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-f873b88348.png\" alt=\"CCK&#x27;s loop. Fat and protein products release CCK; CCK speeds their digestion and absorption and slows the arrival of new chyme, which removes its own stimulus.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,520px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">CCK's loop. Fat and protein products release CCK; CCK speeds their digestion and absorption and slows the arrival of new chyme, which removes its own stimulus. <small>· Flowchart from GI Physiology, Explained</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Worked example: gallstones after pizza</h4>\n<p>An obese, pregnant 40-year-old woman has severe right-upper-quadrant pain 3 hours after a cheesy pizza; stones block the common bile duct. Fat released <b>CCK</b>, which squeezes the gallbladder against the blockage, so more CCK would worsen the pain. She fits the classic \"female, forty, fat, fertile.\"</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-6d427139a8.jpg\" alt=\"Gallstones form in the gallbladder and can lodge in the cystic duct or the common bile duct (red arrows). After a fatty meal, CCK squeezes the gallbladder against the blockage, and that is the pain.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,418px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">Gallstones form in the gallbladder and can lodge in the cystic duct or the common bile duct (red arrows). After a fatty meal, CCK squeezes the gallbladder against the blockage, and that is the pain. <small>· Discussion session (wall, hormones, motility), 2026, slide 26</small></figcaption></figure>","s":"CCK and secretin","sn":3,"pn":"3.2"},{"l":"PHYS-C2","h":"The incretins: GIP and GLP-1","p":"<p>An <b>incretin</b> is a gut hormone, released within minutes of eating, that makes the pancreatic β cells secrete more insulin. There are two.</p>\n<p><b>GIP (glucose-dependent insulinotropic peptide)</b>, old name gastric inhibitory peptide: K cells, duodenum and jejunum; secretin–glucagon family. It is the only GI hormone released by all three nutrients: <b>glucose</b>, amino acids and fatty acids. In short, food. Actions: ↑ <b>insulin</b> (the major one); ↓ gastric H⁺; ↓ gastric motility and emptying. \"Glucose-dependent\" means GIP boosts insulin only while blood glucose is raised, so the insulin matches the meal. Each name describes one side: the new name its insulin effect, the old name its stomach effects.</p>\n<h4>The incretin effect</h4>\n<p>The incretins show up when an oral glucose load is compared with an IV infusion adjusted so blood glucose follows exactly the same curve. The oral load still releases far more insulin. Blood glucose was identical, so the extra insulin must come from the gut. Glucose in the intestine releases GIP and GLP-1, which add to glucose's direct effect on the β cell. IV glucose bypasses the gut and gets only the direct effect. So an oral load is cleared from the blood faster than the same dose given IV.</p>\n<p><b>Worked example: oral glucose cleared faster than IV</b>. The best explanation is <b>GIP-induced insulin release</b>. CCK raises insulin only slightly. VIP (vasoactive intestinal peptide) neither raises insulin nor releases GIP. GIP does not release glucagon, which would raise glucose anyway.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-828b61b49a.png\" alt=\"Top: blood glucose is made identical for the oral load and the IV infusion. Bottom: insulin still rises far higher after oral glucose; the hatched gap is the incretin effect of GIP and GLP-1.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,466px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">Top: blood glucose is made identical for the oral load and the IV infusion. Bottom: insulin still rises far higher after oral glucose; the hatched gap is the incretin effect of GIP and GLP-1. <small>· Graph redrawn from GIP and GLP-1 slides of the GI hormones lecture, part 2 (slides 3, 6–8), discussion session slides 46–47, and Costanzo p. 346 (schematic)</small></figcaption></figure>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-354eab3b99.png\" alt=\"Incretins and the drugs built on them. Oral glucose adds the incretin route to the direct effect of blood glucose on β cells; DPP-4 ends both incretins within minutes, so gliptins block DPP-4 and the analogues resist it.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,508px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">Incretins and the drugs built on them. Oral glucose adds the incretin route to the direct effect of blood glucose on β cells; DPP-4 ends both incretins within minutes, so gliptins block DPP-4 and the analogues resist it. <small>· Flowchart from GI Physiology, Explained</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p><b>GLP-1 (glucagon-like peptide-1)</b>: L cells, small intestine; cut from proglucagon. The name misleads, because GLP-1 is not like glucagon: it <b>inhibits glucagon</b>. It ↑ insulin and β-cell sensitivity to glucose, lowers blood glucose, cuts glucose output by the liver, grows the pancreas, slows gastric emptying (so you feel full), ↓ acid and ↓ appetite. Its half-life is only 1–2 minutes.</p>\n<p>Both incretins are destroyed by the enzyme <b>DPP-4 (dipeptidyl peptidase-4)</b>. Raising incretin activity treats type 2 diabetes in two ways. <b>DPP-4 inhibitors</b> (sitagliptin, saxagliptin, linagliptin: the \"-gliptins\") slow the breakdown of the body's own GIP and GLP-1; they are added on to other diabetes drugs. <b>GLP-1 analogues</b> (exenatide, liraglutide) resist breakdown, so a subcutaneous injection lasts about 12 hours instead of 1–2 minutes. Exenatide came from Gila monster saliva (John Eng, Bronx VA hospital, 1992; Rosalyn Yalow won her Nobel Prize for radioimmunoassay at the same hospital).</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-feb821c21c.jpg\" alt=\"Exenatide&#x27;s story: the Gila monster, whose saliva holds a GLP-1-like peptide that DPP-4 cannot destroy; John Eng, who found it at the Bronx VA in 1992; and the exenatide (Byetta) injection pens made from it.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,608px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:608px\">Exenatide's story: the Gila monster, whose saliva holds a GLP-1-like peptide that DPP-4 cannot destroy; John Eng, who found it at the Bronx VA in 1992; and the exenatide (Byetta) injection pens made from it. <small>· GI hormones lecture, part 2, slide 14</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Worked examples: incretins</h4>\n<p><b>Raises insulin the most</b>: <b>GIP</b>. CCK and secretin raise insulin only slightly.</p>\n<p><b>Degraded by DPP-4</b>: <b>GIP</b> (GLP-1 too, if offered).</p>\n<p><b>Cheeseburger, fries and a shake</b>: fat, carbohydrate <i>or</i> protein in the duodenum releases <b>GIP</b>. CCK needs fat or protein, gastrin needs protein (or stretch and vagus), and secretin needs acid or fat. Carbohydrate releases none of them.</p>","s":"Incretins and candidate hormones","sn":4,"pn":"4.1"},{"l":"PHYS-C2","h":"Somatostatin, histamine and the messengers side by side","p":"<p><b>Somatostatin</b>: D cells of the GI mucosa and pancreatic islets (δ cells), also the hypothalamus; acts as both paracrine and hormone. Released by H⁺ (low luminal pH), and also by CCK and secretin; inhibited by vagal stimulation. It <b>inhibits everything</b>: release of all GI hormones (gastrin included), gastric acid, pancreatic and gallbladder secretion, and insulin and glucagon release. On acid it acts directly on the parietal cell and indirectly by cutting gastrin and histamine (Chapter 5).</p>\n<p><b>Histamine</b>: ECL cells of the gastric body; an amine, not a peptide. It diffuses to H₂ receptors on nearby parietal cells and, with gastrin and ACh, stimulates acid. Gastrin and ACh both release it, so it amplifies their effects (potentiation, Chapter 5).</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-00d29d0df1.jpg\" alt=\"Where the paracrines are made in a gastric gland: ECL cells (histamine) and D cells (somatostatin) sit among the parietal and chief cells, with G cells at the base. Red labels mark messengers that make acid; the green one marks the brake.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,532px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">Where the paracrines are made in a gastric gland: ECL cells (histamine) and D cells (somatostatin) sit among the parietal and chief cells, with G cells at the base. Red labels mark messengers that make acid; the green one marks the brake. <small>· Gastric secretion lecture, slide 11</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p><b>VIP and GRP</b> are neurocrines (Chapter 1) with hormone-like reach. Beyond relaxing sphincters, VIP relaxes the stomach and gallbladder, adds water to pancreatic juice and bile, and inhibits gastric acid secretion and intestinal absorption. GRP is the vagus's messenger to the G cells.</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead><tr><th>Messenger</th><th>Cell, site</th><th>Released by</th><th>Main actions</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>Gastrin</td><td>G cells, antrum</td><td>Distention; peptides, amino acids; vagus (GRP). ↓ by acid, somatostatin</td><td>↑ H⁺ (mostly via histamine); mucosal growth; ↑ gastric motility</td></tr>\n<tr><td>CCK</td><td>I cells, duodenum, jejunum</td><td>Fatty acids, monoglycerides; peptides, amino acids</td><td>Gallbladder contracts, Oddi relaxes; ↑ pancreatic enzymes; potentiates secretin; trophic; ↓ gastric emptying</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Secretin</td><td>S cells, duodenum</td><td>Acid (pH &lt; 4.5); fatty acids</td><td>↑ HCO₃⁻ (pancreas, bile ducts, Brunner's glands); ↓ gastric H⁺; blocks gastrin's growth effect</td></tr>\n<tr><td>GIP</td><td>K cells, duodenum, jejunum</td><td>Glucose, amino acids, fatty acids</td><td>↑ insulin; ↓ gastric H⁺; ↓ gastric emptying</td></tr>\n<tr><td>GLP-1</td><td>L cells, small intestine</td><td>Oral glucose (meal)</td><td>↑ insulin; ↓ glucagon; ↓ emptying; ↓ appetite</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Motilin</td><td>Mo cells, upper duodenum</td><td>Fasting (cyclic); ↓ by food</td><td>Starts MMC; ↑ motility</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Somatostatin</td><td>D cells, mucosa and islets</td><td>H⁺. ↓ by vagus</td><td>Inhibits all GI hormones, acid, pancreatic secretion, insulin, glucagon</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Histamine</td><td>ECL cells, gastric body</td><td>Gastrin, ACh</td><td>↑ H⁺ via H₂ receptors</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h4>Pairs worth knowing</h4>\n<p>Gastrin and secretin are opposites: gastrin brings acid into the stomach, while secretin stops it and neutralizes what reaches the duodenum. CCK and secretin are partners: CCK sends enzymes and bile, secretin sends HCO₃⁻ and water, and each strengthens the other's pancreatic effect (Chapter 6). GIP and GLP-1 are the two incretins. Motilin and ghrelin are the fasting signals. Somatostatin brakes them all.</p>\n<h4>Worked example: four stimuli, four hormones</h4>\n<p>Acid, sugar, fat and distention are the strongest stimuli, in order, for which hormones? <b>Secretin, GIP, CCK, gastrin</b>. Acid in the duodenum releases secretin, oral glucose GIP, fat CCK, and stretch gastrin. The same map settles single-stimulus questions: stimulated most by acid → secretin; most by fat → CCK.</p>\n<h4>Do not confuse</h4>\n<p>GIP and GLP-1 are both incretins broken down by DPP-4, but GIP comes from K cells and GLP-1 from L cells. GLP-1 suppresses glucagon despite its name. Secretin inhibits normal G cells but stimulates gastrinoma cells. CCK answers fat and protein; GIP answers all three nutrients. Motilin and ghrelin rise with fasting; the official hormones rise with a meal.</p>","s":"Paracrines and neural peptides","sn":5,"pn":"5.1"},{"l":"PHYS-C3","h":"Spikes, contraction and what changes them","p":"<p>A slow wave alone gives at most a weak, tonic contraction. When a wave depolarizes the cell to threshold, a burst of <b>spike potentials</b> (true action potentials) rides on its peak. More Ca²⁺ enters, and a strong phasic contraction follows a moment later. The more spikes on a wave, the stronger the contraction; the spikes summate into one long contraction instead of separate twitches. Because every contraction needs a slow wave under it, the slow-wave frequency is the <b>maximum contraction rate</b> of that region. Not every wave reaches threshold: in the duodenum only about 30–40% carry spikes.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-085590d4a3.jpg\" alt=\"Top: a burst of spikes sits on the plateau of each slow wave. Bottom: tension (dotted line) rises just after each burst.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,537px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">Top: a burst of spikes sits on the plateau of each slow wave. Bottom: tension (dotted line) rises just after each burst. <small>· Costanzo, Physiology, 6th ed., p. 349</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p>Nerves and hormones act by shifting the baseline. <b>Stretch, ACh (acetylcholine) and parasympathetic</b> input depolarize the muscle, so more of each wave crosses threshold: more spikes, stronger contractions. <b>NE (norepinephrine) and sympathetic</b> input hyperpolarize it, so the waves stay below threshold and contractions fade.</p>\n<h4>Worked example: what changes the basic electrical rhythm?</h4>\n<p>Does ACh, GIP (glucose-dependent insulinotropic peptide) or CCK (cholecystokinin) change slow-wave frequency? Is the stomach faster than the duodenum? None of these: the rhythm is intrinsic, and the stomach (3/min) is slower than the duodenum (12/min). Likewise, NE placed near the duodenal ICC leaves the frequency unchanged but <b>weakens contractions</b>, because it removes spikes.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-a8021a6aea.jpg\" alt=\"Resting slow waves stay below threshold. Stretch, ACh and parasympathetic input raise the potential and add spikes; NE and sympathetic input hyperpolarize it and silence the spikes. The rhythm itself never changes.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,537px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">Resting slow waves stay below threshold. Stretch, ACh and parasympathetic input raise the potential and add spikes; NE and sympathetic input hyperpolarize it and silence the spikes. The rhythm itself never changes. <small>· Discussion summary (wall, hormones, motility), slide 13</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Worked example: nifedipine and constipation</h4>\n<p>A patient started on <b>nifedipine</b>, a CCB (calcium channel blocker), for hypertension becomes constipated. Spikes and contraction depend on Ca²⁺ entry, so blocking calcium channels weakens gut contractions and slows transit.</p>","s":"Gut smooth muscle and its electrical rhythm","sn":1,"pn":"1.3"},{"l":"PHYS-C3","h":"Swallowing, peristalsis and opening the LES","p":"<p><b>Chewing</b>: voluntary plus a brainstem reflex; mixes food with saliva to lubricate it, shrinks particles for swallowing, and mixes starch with salivary amylase (Chapter 4).</p>\n<p>Swallowing starts voluntarily, then becomes a reflex run by the <b>swallowing center</b> in the medulla. Signals arrive via the vagus and glossopharyngeal nerves; the center drives the striated pharynx and upper esophagus. The <b>oral phase</b> is voluntary: the tongue pushes the bolus back onto touch receptors in the pharynx, which triggers the reflex. The <b>pharyngeal phase</b> is reflex. The soft palate seals the nasopharynx. The epiglottis tips down over the rising larynx, so food cannot enter the trachea. The UES (upper esophageal sphincter) relaxes, a wave pushes the bolus through, and breathing pauses. In the <b>esophageal phase</b>, also reflex, the UES closes behind the bolus so it cannot reflux into the pharynx.</p>\n<h4>The esophagus</h4>\n<p>The bolus moves by <b>peristalsis</b>, the reflex called the \"law of the intestine.\" Stretch from the bolus excites enteric nerves on both sides of it. Behind the bolus, excitatory neurons release ACh and substance P: circular muscle contracts and longitudinal muscle relaxes. In front, inhibitory neurons release VIP (vasoactive intestinal peptide) and NO (nitric oxide): circular muscle relaxes and longitudinal muscle shortens. Each contracting segment makes high pressure behind the bolus and pushes it on. Gravity helps when sitting or standing.</p>\n<p>The <b>primary peristaltic wave</b> follows a swallow. It is set off by a conscious swallow but runs as part of the swallowing reflex, through the vagus. If food remains, local stretch starts a <b>secondary peristaltic wave</b>. It is automatic, run by the ENS (enteric nervous system), and begins at the site of distension. So vagotomy (cutting the vagus; Chapter 1) abolishes primary peristalsis, while secondary waves and slow waves survive. A secondary wave needs no swallow, yet the LES relaxes ahead of it just as it does for a primary wave.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-0e22bb39c3.png\" alt=\"After a swallow the upper sphincter (UES) dips open, a pressure peak passes down the esophagus, and the lower sphincter (LES) falls from 20 mm Hg to zero until the wave arrives.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,488px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">After a swallow the upper sphincter (UES) dips open, a pressure peak passes down the esophagus, and the lower sphincter (LES) falls from 20 mm Hg to zero until the wave arrives. <small>· Graph redrawn from Costanzo Fig. 8.9 (p. 351), schematic</small></figcaption></figure>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-13e03a0d34.jpg\" alt=\"The same pressure record in colour. Skeletal muscle (hatched) runs from the pharynx through the UES; smooth muscle runs below. Follow the blue traces: the UES dips, a peak travels down segment by segment, and the LES falls from 20 mm Hg until the wave arrives.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,646px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:646px\">The same pressure record in colour. Skeletal muscle (hatched) runs from the pharynx through the UES; smooth muscle runs below. Follow the blue traces: the UES dips, a peak travels down segment by segment, and the LES falls from 20 mm Hg until the wave arrives. <small>· Discussion session (wall, hormones, motility), 2026, slide 76</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p>The <b>LES</b> (lower esophageal sphincter) is closed at rest at about 20 mm Hg (millimeters of mercury, the unit of pressure). As the wave starts, vagal inhibitory pathways release <b>VIP and NO</b>, and LES pressure falls to zero. It stays open until the wave arrives, then returns to its resting tone. At the same moment the orad stomach relaxes (receptive relaxation, below). Both sphincters are needed because the esophagus sits in the chest. Its pressure equals intrathoracic pressure, below both atmospheric and abdominal pressure. The UES keeps air out at the top; the LES keeps acid out at the bottom.</p>\n<h4>Worked example: the sphincter you cannot dissect</h4>\n<p>The structure hardest to find in a cadaver is the <b>LES</b>: a functional zone of tonic smooth muscle without a distinct thickening. The upper sphincter, pylorus, ileocecal valve and duodenojejunal junction are visible landmarks.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-5189bb9df8.jpg\" alt=\"The esophagus (teal) runs down the back of the thorax and through the diaphragm to the stomach. No visible thickening marks the LES.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,342px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">The esophagus (teal) runs down the back of the thorax and through the diaphragm to the stomach. No visible thickening marks the LES. <small>· GI motility lecture, slide 11</small></figcaption></figure>","s":"Swallowing and the esophagus","sn":2,"pn":"2.1"},{"l":"PHYS-C3","h":"GERD, achalasia and esophageal spasm","p":"<h4>GERD: a sphincter too loose</h4>\n<p>In <b>GERD</b> (gastroesophageal reflux disease) LES tone is too low, so acid refluxes into the esophagus. Symptoms: epigastric pain; \"heartburn\" rising under the sternum; a bad taste in the mouth. <b>Factors that lower LES tone</b>: CCK, progesterone (pregnancy), nitrates, CCBs, high-fat meals, chocolate, nicotine (smoking), caffeine, alcohol, spicy food, and a large meal stretching the stomach. Alcohol blocks Ca²⁺ entry into esophageal smooth muscle and weakens its contraction. Pregnancy and obesity also raise abdominal pressure, which pushes gastric contents upward. Most accurate test: <b>24-hour pH monitoring</b>. Treatment: a PPI (proton pump inhibitor; Chapter 5) works in 90–95%. If PPIs fail, surgery tightens the closure. In a <b>Nissen fundoplication</b> the top of the stomach is wrapped around the lower esophagus and sutured, and the diaphragmatic hiatus is tightened.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-c764565f9f.jpg\" alt=\"Nissen fundoplication: the hiatus is tightened, and the top of the stomach (fundus) is wrapped around the lower esophagus and sutured, so the wrap squeezes the sphincter zone shut.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,494px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">Nissen fundoplication: the hiatus is tightened, and the top of the stomach (fundus) is wrapped around the lower esophagus and sutured, so the wrap squeezes the sphincter zone shut. <small>· GI motility lecture, slide 13</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Worked example: choking at night and burning chest pain</h4>\n<p>A 250-lb man has burning chest pain and chokes in his sleep, worse after large meals, alcohol, spicy food and coffee, better with acid blockers; heart and lungs are normal. The cause is <b>decreased LES tone</b>. An LES that fails to relax, or a tight UES, would block food, not let acid up.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-e043b974ac.jpg\" alt=\"Esophageal manometry during a swallow, normal person and achalasia patient. Compare the timing of the pressure peaks down the esophagus, and what LES pressure does during the swallow (the text below explains the difference).\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,537px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">Esophageal manometry during a swallow, normal person and achalasia patient. Compare the timing of the pressure peaks down the esophagus, and what LES pressure does during the swallow (the text below explains the difference). <small>· Supplemental problem set 2, page 2</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Achalasia: a sphincter that will not open</h4>\n<p><b>Achalasia</b> means \"failure to relax.\" The <b>inhibitory neurons</b> (VIP and NO) of the LES myenteric plexus are lost, and peristalsis in the lower two-thirds of the esophagus is impaired. The LES has high resting pressure and does not relax when the person swallows. The esophageal body contracts weakly and all at once instead of in sequence. Food therefore piles up above the sphincter and the esophagus dilates. Patients have <b>dysphagia</b> (difficulty swallowing) for solids <i>and</i> liquids, regurgitation, a feeling of food stuck in the chest, and weight loss. Barium swallow shows a dilated esophagus tapering to a narrow \"bird's beak\" at the LES. <b>Manometry</b> (pressure measurement) makes the diagnosis. Treatment opens the sphincter: balloon dilation, botulinum toxin (Botox) injection, or cutting the muscle surgically. None restores normal function.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-610e99390b.jpg\" alt=\"Achalasia on barium swallow: a dilated esophagus full of contrast tapers to a narrow &quot;bird&#x27;s beak&quot; at the LES.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,304px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">Achalasia on barium swallow: a dilated esophagus full of contrast tapers to a narrow \"bird's beak\" at the LES. <small>· GI motility lecture, slide 14</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Worked example: food that \"sticks\"</h4>\n<p>A 49-year-old cannot swallow solids or liquids, regurgitates, feels food stuck, and has lost 10 lb. Manometry shows an LES pressure that is high at rest and stays high during the swallow, plus simultaneous, non-sequential contractions. This is achalasia from selective loss of the inhibitory (VIP/NO) neurons to the LES; the dilation is due to the high LES tone. Balloon dilation of the LES relieves it.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-a2fba5a679.jpg\" alt=\"Balloon (pneumatic) dilation. A: a guidewire crosses the tight LES below a dilated esophagus. B and C: the balloon is inflated across the sphincter and stretches it open. D: food flows into the stomach again.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,722px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:722px\">Balloon (pneumatic) dilation. A: a guidewire crosses the tight LES below a dilated esophagus. B and C: the balloon is inflated across the sphincter and stretches it open. D: food flows into the stomach again. <small>· GI motility lecture, slide 15</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Worked example: the finding that does not fit achalasia</h4>\n<p>A 35-year-old has dysphagia for solids and liquids, a dilated upper esophagus and a tapered lower end. Uncoordinated peristalsis, only small pressure rises with swallowing, and slow passage of food into the stomach all fit. <b>Increased parasympathetic stimulation at the LES</b> does not: the nerves that open the LES are parasympathetic inhibitory neurons, and achalasia is their loss.</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead><tr><th></th><th>GERD</th><th>Achalasia</th><th>Esophageal spasm</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>Defect</td><td>LES too loose</td><td>LES too tight, fails to relax; weak lower peristalsis</td><td>Sudden uncoordinated contractions</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Main symptom</td><td>Heartburn, bad taste</td><td>Dysphagia for solids and liquids</td><td>Chest pain</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Best test</td><td>24-h pH monitor</td><td>Manometry</td><td>Manometry</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Treatment</td><td>PPI; fundoplication</td><td>Balloon, Botox, cut LES</td><td>CCB, nitroglycerin</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h4>Esophageal spasm</h4>\n<p>Sudden, unprovoked contractions of the esophagus cause chest pain. Manometry shows the spasms. Smooth-muscle relaxants treat it: CCBs and nitroglycerin.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-d5f48bdff8.jpg\" alt=\"Esophageal spasm on barium swallow: uncoordinated contractions pinch the barium column into beaded, &quot;corkscrew&quot; segments.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,570px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:570px\">Esophageal spasm on barium swallow: uncoordinated contractions pinch the barium column into beaded, \"corkscrew\" segments. <small>· GI motility lecture, slide 17</small></figcaption></figure>","s":"Swallowing and the esophagus","sn":2,"pn":"2.2"},{"l":"PHYS-C3","h":"Two regions and receptive relaxation","p":"<p>The stomach's extra inner <b>oblique</b> muscle layer (Chapter 1) adds churning, and its wall thickens from the fundus down to the antrum and pylorus. The myenteric and submucosal plexuses coordinate the churning. Its <b>rugae</b> (mucosal folds) flatten as it fills, giving an enormous potential space. For motility the stomach has two regions. The <b>orad</b> region (fundus and upper body) is thin and tonic; it receives and stores. The <b>caudad</b> region (lower body and antrum) is thick and phasic; it grinds, mixes and empties.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-81f50da153.jpg\" alt=\"The orad region (fundus and upper body) relaxes to store food; the caudad region (lower body and antrum) contracts to grind it and empty it.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,537px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">The orad region (fundus and upper body) relaxes to store food; the caudad region (lower body and antrum) contracts to grind it and empty it. <small>· Costanzo, Physiology, 6th ed., p. 352</small></figcaption></figure>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-1b7a29af93.jpg\" alt=\"The stomach&#x27;s three muscle layers peeled back: longitudinal outside, then circular, then the oblique layer innermost. Rugae line the inside; the pyloric sphincter opens into the duodenum.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,646px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:646px\">The stomach's three muscle layers peeled back: longitudinal outside, then circular, then the oblique layer innermost. Rugae line the inside; the pyloric sphincter opens into the duodenum. <small>· GI motility lecture, slide 22</small></figcaption></figure>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-65b2479fc9.jpg\" alt=\"An opened stomach: the rugae are deep mucosal folds that flatten out as the stomach fills.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,456px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">An opened stomach: the rugae are deep mucosal folds that flatten out as the stomach fills. <small>· GI motility lecture, slide 24</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Receptive relaxation</h4>\n<p>When a person swallows, the orad stomach relaxes together with the LES. This <b>receptive relaxation</b> lets the stomach go from about 50 mL to 1,500 mL with little rise in pressure. It is a <b>vago-vagal reflex</b> (Chapter 1). Mechanoreceptors sense stretch of the esophagus and stomach and signal the brainstem through vagal afferents. Vagal efferents return: preganglionic fibers release ACh onto enteric inhibitory neurons, which release <b>VIP and NO</b> onto the muscle. The fundus and upper body relax, so pressure stays low as volume rises. <b>Vagotomy</b> abolishes the reflex, so pressure climbs steeply as the stomach fills.</p>\n<p>The balance matters. A stomach that relaxed too much would never sense stretch, a main trigger for gastrin, so acid secretion would fall. One that could not relax would allow only tiny meals, and the person would starve. Receptive relaxation is what lets a competitive eater hold so much.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-613f70ffba.png\" alt=\"With receptive relaxation, pressure stays low until about 1.5 L. After vagotomy every extra milliliter raises pressure.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,468px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">With receptive relaxation, pressure stays low until about 1.5 L. After vagotomy every extra milliliter raises pressure. <small>· Graph redrawn from GI motility lecture, slide 26 (50 mL to 1,500 mL), and Costanzo p. 353 (schematic)</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Worked example: which motility event is vagal?</h4>\n<p>Of secondary esophageal peristalsis, the MMC (migrating myoelectric complex), slowing of emptying by duodenal fat, receptive relaxation and vomiting, only <b>receptive relaxation</b> is mediated by the vagus. Secondary peristalsis runs on the ENS, the MMC on motilin, the fat brake on CCK, and vomiting on the medullary vomiting center.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-a8cc0041bc.png\" alt=\"The vago-vagal reflex: stretch goes up the vagus, and the vagus sends back a relaxing signal carried by VIP and NO.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,464px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">The vago-vagal reflex: stretch goes up the vagus, and the vagus sends back a relaxing signal carried by VIP and NO. <small>· Flowchart from GI Physiology, Explained</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Worked example: the hot-dog eating champion</h4>\n<p>The agent that best helps someone eat a huge meal is <b>ACh</b>, the parasympathetic transmitter driving the vago-vagal reflex of receptive relaxation. NE is the sympathetic \"stop digesting\" signal and tightens sphincters; atropine blocks ACh; propranolol (a β-blocker) plays no part; motilin drives fasting contractions.</p>","s":"The stomach: receive, mix, empty","sn":3,"pn":"3.1"},{"l":"PHYS-C3","h":"Retropulsion: grinding in the antrum","p":"<h4>Mixing, grinding and retropulsion</h4>\n<p>The caudad stomach contracts at the basic electrical rhythm, about <b>3 per minute</b>. Each wave starts mid-body and strengthens toward the pylorus. Each wave pushes about 2–3 mL into the duodenum. But it also closes the pylorus, so most of the chyme is squirted back into the stomach. This backward flow is <b>retropulsion</b>: it grinds and mixes and keeps emptying slow. Only particles of 1 mm or less pass. Vagal stimulation raises the <b>force</b> of these contractions, not their frequency. Parasympathetic input, gastrin and motilin increase spikes and force; sympathetic input, secretin and GIP decrease them.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-f76d9f09ee.jpg\" alt=\"The regions in colour: fundus, body, and the antrum (orange) ending at the pylorus. The thick-walled antrum does the grinding; each wave closes the pylorus and throws chyme back.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,380px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">The regions in colour: fundus, body, and the antrum (orange) ending at the pylorus. The thick-walled antrum does the grinding; each wave closes the pylorus and throws chyme back. <small>· GI motility lecture, slide 21</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Worked example: after pyloric surgery</h4>\n<p>After surgery on the pyloric sphincter, the movement most affected while it heals is <b>retropulsion</b>, which needs the antral wave to close the pylorus. Receptive relaxation, segmentation and haustration do not involve the pylorus.</p>","s":"The stomach: receive, mix, empty","sn":3,"pn":"3.2"},{"l":"PHYS-C3","h":"Gastric emptying, gastroparesis and vagotomy","p":"<h4>Gastric emptying and what slows it</h4>\n<p>After a meal the stomach holds up to about 1.5 L. Emptying takes 30 minutes to 3 hours. Liquids empty before solids; carbohydrate and protein before fat; <b>fat is slowest</b>. Isotonic contents empty faster than hypotonic or hypertonic ones, and an isotonic liquid without calories is fastest of all. Acid in the stomach also slows emptying. The rate must stay slow enough for the duodenum to neutralize acid with pancreatic HCO₃⁻ (bicarbonate) and to digest and absorb fat.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-49919b1804.png\" alt=\"Schematic: a calorie-free isotonic liquid leaves within about 30 minutes; solids wait to be ground first; fatty or hypertonic meals take about 3 hours.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,503px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">Schematic: a calorie-free isotonic liquid leaves within about 30 minutes; solids wait to be ground first; fatty or hypertonic meals take about 3 hours. <small>· Graph redrawn from GI motility lecture, slide 28 (30 min to 3 h), and Costanzo p. 353 (schematic)</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p>The brakes sit in the duodenum, which senses four things: <b>stretch, acid, fat and high osmolarity</b>. <b>Fat</b> (fatty acids and monoglycerides) releases CCK from I cells; CCK is the main hormone that slows emptying (Chapter 2). <b>Acid</b> (H⁺) is sensed by H⁺ receptors in the duodenal mucosa. A reflex through myenteric interneurons then slows the stomach until pancreatic HCO₃⁻ neutralizes the acid. Stretch and pH changes also start the <b>enterogastric reflex</b>, which briefly inhibits gastrin, weakens gastric contractions and tightens the pyloric sphincter. GIP and secretin also inhibit emptying. Gastrin is the classic hormone that speeds the stomach.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-c16abd2234.png\" alt=\"Four duodenal signals converge on one result: weaker antral contractions and a tighter pylorus.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,498px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">Four duodenal signals converge on one result: weaker antral contractions and a tighter pylorus. <small>· Flowchart from GI Physiology, Explained</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p>In the intestine, gastrin, CCK, motilin and serotonin <b>increase intestinal motility</b>; secretin and glucagon decrease small-intestinal motility. So CCK slows the stomach but speeds the intestine.</p>\n<h4>Worked example: the fat-and-protein brake</h4>\n<p>The hormone released by fat and protein in the small intestine that strongly inhibits gastric emptying is <b>CCK</b>. GIP is the tempting choice: it also slows the stomach, but it answers glucose too, is the weaker brake, and its main job is insulin release. Gastrin speeds the stomach; CCK never does.</p>\n<h4>Worked example: Coke, brownie and pizza</h4>\n<p>The emptying order is <b>Coke, brownie, pizza</b>: liquid, then carbohydrate solid, then fatty solid held back by CCK.</p>\n<h4>Diabetic gastroparesis and vagotomy</h4>\n<p><b>Diabetic gastroparesis</b> is slow emptying from autonomic neuropathy. Damaged vagal afferents no longer sense distension, so the reflexes that relax and drive the stomach fail. Symptoms: bloating, nausea, pain, constipation, sometimes alternating with diarrhea. <b>Vagotomy</b> has similar effects. Without receptive relaxation, intragastric pressure rises, which can push liquids out too fast (<b>dumping syndrome</b>), while weak antral grinding delays emptying of solids. Other effects: diarrhea, and a sluggish gallbladder with cholestasis and gallstones.</p>\n<h4>Worked example: treating diabetic gastroparesis</h4>\n<p>The best drug is <b>erythromycin</b>, an antibiotic that binds motilin receptors and so triggers strong, MMC-like contractions that speed emptying. Every distractor slows the gut: NE (sympathetic), metoprolol (β-blocker), tricyclic antidepressants (anticholinergic) and diltiazem (CCB).</p>","s":"The stomach: receive, mix, empty","sn":3,"pn":"3.3"},{"l":"PHYS-C3","h":"The MMC and other intestinal reflexes","p":"<h4>The MMC and motilin</h4>\n<p>Between meals the gut runs a <b>housekeeping</b> program, the <b>MMC</b>. It begins about 90–120 minutes after eating and repeats about every 90 minutes while fasting. Each cycle is a band of intense contractions traveling from stomach to terminal ileum, mostly in the small bowel. It sweeps undigested residue and bacteria into the colon. The MMC is driven by <b>motilin</b>, released cyclically during fasting from Mo cells of the upper duodenum; each motilin peak sets off a sweep (motilin as a hormone: Chapter 2). Eating stops the MMC and restores the fed pattern of segmentation and peristalsis. Without MMCs, residue and bacteria stay behind, which favors bacterial overgrowth in the small intestine.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-60eabea998.png\" alt=\"Schematic: after the meal, mixing contractions continue for 1.5–2 hours. Then the gut falls quiet except for intense sweeps about every 90 minutes, each preceded by a rise in motilin.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,478px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">Schematic: after the meal, mixing contractions continue for 1.5–2 hours. Then the gut falls quiet except for intense sweeps about every 90 minutes, each preceded by a rise in motilin. <small>· Graph redrawn from GI motility lecture, slide 32, and Costanzo p. 353 (schematic)</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Worked example: contractions every 90 minutes between meals</h4>\n<p>Periodic contractions every 90 minutes during fasting are MMCs, mediated by <b>motilin</b>. Mass movements are the trap: colon, after meals, 1–3 times a day. Gastrin belongs to the fed state.</p>\n<p><b>Other intestinal reflexes</b>. Gastroileal: food in the stomach and gastric peristalsis → ileal peristalsis and the ileocecal valve opens. Intestino-intestinal: stretch of one segment → ↓ contraction of the intestine below it. Duodenocolic: duodenal stretch → myenteric plexus → faster colonic propulsion.</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead><tr><th>Pattern</th><th>Muscle or driver</th><th>Where, when</th><th>Job</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>Segmentation</td><td>Circular only</td><td>Small intestine, fed, ≤ 8–12/min</td><td>Mix</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Peristalsis</td><td>Circular + longitudinal, reciprocal</td><td>Esophagus, small intestine</td><td>Propel one way</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Retropulsion</td><td>Antral wave closes pylorus</td><td>Caudad stomach, 3/min</td><td>Grind; slow emptying</td></tr>\n<tr><td>MMC</td><td>Motilin</td><td>Stomach → ileum, fasting, every 90 min</td><td>Sweep residue, bacteria</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Haustration</td><td>Circular (segmentation)</td><td>Colon, about every 30 min</td><td>Mix; absorb water</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Mass movement</td><td>Strong peristalsis</td><td>Transverse → sigmoid colon, 1–3/day</td><td>Move feces far</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>","s":"The small intestine","sn":4,"pn":"4.2"},{"l":"PHYS-C3","h":"Colon movements, the gastrocolic reflex and defecation","p":"<p>Chyme enters the colon as a thin soup, as watery as urine; the colon absorbs water, so its contents become semisolid. Once chyme passes, the <b>ileocecal sphincter</b> contracts to stop reflux. <b>Haustration</b> is the colon's segmentation: circular contractions form saclike pouches, the <b>haustra</b>, which mix contents against the mucosa. In the cecum and proximal colon they occur about every 30 minutes and last about 1 minute each. <b>Mass movements</b> are strong peristaltic waves over 20 cm or more of the transverse and descending colon, 1–3 times a day. Triggers: stretch or irritation of the colon, local enteric reflexes, intense parasympathetic stimulation, and meals. A final mass movement fills the rectum, and that sudden stretch starts defecation.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-5b26f37b2a.jpg\" alt=\"The colon&#x27;s outpouchings are the haustra. Mass movements sweep contents from the transverse colon through the descending and sigmoid colon toward the rectum.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,570px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:570px\">The colon's outpouchings are the haustra. Mass movements sweep contents from the transverse colon through the descending and sigmoid colon toward the rectum. <small>· GI structure &amp; innervation lecture, slide 16</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p>The <b>gastrocolic reflex</b> (\"eat, then defecate\"): stretch of the stomach by a meal increases colonic motility and mass movements. Its afferent limb is parasympathetic; its efferent limb is carried by CCK and gastrin.</p>\n<p><b>Defecation</b>. As the rectum fills, the <b>rectosphincteric reflex</b> contracts the rectal wall and briefly relaxes the internal anal sphincter (smooth, involuntary). The external anal sphincter (striated, voluntary) stays closed. At about 25% of rectal capacity the urge appears. When appropriate, the external sphincter is relaxed voluntarily and the rectum contracts. A <b>Valsalva maneuver</b> (breathing out against a closed glottis) raises abdominal pressure to help.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-d90c168de2.png\" alt=\"From meal to defecation: the gastrocolic reflex brings a mass movement, rectal stretch relaxes the involuntary sphincter, and the voluntary sphincter has the last word.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,380px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">From meal to defecation: the gastrocolic reflex brings a mass movement, rectal stretch relaxes the involuntary sphincter, and the voluntary sphincter has the last word. <small>· Flowchart from GI Physiology, Explained</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p><b>IBS</b> (irritable bowel syndrome) is altered intestinal contraction. Contractions that are too strong and long cause gas, bloating and diarrhea; weak ones slow passage and give hard, dry stools. Other motility disorders: esophageal spasm, delayed gastric emptying, diarrhea.</p>\n<h4>Worked example: urge to defecate 20 minutes after a meal</h4>\n<p>Stomach stretch after a meal triggers the <b>gastrocolic reflex</b>, which increases mass movements. The rectosphincteric reflex is the trap: it relaxes the internal sphincter in response to rectal, not gastric, stretch.</p>\n<h4>Worked example: the commonest movement in the descending colon</h4>\n<p>The most frequent colonic movement is <b>haustration</b>, slow segmentation about every 30 minutes. Mass movements are stronger but occur only 1–3 times a day; retropulsion belongs to the stomach.</p>\n<h4>Do not confuse</h4>\n<p>Slow-wave frequency is fixed by the ICC; nerves, hormones and drugs change only spikes and force. Primary peristalsis needs the vagus; secondary peristalsis runs on the ENS. Receptive relaxation is vagal (VIP/NO) and happens while eating; the MMC is driven by motilin and happens only while fasting. GERD is a loose LES; achalasia is a tight LES that never relaxes. CCK slows the stomach but speeds the intestine.</p>","s":"The large intestine and defecation","sn":6,"pn":"6.1"},{"l":"PHYS-C4","h":"Two-step formation: why saliva ends up hypotonic","p":"<p>Saliva is <b>hypotonic</b>: its osmolarity (dissolved particles per liter) is lower than plasma's. Compared with plasma it has less Na⁺ (sodium) and Cl⁻ (chloride) and more K⁺ (potassium) and HCO₃⁻ (bicarbonate). So it is not filtered plasma; it is made in two steps.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-1a1a9a198a.jpg\" alt=\"Step 1: acinar cells secrete an isotonic fluid. Step 2: down the duct, ductal cells take out Na⁺ and Cl⁻ and add K⁺ and HCO₃⁻, so hypotonic saliva leaves. The small box marks the cell enlarged in the next figure.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,340px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">Step 1: acinar cells secrete an isotonic fluid. Step 2: down the duct, ductal cells take out Na⁺ and Cl⁻ and add K⁺ and HCO₃⁻, so hypotonic saliva leaves. The small box marks the cell enlarged in the next figure. <small>· Costanzo, Physiology, 6th ed., Fig. 8.12</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p><b>Step 1, acinus</b>: acinar cells secrete an <b>isotonic</b> fluid, the same strength as plasma (and as normal saline). Its Na⁺, K⁺, Cl⁻ and HCO₃⁻ concentrations are close to plasma values.</p>\n<p><b>Step 2, ducts</b>: ductal cells change the ions as the fluid passes. Their luminal membrane carries three exchangers: Na⁺–H⁺, Cl⁻–HCO₃⁻ and H⁺–K⁺. Their blood-side membrane has the Na⁺–K⁺ ATPase (the pump that moves Na⁺ out of the cell and K⁺ in) and Cl⁻ channels. Working together, these transporters <b>reabsorb Na⁺ and Cl⁻</b> and <b>secrete K⁺ and HCO₃⁻</b>. More NaCl leaves the saliva than KHCO₃ enters it, so the duct removes solute overall. Water would normally follow solute out, but the ducts are <b>impermeable to water</b>. The water stays behind, so the final saliva is hypotonic.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-4627abee64.jpg\" alt=\"One ductal cell. Luminal exchangers swap Na⁺ for H⁺, Cl⁻ for HCO₃⁻ and H⁺ for K⁺; the blood side has the Na⁺–K⁺ ATPase and a Cl⁻ channel. Net result: NaCl leaves the saliva, KHCO₃ enters, and no water moves.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,537px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">One ductal cell. Luminal exchangers swap Na⁺ for H⁺, Cl⁻ for HCO₃⁻ and H⁺ for K⁺; the blood side has the Na⁺–K⁺ ATPase and a Cl⁻ channel. Net result: NaCl leaves the saliva, KHCO₃ enters, and no water moves. <small>· Costanzo, Physiology, 6th ed., p. 357</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p>mEq/L (milliequivalents per liter) measures how much ionic charge a fluid carries per liter. Plasma Na⁺ is about 140 mEq/L and Cl⁻ about 110; resting saliva has only about 15 of each. Resting salivary pH is about 6–7.</p>\n<p>Being dilute has a purpose: flavor molecules leave the food, dissolve in saliva and reach the taste buds.</p>\n<h4>Worked example: why we can taste food</h4>\n<p>What mainly lets us taste food? <b>Low salivary osmolality</b>, for the reason just given. Saliva's Na⁺ is low, not high; its high K⁺ and its HCO₃⁻ are real but release no flavor; and it has no proteases.</p>\n<h4>Worked example: what ductal cells do</h4>\n<p>Ductal cells produce saliva whose <b>K⁺</b> is the highest relative to plasma of any ion. Contracting to eject saliva is the myoepithelial cells' job; making the isotonic first fluid is the acinar cells' job; no cell adds proteolytic enzymes.</p>","s":"How saliva is made","sn":1,"pn":"1.2"},{"l":"PHYS-C4","h":"Nerves only, and both divisions stimulate","p":"<p>Saliva is the only GI (gastrointestinal) secretion under purely <b>neural</b> control: no hormone sets its rate. It is also the only GI secretion that <b>both</b> autonomic divisions increase, although the parasympathetic division dominates.</p>\n<p><b>Parasympathetic</b> (main control): CN (cranial nerve) VII, facial, and CN IX, glossopharyngeal → ACh (acetylcholine) → <b>muscarinic</b> receptors on acinar and ductal cells → IP₃ (inositol trisphosphate) → ↑ intracellular Ca²⁺ (calcium) → a large volume of watery saliva, more HCO₃⁻ and enzymes, and myoepithelial contraction. ↑ by food, smell, taste, nausea and conditioning (Pavlov's dog); <b>citric acid</b> is the strongest stimulant. ↓ by sleep, dehydration and fear. <b>Atropine</b> blocks the muscarinic receptor and cuts the high-volume secretion.</p>\n<p><b>Sympathetic</b>: T1–T3 spinal segments → superior cervical ganglion → postganglionic fibers release NE (norepinephrine) → <b>β receptors</b> → adenylyl cyclase → cAMP (cyclic AMP) → a small volume of thick, mucus-rich saliva. This is why the mouth goes dry when you are nervous: fear turns down the parasympathetic flow, and the sympathetic adds only a little thick saliva.</p>\n<p><b>Aldosterone</b> changes composition, not rate. It acts on the ducts as on the kidney: more Na⁺ reabsorbed and more K⁺ secreted, so salivary Na⁺ falls further. Because the ducts are water-impermeable, little water follows the extra Na⁺.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-3384f45b0a.png\" alt=\"Both divisions increase secretion: the parasympathetic gives a large watery volume, the sympathetic a small mucus-rich one. Atropine blocks the parasympathetic arm at the muscarinic receptor.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,529px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">Both divisions increase secretion: the parasympathetic gives a large watery volume, the sympathetic a small mucus-rich one. Atropine blocks the parasympathetic arm at the muscarinic receptor. <small>· Flowchart from GI Physiology, Explained</small></figcaption></figure>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-e82c65cad2.jpg\" alt=\"Costanzo&#x27;s version, colour-coded by arm. Pink, parasympathetic: CN VII and IX, ACh on muscarinic (M) receptors, IP₃ and Ca²⁺; atropine blocks it; food, smell and nausea turn it up, dehydration, fear and sleep turn it down. Blue, sympathetic: T1–T3, NE on β receptors, cAMP. Both arms end in more saliva.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,532px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">Costanzo's version, colour-coded by arm. Pink, parasympathetic: CN VII and IX, ACh on muscarinic (M) receptors, IP₃ and Ca²⁺; atropine blocks it; food, smell and nausea turn it up, dehydration, fear and sleep turn it down. Blue, sympathetic: T1–T3, NE on β receptors, cAMP. Both arms end in more saliva. <small>· Costanzo, Physiology, 6th ed., Fig. 8.14</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Worked example: which nerves</h4>\n<p>Salivary secretion rate rises with parasympathetic input through <b>CN VII and IX</b>. CN X is wrong; so is \"decreased by both divisions\" (both increase it) and \"sympathetic stronger than parasympathetic\" (the parasympathetic dominates).</p>\n<h4>Worked example: what lowers saliva</h4>\n<p>Of ACh, atropine, bradykinin, citric acid and facial-nerve stimulation, only <b>atropine</b> decreases salivary secretion, because it blocks ACh at the muscarinic receptor. Sleep and dehydration also lower it. Citric acid, ACh and CN VII stimulation raise it, and bradykinin raises gland blood flow.</p>","s":"Control of secretion","sn":2,"pn":"2.1"},{"l":"PHYS-C5","h":"Gastric juice and the cells that make it","p":"<p>The stomach secretes acid, an enzyme precursor, intrinsic factor and mucus. This chapter follows how the parietal cell makes acid, how acid is switched on and off, and what happens when acid wins: peptic ulcers and Zollinger–Ellison syndrome.</p>\n<p>Gastric juice has four main parts: <b>HCl</b> (hydrochloric acid), <b>pepsinogen</b>, <b>intrinsic factor</b> and <b>mucus</b>. HCl and pepsinogen start protein digestion. Intrinsic factor is needed to absorb vitamin B₁₂. Mucus protects the lining from acid and lubricates the food.</p>\n<p><b>Body</b> (oxyntic glands): parietal (oxyntic) cells → HCl + intrinsic factor; chief (peptic) cells → pepsinogen (+ gastric lipase, Chapter 8); mucous neck cells → mucus, HCO₃⁻ (bicarbonate), some pepsinogen; ECL (enterochromaffin-like) cells → histamine. <b>Antrum</b> (pyloric glands, deeper pits): G cells → <b>gastrin, released into the blood</b>, not into the gland; mucous cells → mucus, HCO₃⁻. D cells → somatostatin. In a gland, mucous cells line the pit near the surface, parietal cells sit in the middle and chief cells lie near the base.</p>\n<p>Do not confuse ECL cells with <b>enterochromaffin (EC) cells</b>: enteroendocrine cells scattered along the gut lining that make <b>serotonin</b>, which acts on enteric neurons to adjust motility and secretion.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-7e075f2d35.jpg\" alt=\"One gastric gland. Mucous cells line the pit near the surface, parietal cells (purple) sit in the middle and chief cells crowd the base; a G cell and an ECL cell are drawn beside it.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,342px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">One gastric gland. Mucous cells line the pit near the surface, parietal cells (purple) sit in the middle and chief cells crowd the base; a G cell and an ECL cell are drawn beside it. <small>· Gastric secretion lecture, slide 10</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p>Parietal cells are packed with mitochondria, which supply ATP (adenosine triphosphate, the cell's energy currency) for the acid pump. Their membrane folds into deep channels called canaliculi, where acid is poured into the gland.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-91873ace49.jpg\" alt=\"A parietal cell. Its apical membrane folds into deep canaliculi that pour acid into the gland, and its cytoplasm is packed with mitochondria to power the pump.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,418px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">A parietal cell. Its apical membrane folds into deep canaliculi that pour acid into the gland, and its cytoplasm is packed with mitochondria to power the pump. <small>· Gastric secretion lecture, slide 6</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Worked example: matching cells to secretions</h4>\n<p>Only \"S cells → secretin\" is correct (duodenum; Chapter 2). Each wrong pair swaps a product: trypsinogen comes from pancreatic acinar cells, not mucous cells; intrinsic factor from parietal cells, not chief cells; gastrin from G cells; pepsinogen from chief cells.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-bedb2cc440.jpg\" alt=\"Parietal and chief cells sit in the body; G cells and mucous cells sit in the antrum. Note that gastrin leaves the G cell into the circulation, not into the lumen.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,537px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">Parietal and chief cells sit in the body; G cells and mucous cells sit in the antrum. Note that gastrin leaves the G cell into the circulation, not into the lumen. <small>· Costanzo, Physiology, 6th ed., Fig. 8.15</small></figcaption></figure>","s":"Gastric juice and acid","sn":1,"pn":"1.1"},{"l":"PHYS-C5","h":"How the parietal cell makes HCl","p":"<p>Parietal cells acidify the gastric contents to <b>pH 1–2</b> (pH falls as H⁺, hydrogen ion, concentration rises). The job of this acid is to turn inactive pepsinogen into active pepsin. The cell does it in four steps.</p>\n<p><b>Step 1, inside the cell.</b> CO₂ (carbon dioxide) from the cell's metabolism and from blood joins water to form carbonic acid. The enzyme <b>carbonic anhydrase</b> speeds this up. Carbonic acid then splits into H⁺ and HCO₃⁻. In symbols: CO₂ + H₂O ⇌ H₂CO₃ ⇌ H⁺ + HCO₃⁻.</p>\n<p><b>Step 2, apical (lumen) side.</b> The <b>H⁺/K⁺ ATPase</b>, or proton pump, splits ATP to push H⁺ into the lumen and pull K⁺ (potassium) into the cell. Both ions move against their gradients, so this is primary active transport. K⁺ leaks back to the lumen through K⁺ channels and is reused. Cl⁻ (chloride) follows H⁺ out through apical Cl⁻ channels, so the product is HCl.</p>\n<p><b>Step 3, basolateral (blood) side.</b> Every H⁺ pumped out leaves one HCO₃⁻ behind. A <b>Cl⁻/HCO₃⁻ exchanger</b> (AE2, anion exchanger 2) moves that HCO₃⁻ into the blood and brings Cl⁻ in. This Cl⁻ is the supply for the apical Cl⁻ channel. A Na⁺/K⁺ ATPase (sodium–potassium pump) on the same side keeps cell Na⁺ low.</p>\n<p><b>Step 4, the net result.</b> HCl goes to the lumen and HCO₃⁻ goes to the blood. After a meal, gastric venous blood therefore turns alkaline: the <b>alkaline tide</b>. The pancreas later secretes this HCO₃⁻ into the duodenum to neutralize the same acid, and pancreatic venous blood turns acidic while it does (Chapter 6).</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-e8d10889f7.jpg\" alt=\"Follow one CO₂: carbonic anhydrase splits it into H⁺, pumped into the lumen in exchange for K⁺, and HCO₃⁻, swapped into the blood for Cl⁻. The Cl⁻ then leaves through the apical channel to pair with H⁺.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,537px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">Follow one CO₂: carbonic anhydrase splits it into H⁺, pumped into the lumen in exchange for K⁺, and HCO₃⁻, swapped into the blood for Cl⁻. The Cl⁻ then leaves through the apical channel to pair with H⁺. <small>· Costanzo, Physiology, 6th ed., Fig. 8.17</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Worked example: which blood has the highest pH?</h4>\n<p>Choices: cerebral artery, gastric vein, pancreatic vein, portal vein, renal artery. The <b>gastric vein</b> wins because it carries the alkaline tide. The pancreatic veins have the lowest pH, because duct cells send H⁺ into the blood as they secrete HCO₃⁻.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-cd38413b62.jpg\" alt=\"The same steps in a simpler colour drawing: carbonic anhydrase inside the cell, the H⁺/K⁺ pump (blue) on the lumen side, and the Cl⁻/HCO₃⁻ exchanger (pink) sending HCO₃⁻ toward the capillary. The label marks the pump that proton pump inhibitors block.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,570px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:570px\">The same steps in a simpler colour drawing: carbonic anhydrase inside the cell, the H⁺/K⁺ pump (blue) on the lumen side, and the Cl⁻/HCO₃⁻ exchanger (pink) sending HCO₃⁻ toward the capillary. The label marks the pump that proton pump inhibitors block. <small>· Gastric secretion lecture, slide 17</small></figcaption></figure>","s":"Gastric juice and acid","sn":1,"pn":"1.2"},{"l":"PHYS-C5","h":"Phases of acid secretion","p":"<p>Acid secretion during a meal falls into three phases. In the first two the vagus nerve acts by two routes. The <b>direct</b> route releases ACh onto parietal cells. The <b>indirect</b> route releases GRP onto G cells, whose gastrin travels in the blood to the parietal cells.</p>\n<p><b>Cephalic phase (~30%, \"anticipatory\")</b>: smell, taste, chewing, swallowing and conditioned reflexes act through the brain and the vagus, using both routes.</p>\n<p><b>Gastric phase (~60%, \"reactionary\")</b>: the largest, with four mechanisms. First and second, distention stretches the wall. Stretch receptors send signals up vagal afferents to the brain stem, which answers down vagal efferents. Both limbs run in the vagus, so this is a <b>vago-vagal reflex</b> (Chapter 1), and it again uses ACh on parietal cells and GRP on G cells. Third, stretch of the antrum triggers local ENS (enteric nervous system) reflexes that release gastrin. Fourth, <b>amino acids and small peptides</b> act directly on G cells. Alcohol and caffeine also stimulate acid.</p>\n<p>During a meal the same vago-vagal reflex also relaxes the orad stomach so the meal fits. That relaxation is carried by VIP (vasoactive intestinal peptide) and NO (nitric oxide), as Chapter 3 explains.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-a56ca8ca73.png\" alt=\"Trace each phase to the parietal cell. The vagus feeds both the direct ACh route and the GRP → G cell → gastrin route; the dotted arrow is ACh releasing histamine. Low antral pH brakes the G cell.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,760px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:760px\">Trace each phase to the parietal cell. The vagus feeds both the direct ACh route and the GRP → G cell → gastrin route; the dotted arrow is ACh releasing histamine. Low antral pH brakes the G cell. <small>· Flowchart from GI Physiology, Explained</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p><b>Intestinal phase (~10%, \"finishing touches\")</b>: products of protein digestion in the duodenum add a little more acid.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-8daf595812.jpg\" alt=\"The same two vagal routes on the stomach: ACh straight onto parietal cells, and GRP onto antral G cells, whose gastrin reaches the parietal cells through the circulation. Distention and amino acids feed in at the antrum; the table gives each phase&#x27;s share, stimuli and route.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,760px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:760px\">The same two vagal routes on the stomach: ACh straight onto parietal cells, and GRP onto antral G cells, whose gastrin reaches the parietal cells through the circulation. Distention and amino acids feed in at the antrum; the table gives each phase's share, stimuli and route. <small>· Costanzo, Physiology, 6th ed., Fig. 8.19</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Worked example: a distended stomach</h4>\n<p>Which agents raise HCl secretion by acting on parietal cells during distention? <b>ACh, gastrin and histamine all do.</b> Stretch triggers vagal ACh release and vagal plus local gastrin release, and both then release histamine. Choosing \"gastrin and histamine, not ACh\" forgets the vago-vagal reflex.</p>\n<h4>Turning acid off</h4>\n<p>Food is itself a buffer. While a meal sits in the stomach, much of the secreted H⁺ is soaked up and the pH falls only partly. When chyme moves to the duodenum, buffering drops and the pH falls steeply. <b>Low pH then inhibits gastrin release</b>, directly and by making D cells release somatostatin. This negative feedback stops acid once it is no longer needed to activate pepsin. Vagal stimulation does the opposite: it reduces somatostatin release. From the duodenum, CCK stimulates somatostatin release, and secretin and GIP (glucose-dependent insulinotropic peptide) also brake acid (Chapter 2).</p>\n<h4>Before PPIs: vagotomy</h4>\n<p>Ulcers were once treated surgically. A <b>truncal vagotomy</b> cut the anterior and posterior vagal trunks, removing vagal ACh and GRP drive. It was often combined with an <b>antrectomy</b>, removal of the antrum and its G cells, joining the stomach to the duodenum (Billroth I). Vagotomy also slows gastric emptying (Chapter 3).</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-5742643ec6.jpg\" alt=\"A: cutting the vagal trunks removes nerve drive; removing the antrum (dashed line) removes the G cells. B: the remaining stomach is joined to the duodenum.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,537px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">A: cutting the vagal trunks removes nerve drive; removing the antrum (dashed line) removes the G cells. B: the remaining stomach is joined to the duodenum. <small>· Gastric secretion lecture, slide 22</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Worked example: after complete vagotomy</h4>\n<p>Which function is fully abolished? <b>Primary esophageal peristalsis</b>, which swallowing triggers through vagal pathways (Chapter 3). Gastrin release survives (amino acids act on G cells directly; antral stretch works through local reflexes), and so does histamine release, because gastrin still reaches ECL cells. Slow waves start in the gut wall itself.</p>","s":"Phases, pepsin and intrinsic factor","sn":3,"pn":"3.1"},{"l":"PHYS-C5","h":"The mucosal barrier and NSAIDs","p":"<p>Why does the stomach not digest itself? Mucous cells secrete <b>mucus</b>, a gel that covers the epithelium, and <b>HCO₃⁻</b>, which is trapped in the gel. H⁺ that enters the gel meets HCO₃⁻ and becomes CO₂ and water, and pepsin is inactivated in this alkaline zone. So the pH is about 2 in the lumen but about 7 at the cell surface. <b>Mucosal blood flow</b> supplies O₂ (oxygen) and HCO₃⁻ and carries away H⁺ and toxins. Growth factors speed repair. Part of the HCO₃⁻ that parietal cells send into the blood rides the mucosal capillaries up to the surface cells, which add it to the protective layer.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-15c1212ccc.jpg\" alt=\"HCO₃⁻ from the mucous cells is trapped in the mucus layer, so the pH climbs from about 2 in the lumen to about 7 at the cell surface.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,537px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">HCO₃⁻ from the mucous cells is trapped in the mucus layer, so the pH climbs from about 2 in the lumen to about 7 at the cell surface. <small>· Gastric secretion lecture, slide 27</small></figcaption></figure>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-238a4fb591.jpg\" alt=\"The barrier in colour, lumen at the top. H⁺ (green) in the lumen activates pepsinogen (1 → 2). HCO₃⁻ from the surface cells and from the blood below (blue arrows; the alkaline tide) meets H⁺ in the mucus and becomes H₂O + CO₂, so the pH scale at right climbs from 1.5 to 7.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,608px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:608px\">The barrier in colour, lumen at the top. H⁺ (green) in the lumen activates pepsinogen (1 → 2). HCO₃⁻ from the surface cells and from the blood below (blue arrows; the alkaline tide) meets H⁺ in the mucus and becomes H₂O + CO₂, so the pH scale at right climbs from 1.5 to 7. <small>· Gastric secretion lecture, slide 9</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p><b>Prostaglandins</b> (mainly PGE₂) hold the barrier together: ↑ mucus, ↑ HCO₃⁻, ↑ blood flow (vasodilation), ↓ acid (Gi blunts histamine's cAMP). Gastric <b>stretch</b> makes prostaglandins. So the same stretch that calls up acid and pepsin also makes the prostaglandins that make the mucus that protects against that acid and pepsin.</p>\n<h4>Worked example: duodenal cell turnover</h4>\n<p>Why are duodenal enterocytes replaced every few days (Chapter 7)? <b>Acid, proteolytic enzymes and mechanical wear all damage them</b>, so \"all of the above\" is right.</p>\n<h4>NSAIDs</h4>\n<p><b>NSAIDs</b> (nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs, e.g., aspirin) block <b>COX</b> (cyclooxygenase), the enzyme that turns arachidonic acid into prostaglandins. COX-1 is \"constitutive\", always on; its prostaglandins protect the gastric mucosa and activate platelets. COX-2 is \"inducible\"; its prostaglandins cause pain, inflammation and fever. Ordinary NSAIDs block both, and COX-2 inhibitors block only COX-2. Without PGE₂, mucus, HCO₃⁻ and blood flow fall and the brake on acid is lost, so acid \"cooks\" the lining.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-d3757f773a.png\" alt=\"NSAIDs hit both branches, so they relieve pain but also strip the stomach&#x27;s protection. COX-2 inhibitors spare the COX-1 branch.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,490px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">NSAIDs hit both branches, so they relieve pain but also strip the stomach's protection. COX-2 inhibitors spare the COX-1 branch. <small>· Flowchart from GI Physiology, Explained</small></figcaption></figure>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-239a5c2576.jpg\" alt=\"The lecture&#x27;s colour version of the same pathway. COX-1 prostaglandins protect the gastric mucosa and support platelet hemostasis; COX-2 prostaglandins mediate pain, inflammation and fever. NSAIDs (green) block both enzymes.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,608px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:608px\">The lecture's colour version of the same pathway. COX-1 prostaglandins protect the gastric mucosa and support platelet hemostasis; COX-2 prostaglandins mediate pain, inflammation and fever. NSAIDs (green) block both enzymes. <small>· Gastric secretion lecture, slide 42</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Worked example: back pain pills and stomach pain</h4>\n<p>A woman takes regular medication for back pain and develops abdominal pain. Mechanism: <b>decreased mucus production</b>, because NSAIDs cut prostaglandins and the mucosal barrier breaks down. \"Excess acid secretion\" is close, since losing PGE₂ does raise acid, but barrier loss is what exposes the epithelium.</p>","s":"The mucosal barrier and peptic ulcers","sn":4,"pn":"4.1"},{"l":"PHYS-C5","h":"Peptic ulcers and Helicobacter pylori","p":"<p>A <b>peptic ulcer</b> is an erosion of gastric or duodenal mucosa by H⁺ and pepsin. The name misleads: pepsin does not cause ulcers by itself. An ulcer needs loss of the barrier, too much H⁺ and pepsin, or both: an imbalance between protective and damaging factors (\"no acid, no ulcer\"). <b>Protective</b>: mucus, HCO₃⁻, prostaglandins, blood flow, growth factors. <b>Damaging</b>: H⁺, pepsin, <i>Helicobacter pylori</i>, NSAIDs, stress (head trauma, burns, ventilators/intubation), smoking, alcohol, cancer, IBD (inflammatory bowel disease). Diagnosis: upper endoscopy (EGD, esophagogastroduodenoscopy) with biopsy.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-83c194d40d.jpg\" alt=\"The balance behind every peptic ulcer: H⁺ and pepsin attack a barrier of HCO₃⁻ and mucus. The columns list what protects the mucosa and what damages it.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,760px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:760px\">The balance behind every peptic ulcer: H⁺ and pepsin attack a barrier of HCO₃⁻ and mucus. The columns list what protects the mucosa and what damages it. <small>· Costanzo, Physiology, 6th ed., Fig. 8.20</small></figcaption></figure>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-f8b1c546f8.jpg\" alt=\"A chronic peptic ulcer in a resected stomach: a punched-out crater with the mucosal folds radiating to its edge.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,456px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">A chronic peptic ulcer in a resected stomach: a punched-out crater with the mucosal folds radiating to its edge. <small>· Gastric secretion lecture, slide 34</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Helicobacter pylori</h4>\n<p><b><i>H. pylori</i></b> is a gram-negative bacterium that lives in gastric mucus, often in the antrum. It survives the acid with <b>urease</b>. Urease splits urea into NH₃ (ammonia) and CO₂, and NH₃ grabs H⁺ to form NH₄⁺ (ammonium). This neutralizes the acid around the bacterium, so it can bind the epithelium instead of being shed. The ammonia is safe for the bug but harmful to the host: it impairs mitochondrial respiration and kills mucosal cells. Cytotoxins (CagA) and mucinase destroy the mucus and epithelium, and recruited leukocytes add inflammation.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-423a61c8cf.jpg\" alt=\"Infection in four steps: (1) the bacteria swim through the gastric fluid into the mucus; (2) urease protects them from the acid; (3) they multiply on the epithelium; (4) mucinase, cytotoxins and ammonia damage the mucosa, and leukocytes are drawn in.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,646px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:646px\">Infection in four steps: (1) the bacteria swim through the gastric fluid into the mucus; (2) urease protects them from the acid; (3) they multiply on the epithelium; (4) mucinase, cytotoxins and ammonia damage the mucosa, and leukocytes are drawn in. <small>· Gastric secretion lecture, slide 36</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p>It causes ulcers by two routes. A <b>gastric ulcer</b> is direct: the barrier breaks down where the bacteria live. A <b>duodenal ulcer</b> is indirect. Infection suppresses somatostatin from antral D cells, so gastrin and acid rise (\"inhibition of inhibition\"). Infection that spreads to the duodenum also cuts duodenal HCO₃⁻ secretion. More acid arrives and less buffer meets it.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-e8b3496121.png\" alt=\"Left branch: direct damage gives a gastric ulcer. Right branches: less somatostatin raises acid while duodenal infection lowers HCO₃⁻, giving a duodenal ulcer.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,520px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">Left branch: direct damage gives a gastric ulcer. Right branches: less somatostatin raises acid while duodenal infection lowers HCO₃⁻, giving a duodenal ulcer. <small>· Flowchart from GI Physiology, Explained</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p><b>Diagnosis</b>: biopsy, stool antigen, serology, or the <b>¹³C-urea breath test</b>. The patient drinks urea labeled with carbon-13, a heavy, nonradioactive carbon. Urease, if present, splits off the label as ¹³CO₂, which is absorbed, exhaled and measured. No <i>H. pylori</i> means no urease and no ¹³CO₂. <b>Treatment</b>: PPI + clarithromycin + amoxicillin.</p>\n<h4>Worked example: surviving the acid</h4>\n<p>A curved organism in a gastric biopsy survives the acid by <b>ammonia neutralizing H⁺</b>. It does not inhibit proton pumps or raise prostaglandins, and it uses urease rather than inhibiting it.</p>","s":"The mucosal barrier and peptic ulcers","sn":4,"pn":"4.2"},{"l":"PHYS-C5","h":"Gastritis, and gastric versus duodenal ulcers","p":"<h4>Worked example: chronic antral gastritis</h4>\n<p>A 45-year-old man has three years of vague pain and nausea, not relieved by antacids; endoscopy shows antral redness without ulcer or mass, and biopsy shows chronic nonspecific gastritis. Answer: <b><i>H. pylori</i> infection</b>. Zollinger–Ellison syndrome (ZES) would produce ulcers, and pernicious anemia is an autoimmune gastritis of the gastric body, not the antrum. Serum gastrin would be raised, because infection lowers somatostatin.</p>\n<h4>Worked example: duodenal ulcer with a positive breath test</h4>\n<p>A 59-year-old woman has burning epigastric pain that eases with food or antacids. Serology and the ¹³C-urea breath test are positive, and endoscopy shows a duodenal ulcer. She gets antibiotics to clear <i>H. pylori</i> plus omeprazole, which lowers the H⁺ load reaching the duodenum. Food helps because it buffers acid.</p>\n<h4>Gastric versus duodenal ulcers</h4>\n<p><b>Duodenal ulcers</b> are more common. They form because H⁺ secretion is high; gastrin rises more after meals, and its trophic effect enlarges the parietal cell mass. <b>Gastric ulcers</b> form because the barrier fails. Net H⁺ secretion is actually low, because H⁺ leaks back into the damaged mucosa, and gastrin is high because less acid means less feedback. A gastric ulcer can become cancer (about 4%), so it <b>must be biopsied</b> and rescoped to confirm healing.</p>\n<p>Where <i>H. pylori</i> inflames the acid-secreting body of the stomach, the inflammation also suppresses the parietal cells directly, which lowers acid further.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-923426dc46.jpg\" alt=\"Typical sites: gastric ulcers along the stomach&#x27;s curvatures, and a duodenal ulcer in the first part of the duodenum.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,456px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">Typical sites: gastric ulcers along the stomach's curvatures, and a duodenal ulcer in the first part of the duodenum. <small>· Gastric secretion lecture, slide 39</small></figcaption></figure>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-f3217e9824.jpg\" alt=\"Endoscopic view of a gastric ulcer: a sharply punched-out crater in reddened gastric mucosa. Because about 4% of gastric ulcers are cancers, every one is biopsied.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,418px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">Endoscopic view of a gastric ulcer: a sharply punched-out crater in reddened gastric mucosa. Because about 4% of gastric ulcers are cancers, every one is biopsied. <small>· Gastric secretion lecture, slide 40</small></figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead><tr><th></th><th>Gastric ulcer</th><th>Duodenal ulcer</th><th>Zollinger–Ellison</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>H⁺ secretion</td><td>↓ (leaks back)</td><td>↑</td><td>↑↑</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Gastrin</td><td>↑ (less H⁺ feedback)</td><td>↑ after meals</td><td>↑↑ from tumor</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Main cause</td><td>barrier loss: <i>H. pylori</i> (direct), NSAIDs</td><td>acid excess: <i>H. pylori</i> (indirect)</td><td>gastrinoma</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Key point</td><td>~4% cancer: biopsy</td><td>parietal mass ↑</td><td>large, multiple, distal ulcers</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>","s":"The mucosal barrier and peptic ulcers","sn":4,"pn":"4.3"},{"l":"PHYS-C5","h":"ZES cases and look-alikes","p":"<h4>Worked example: oily diarrhea and a pancreatic mass</h4>\n<p>A 47-year-old man has abdominal pain eased by eating and antacids, oily diarrhea and a duodenal-bulb ulcer. Gastrin is 800 pg/mL (picograms per milliliter; normal 0–130), basal H⁺ is 100 mEq/h (normal 10), pentagastrin adds no acid, and secretin raises gastrin to 1,100: a <b>gastrinoma</b>, here a 3-cm pancreatic-head mass. Food eased his pain because it buffers acid. Omeprazole controls acid until surgery, and removing the tumor heals the ulcer.</p>\n<h4>Worked example: reading gastrin and acid together</h4>\n<p>The correct statement: <b>increased acid with elevated gastrin indicates ZES</b>. H₂ blockers are not preferred for metastatic gastrinoma (lifelong PPI is), and they do not lower gastrin. PPIs do affect gastrin: they <b>raise</b> it by removing acid feedback.</p>\n<h4>Worked example: a hyperactive gut peptide</h4>\n<p>A 51-year-old man has oily, foul-smelling stool and a hypertrophied gastric mucosa; a mutation makes one gut peptide tenfold more active. Answer: <b>gastrin</b>, because mucosal hypertrophy is its trophic signature; CCK, GIP, secretin and histamine do not thicken the gastric mucosa. The steatorrhea comes from acid <b>inactivating lipase</b> (the same result orlistat gets by blocking lipase), not from a faster colon or from amylase or trypsinogen.</p>\n<h4>Do not confuse</h4>\n<p>High gastrin with <b>high acid</b> = ZES. High gastrin with <b>low acid</b> = lost feedback: gastric ulcer, acid-suppressing drugs, or the atrophic gastritis of pernicious anemia. Gastrin reaches parietal cells through the blood; histamine diffuses locally. ACh and gastrin use IP₃/Ca²⁺, histamine uses cAMP, and somatostatin and PGE₂ use Gi. Atropine cannot block vagal gastrin release, because that synapse uses GRP.</p>","s":"Zollinger–Ellison syndrome","sn":5,"pn":"5.3"},{"l":"PHYS-C6","h":"Acinar enzymes and acute pancreatitis","p":"<p>Acinar cells secrete enzymes for all three food groups, and these enzymes are essential for life. <b>Amylase</b> and <b>lipase</b> leave the cell already active. The proteases leave as inactive precursors called <b>zymogens</b>: trypsinogen, chymotrypsinogen, proelastase and procarboxypeptidases A and B. <b>Colipase</b> also leaves inactive, as procolipase, and so does phospholipase A₂ (their jobs in fat digestion: Chapter 8).</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-767fd7b1bb.jpg\" alt=\"Inside an acinar cell (scanning electron micrograph, false colour, about 2200×): red zymogen granules hold the stored enzymes; the yellow stacks at the bottom are the Golgi that packed them.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,418px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">Inside an acinar cell (scanning electron micrograph, false colour, about 2200×): red zymogen granules hold the stored enzymes; the yellow stacks at the bottom are the Golgi that packed them. <small>· Pancreatic &amp; biliary secretion lecture, slide 17</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p>The pancreas is made of protein, not of bread or butter. Active amylase and lipase find no starch or fat to attack in the gland, so they are safe. (Mnemonic: a gingerbread man could not secrete active amylase; it would digest itself.) Active proteases would digest the pancreas itself. So each zymogen carries an extra piece, a <b>negative regulatory domain</b>, that blocks its active site until it is cut off.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-1f65e538fd.jpg\" alt=\"The lecture&#x27;s gingerbread man, bitten in half: a cookie made of starch could not hold active amylase. The pancreas is protein, so only its proteases must be kept inactive.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,228px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">The lecture's gingerbread man, bitten in half: a cookie made of starch could not hold active amylase. The pancreas is protein, so only its proteases must be kept inactive. <small>· Pancreatic &amp; biliary secretion lecture, slide 10</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p>Three safeguards keep activation out of the gland. First, the zymogens are stored and shipped inactive. Second, acinar cells pack a <b>trypsin inhibitor</b> into the granules to neutralize any trypsin that forms early. Third, the trigger enzyme lives only in the duodenum. <b>Enterokinase</b> (enteropeptidase), a duodenal brush-border enzyme, cuts trypsinogen into <b>trypsin</b>. Trypsin then activates the other zymogens, procolipase and prophospholipase A₂ included, plus more trypsinogen (autocatalysis). Trypsin is the master switch and the most abundant pancreatic protease: without it, every protease acts as if missing.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-bd9410d027.png\" alt=\"Enterokinase makes the first trypsin in the duodenum; trypsin switches on everything else, including more of itself. The dashed path is acute pancreatitis.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,510px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">Enterokinase makes the first trypsin in the duodenum; trypsin switches on everything else, including more of itself. The dashed path is acute pancreatitis. <small>· Flowchart from GI Physiology, Explained</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Worked example: which enzyme has a negative regulatory domain?</h4>\n<p>Choices: chymotrypsinogen, salivary amylase, lingual lipase, pancreatic amylase, pancreatic lipase. Only <b>chymotrypsinogen</b> is a zymogen. The amylases and lipases are all secreted active.</p>\n<h4>Worked example: acute pancreatitis</h4>\n<p>After an alcohol binge, trauma or a gallstone blocking the duct, a patient has epigastric pain, vomiting and high serum amylase and lipase. The mechanism is <b>premature activation of trypsinogen</b> inside the pancreas: trypsin activates the other zymogens there, and they digest the gland and the tissue around it. Amylase and lipase are markers, not the cause (they are secreted active anyway), and chymotrypsin and elastase are activated by trypsin, not the reverse. <i>H. pylori</i> (<i>Helicobacter pylori</i>) causes ulcers, not pancreatitis (Chapter 5).</p>","s":"The exocrine pancreas","sn":1,"pn":"1.2"},{"l":"PHYS-C6","h":"Pancreatic insufficiency: chronic pancreatitis and cystic fibrosis","p":"<p><b>Chronic pancreatitis</b> is a dead, burnt-out pancreas (classically from alcohol). Fibrosis and cell death leave <b>no enzymes</b>. Serum amylase and lipase therefore drop (they rise only in acute pancreatitis). No trypsin is released, so every protease fails and protein digestion fails too. Duct cells are lost too, so secretin releases markedly <b>decreased HCO₃⁻</b>. Undigested fat gives oily, floating stools (<b>steatorrhea</b>), weight loss, and poor absorption of vitamins A, D, E and K.</p>\n<p>The <b>IV (intravenous) secretin stimulation test</b> uses this. Secretin is injected into a vein and duodenal juice is collected. A normal pancreas answers with a massive HCO₃⁻ release; a chronically damaged one releases little.</p>\n<h4>Worked example: oily floating stools, alcohol overuse, weight loss, equivocal celiac tests</h4>\n<p>The most specific test for chronic pancreatitis is the <b>secretin stimulation test</b>. Biopsy is too invasive; vitamin A, D, E, K levels fall with any fat malabsorption; CCK levels may be normal. Endoscopic ultrasound is the most sensitive imaging for early changes, and ERCP (endoscopic retrograde cholangiopancreatography) images the ducts. Cystic fibrosis and pancreatic cancer can also blunt the secretin response.</p>\n<p><b>Cystic fibrosis (CF)</b> is a CFTR defect. Without CFTR, Cl⁻ cannot return to the duct lumen, the Cl⁻/HCO₃⁻ exchanger stalls, and little HCO₃⁻ or water is secreted. Thick secretions <b>clog the ducts</b>. The acini still make enzymes, but the enzymes cannot flow out, and the blocked gland becomes cystic and fibrotic. The result mimics chronic pancreatitis: fat and vitamin A, D, E, K malabsorption. CF is the <b>opposite of cholera</b>: cholera toxin keeps CFTR open and floods the gut with fluid (Chapter 9), while CF shuts it.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-f4efaf4adf.jpg\" alt=\"The 2026 discussion&#x27;s drawing of the whole system. CCK acts on the acinar cells (enzymes), secretin on the centroacinar and duct cells. In the duct cell, the red-circled CFTR returns Cl⁻ to the lumen for the Cl⁻/HCO₃⁻ exchanger (blue); Na⁺ follows between the cells (red box). In CF this channel fails.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,722px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:722px\">The 2026 discussion's drawing of the whole system. CCK acts on the acinar cells (enzymes), secretin on the centroacinar and duct cells. In the duct cell, the red-circled CFTR returns Cl⁻ to the lumen for the Cl⁻/HCO₃⁻ exchanger (blue); Na⁺ follows between the cells (red box). In CF this channel fails. <small>· Discussion session (saliva, stomach, pancreas), 2026, slide 37</small></figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead><tr><th></th><th>Chronic pancreatitis</th><th>Cystic fibrosis</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>Enzymes</td><td>Not made</td><td>Made, not released</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Ducts</td><td>Dry, burnt out</td><td>Plugged, cystic</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Image</td><td>No beer left</td><td>Beer tap blocked</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>","s":"Pancreatic juice and its control","sn":2,"pn":"2.3"},{"l":"PHYS-C6","h":"Bile salts at work","p":"<p>Bile salts are <b>amphipathic</b>: the fat-soluble side touches fat, while the charged glycine or taurine side faces water. So they do two jobs: they emulsify large fat blobs into small droplets for lipase, and they carry the digestion products, with vitamins A, D, E and K, in micelles (both in Chapter 8). <b>Phospholipids</b> are amphipathic too and help form micelles. <b>Cholesterol</b> in bile is the body's main route for excreting cholesterol; it does not aid digestion. <b>Bilirubin</b>, from hemoglobin breakdown, gives bile and stool their color (Chapter 9).</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-4468d24f66.jpg\" alt=\"Bile salts at work, top to bottom: pancreatic lipase digests the dietary fat, and the products ride in micelles, each a ball of bile salts with their water-loving heads facing out, down through the unstirred layer to the brush border.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,304px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">Bile salts at work, top to bottom: pancreatic lipase digests the dietary fat, and the products ride in micelles, each a ball of bile salts with their water-loving heads facing out, down through the unstirred layer to the brush border. <small>· Lipid digestion &amp; absorption lecture, slide 19</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Worked example: which statement about bile is true?</h4>\n<p>True: bile salts <b>emulsify</b> large triglyceride droplets. Bile is not an enzyme and catalyzes nothing. Emulsification <i>increases</i> droplet surface area. More than 95% of bile salts are recycled, not lost in feces.</p>","s":"Bile and bile salts","sn":3,"pn":"3.2"},{"l":"PHYS-C6","h":"Enterohepatic circulation and losing the ileum","p":"<p>Bile salts are recycled between gut and liver. They work in the duodenum and jejunum, where most fat is absorbed by mid-jejunum, and stay in the lumen when the lipids enter the cells. In the <b>terminal ileum</b>, a <b>Na⁺–bile salt cotransporter</b> carries them out of the lumen, and they pass into portal blood. The liver extracts them and secretes them again. Bacteria deconjugate some on the way, so the circuit carries bile acids as well as bile salts. Uptake at the far end of the small intestine keeps bile salts present along its whole length.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-d85bc3041d.jpg\" alt=\"Follow the red line: bile flows from the liver down the common bile duct (filling the gallbladder on the way) into the duodenum, travels the small intestine, leaves at the terminal ileum and returns in the hepatic portal vein to the liver.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,494px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">Follow the red line: bile flows from the liver down the common bile duct (filling the gallbladder on the way) into the duodenum, travels the small intestine, leaves at the terminal ileum and returns in the hepatic portal vein to the liver. <small>· Lipid digestion &amp; absorption lecture, slide 33</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p>The pool is only about <b>2.5 g</b>, yet the liver secretes about <b>24 g/day</b>, so the pool cycles several times a day. More than <b>95%</b> is reabsorbed in the terminal ileum. About <b>600 mg/day</b> (also quoted as 0.4 g/day), roughly a quarter of the pool, is lost in stool, and new synthesis replaces exactly that. Returning bile salts inhibit 7α-hydroxylase, so less return means more synthesis. Returning bile salts also stimulate bile secretion.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-7e8533d286.png\" alt=\"The bile-salt loop: about 24 g/day circulates from a 2.5 g pool; more than 95% is reclaimed in the ileum, and the ≈ 600 mg/day lost in stool is newly made.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,508px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">The bile-salt loop: about 24 g/day circulates from a 2.5 g pool; more than 95% is reclaimed in the ileum, and the ≈ 600 mg/day lost in stool is newly made. <small>· Flowchart from GI Physiology, Explained</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Worked example: which step belongs to the enterohepatic circulation?</h4>\n<p>Correct: bile salts move from the <b>intestinal lumen into blood on Na⁺–bile salt cotransporters</b> (terminal ileum). They are not taken up in the duodenum, and portal blood carries them from the ileum, not from the bile duct.</p>\n<h4>Worked example: distal ileum removed</h4>\n<p>Crohn disease or cancer surgery removes the terminal ileum (with or without the colon); the patient already gets B₁₂ injections. Bile acids escape in stool, so the liver <b>raises bile-acid synthesis</b>, and cholesterol synthesis to feed it, but cannot keep pace: the pool shrinks, too few micelles form, and the result is <b>steatorrhea</b> with fat-soluble-vitamin (e.g., vitamin A) deficiency, not toxicity. The pancreas is intact, so protein digestion is normal. Bile acids reaching an intact colon also trigger cAMP-driven Cl⁻ secretion, with Na⁺ and water following (<b>bile-acid diarrhea</b>), but this secretory diarrhea is a secondary effect, not the best answer.</p>\n<p>Normally no bile acids reach the colon. <b>Cholestyramine</b>, a resin that binds bile acids, stops bile-acid diarrhea but not the steatorrhea, since bound bile acids are lost too. The same resin lowers LDL (low-density lipoprotein) cholesterol, because the liver uses cholesterol to replace the lost bile acids. Loss of the ileum also blocks vitamin B₁₂ uptake (Chapter 9).</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-51ec7bd1b3.png\" alt=\"Ileal resection. The solid line ends in steatorrhea, the best answer. Dashed boxes are side effects: bile-acid diarrhea (only while the colon remains) and B₁₂ loss (Chapter 9).\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,513px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">Ileal resection. The solid line ends in steatorrhea, the best answer. Dashed boxes are side effects: bile-acid diarrhea (only while the colon remains) and B₁₂ loss (Chapter 9). <small>· Flowchart from GI Physiology, Explained</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Do not confuse</h4>\n<p>Acute pancreatitis: trypsin activated inside the gland; serum amylase and lipase ↑. Chronic pancreatitis: gland burnt out; enzymes, HCO₃⁻ and serum enzymes ↓. CF: enzymes made but trapped. Emulsion droplets (bile salts coating fat, before lipase) are not micelles (bile salts plus digestion products, after lipase). Bile salts are reabsorbed in the terminal ileum, not the duodenum.</p>\n<p>The lecture slides use the word loosely: they call the emulsified fat droplets \"microdroplet micelles\" that lipase then digests. Strictly, micelles form only from lipase's products.</p>","s":"Bile flow and recycling","sn":4,"pn":"4.2"},{"l":"PHYS-C7","h":"Digestion, absorption and the absorptive surface","p":"<p>This chapter follows starch, sugars and protein from the lumen into the blood: how enzymes cut them into small pieces, and how the intestinal cells pull those pieces in.</p>\n<h4>Digestion and absorption</h4>\n<p><b>Digestion</b> is the chemical breakdown of food into molecules small enough to absorb. The enzymes come from saliva, the stomach (pepsin), the pancreas (amylase, lipase, trypsin and the other proteases) and the brush border of the small intestine. We need only <b>one amylase</b>: pancreatic amylase alone can digest all the starch, and salivary amylase is a bonus (Chapter 4). Fat likewise needs essentially one lipase, pancreatic lipase (Chapter 8). Protein is different: it needs a whole team of proteases, like a butcher who needs many knives for one cow.</p>\n<p><b>Absorption</b> is the movement of nutrients, water and electrolytes from the lumen into the blood. In the <b>cellular (transcellular) path</b>, a substance crosses the apical (luminal) membrane, passes through the cytoplasm, and leaves across the basolateral membrane into blood. It may cross each membrane by simple diffusion, on a transporter, or inside vesicles after binding a receptor (transcytosis). In the <b>paracellular path</b>, it slips between cells through the tight junctions and the lateral intercellular spaces into blood. Sugars and amino acids take the cellular path on transporters.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-5ad141992d.jpg\" alt=\"Transcellular path: through SGLT1 or GLUT5, across the cell, out through GLUT2. Paracellular path: through the junction and the lateral space.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,537px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">Transcellular path: through SGLT1 or GLUT5, across the cell, out through GLUT2. Paracellular path: through the junction and the lateral space. <small>· Carbohydrate &amp; protein digestion lecture, slide 7</small></figcaption></figure>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-816d8d30dc.jpg\" alt=\"The ways across the lining. Through the cell (transcellular): simple diffusion and transcytotic vesicles (green) or a transporter (purple). Between the cells (paracellular): through the tight junction (orange).\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,494px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">The ways across the lining. Through the cell (transcellular): simple diffusion and transcytotic vesicles (green) or a transporter (purple). Between the cells (paracellular): through the tight junction (orange). <small>· Carbohydrate &amp; protein digestion lecture, slide 5</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>The absorptive surface</h4>\n<p>The small-intestinal lining is built to give enzymes and transporters a huge surface. <b>Folds of Kerckring</b> (circular folds of the mucosa, named for a 17th-century Dutch anatomist) triple the area of a smooth tube. Fingerlike <b>villi</b> project from the folds and add another 10-fold. Villi are longest in the duodenum, where most digestion and absorption happen, and shortest in the terminal ileum. Each villus is covered by absorptive cells (<b>enterocytes</b>) with mucus-secreting goblet cells between them. Its core holds capillaries, which drain to the hepatic portal vein and the liver, and a central lymph vessel, the <b>lacteal</b>, which carries absorbed fat (Chapter 8).</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-ec707cb37e.jpg\" alt=\"Left: villi on the circular folds; the portal vein carries absorbed sugars and amino acids to the liver. Right: one villus with its capillary bed and central lacteal.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,537px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">Left: villi on the circular folds; the portal vein carries absorbed sugars and amino acids to the liver. Right: one villus with its capillary bed and central lacteal. <small>· Carbohydrate &amp; protein digestion lecture, slide 11</small></figcaption></figure>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-9956de01b0.jpg\" alt=\"The circular folds of Kerckring seen through an endoscope: ridges that ring the wall all the way around the lumen.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,342px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">The circular folds of Kerckring seen through an endoscope: ridges that ring the wall all the way around the lumen. <small>· Carbohydrate &amp; protein digestion lecture, slide 9</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p>The apical membrane of each enterocyte is folded into tiny <b>microvilli</b>, which add another 20-fold. Under the microscope they look like bristles, so this surface is called the <b>brush border</b>. It carries the final digestive enzymes (disaccharidases, peptidases, enterokinase) and the nutrient transporters. Altogether 3 × 10 × 20 gives a <b>600-fold</b> increase: more than 200 m² (square meters) of absorptive surface.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-154140daea.jpg\" alt=\"Three magnifications: villi (left), the enterocytes covering one villus (middle), and the microvilli on their apical surface (right). The brush border is made of microvilli; villi are the much larger projections of the whole mucosa.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,684px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:684px\">Three magnifications: villi (left), the enterocytes covering one villus (middle), and the microvilli on their apical surface (right). The brush border is made of microvilli; villi are the much larger projections of the whole mucosa. <small>· Carbohydrate &amp; protein digestion lecture, slide 13</small></figcaption></figure>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-2220f701e9.png\" alt=\"Each structure multiplies the area of the one before: folds ×3, villi ×10 more, microvilli ×20 more, for ×600 in all. The y-axis is logarithmic.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,501px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">Each structure multiplies the area of the one before: folds ×3, villi ×10 more, microvilli ×20 more, for ×600 in all. The y-axis is logarithmic. <small>· Graph redrawn from Digestion &amp; absorption discussion (2026), slides 23–24 (surface-area table: folds ×3, villi ×10, microvilli ×20)</small></figcaption></figure>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-36fbaaeec6.jpg\" alt=\"The same steps as drawn in the 2026 discussion: the intestine as a smooth tube, then folds of Kerckring, villi and microvilli. The table gives the area each step reaches (×3, ×30, ×600).\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,684px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:684px\">The same steps as drawn in the 2026 discussion: the intestine as a smooth tube, then folds of Kerckring, villi and microvilli. The table gives the area each step reaches (×3, ×30, ×600). <small>· Discussion session (digestion &amp; absorption), 2026, slide 23</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p>Enterocytes are replaced every 3–5 days, one of the fastest turnovers in the body. In the duodenum the lining takes constant wear: acid arriving from the stomach, digestive enzymes and the mechanical rub of chyme all damage the cells, so crypt stem cells keep replacing them. Shed cells are one reason stool keeps forming even during fasting. Fast turnover also makes the mucosa vulnerable to radiation and chemotherapy. When villi are flattened, as in celiac disease, the surface collapses and absorption of everything suffers (Chapter 8).</p>","s":"The absorbing lining","sn":1,"pn":"1.1"},{"l":"PHYS-C7","h":"Fructose and GLUT2: why both carriers","p":"<h4>Fructose: just a door</h4>\n<p>Fructose has no Na⁺ partner. GLUT5 is simply a door: a carrier protein that speeds fructose across the membrane but adds no energy. That is what facilitated diffusion means: carrier-assisted, but downhill only. So fructose, unlike glucose and galactose, <b>cannot be absorbed against its gradient</b>. It leaves on GLUT2, also downhill. Without any carrier, a sugar would have to cross by unassisted (simple) diffusion through the lipid membrane, which is far too slow to matter.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-cc43e2d0c0.jpg\" alt=\"Fructose just gets a door: GLUT5 on the lumen side lets it in and GLUT2 on the blood side lets it out. No pump and no Na⁺, so it can only move downhill.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,456px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">Fructose just gets a door: GLUT5 on the lumen side lets it in and GLUT2 on the blood side lets it out. No pump and no Na⁺, so it can only move downhill. <small>· Carbohydrate &amp; protein digestion lecture, slide 28</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Worked example: why the enterocyte needs both SGLT1 and GLUT2</h4>\n<p>Glucose absorption needs both because of a division of labor: <b>SGLT1 drives glucose from the lumen into the cell against its gradient</b>, and <b>GLUT2 carries it out into blood with its gradient</b>. Wrong choices swap the jobs or say GLUT2 brings fructose in; fructose enters on GLUT5. GLUT2 sits on the basolateral membrane, not in the brush border.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-8d05c06e55.jpg\" alt=\"Two doors in, one door out: glucose and galactose enter on SGLT1 with Na⁺, fructose on GLUT5, and all three leave through GLUT2 on the basolateral surface.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,494px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">Two doors in, one door out: glucose and galactose enter on SGLT1 with Na⁺, fructose on GLUT5, and all three leave through GLUT2 on the basolateral surface. <small>· Carbohydrate &amp; protein digestion lecture, slide 20</small></figcaption></figure>","s":"Carbohydrates","sn":2,"pn":"2.3"},{"l":"PHYS-C7","h":"Lactase deficiency and α-glucosidase inhibitors","p":"<p><b>Lactase deficiency</b> (lactose intolerance) is the commonest failure of carbohydrate digestion. Brush-border lactase is low or absent, often because its activity fades after childhood (lactase non-persistence). Milk lactose is then not split into glucose and galactose. A disaccharide has no transporter, so lactose cannot be absorbed anywhere in the gut. It stays in the lumen, holds water there and feeds colon bacteria. The result is <b>osmotic diarrhea</b>, <b>flatus</b>, bloating and cramping after milk products (mechanism in Chapter 9). Only lactase is missing; the other brush-border enzymes work normally. Treatment: stop milk products, or take lactase tablets with them.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-5bf92ada3c.jpg\" alt=\"Lactase splits lactose, with water, into glucose and galactose, two absorbable monosaccharides.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,304px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">Lactase splits lactose, with water, into glucose and galactose, two absorbable monosaccharides. <small>· Carbohydrate &amp; protein digestion lecture, slide 21</small></figcaption></figure>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-aea4a5b19e.png\" alt=\"Undigested lactose causes symptoms two ways: it holds water in the lumen, and colon bacteria ferment it into gas and extra osmotic particles.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,456px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">Undigested lactose causes symptoms two ways: it holds water in the lumen, and colon bacteria ferment it into gas and extra osmotic particles. <small>· Flowchart from GI Physiology, Explained</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Worked example: lactose intolerance and frozen yogurt</h4>\n<p>A lactose-intolerant patient stops milk and cheese but keeps eating frozen yogurt. Expect <b>resolution of all symptoms in 1–2 days</b>. The symptoms are purely osmotic and fermentative, so they stop once the lactose load clears; no damaged mucosa needs 1–2 months to heal. Yogurt is tolerated because its live bacterial cultures carry their own lactase and digest much of its lactose.</p>\n<h4>Worked example: why no weight loss</h4>\n<p>Lactose intolerance does not cause weight loss because <b>fat, protein and the other disaccharides are still absorbed</b>; only lactose is lost. \"Galactose has no caloric content\" is wrong: galactose yields as much energy as glucose.</p>\n<h4>Worked example: what does not happen in lactose intolerance</h4>\n<p>Lactase non-persistence at the jejunal brush border, bloating and pain, and bacterial breakdown of lactose into osmotically active solutes all occur. What does <b>not</b> happen is absorption of undigested lactose by ileal cells: the intestine absorbs only monosaccharides.</p>\n<h4>α-Glucosidase inhibitors</h4>\n<p><b>Acarbose</b> and <b>miglitol</b> are diabetes drugs that inhibit the brush-border <b>α-glucosidases</b>, the maltase-like enzymes that split maltose, maltotriose, α-limit dextrins and sucrose. Less glucose is freed and absorbed after a meal, so blood glucose rises less. But the undigested carbohydrate travels on to the colon, exactly like lactose in lactase deficiency, where it pulls water and feeds bacteria.</p>\n<h4>Worked example: why acarbose and miglitol failed</h4>\n<p>These drugs do lower post-meal glucose, so \"no decrease in serum glucose\" is wrong. Patients abandon them because of <b>flatus and diarrhea</b>, the predictable result of drug-induced carbohydrate malabsorption.</p>","s":"Carbohydrates","sn":2,"pn":"2.4"},{"l":"PHYS-C7","h":"Pancreatic proteases and the trypsin cascade","p":"<h4>Pancreatic proteases</h4>\n<p>The pancreas secretes its five proteases as inactive <b>zymogens</b>: trypsinogen, chymotrypsinogen, proelastase and procarboxypeptidases A and B. In the duodenal lumen, brush-border <b>enterokinase</b> turns trypsinogen into <b>trypsin</b>, and trypsin activates all the others, including more trypsinogen. Trypsin is the most abundant pancreatic protease. Activation and its diseases belong to Chapter 6. In chronic pancreatitis and cystic fibrosis the proteases never work in the lumen, so protein is poorly digested and lost in stool. <b>Acute pancreatitis</b> (alcohol, trauma, a gallstone blocking the duct) is premature trypsin activation inside the gland.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-4a43d38193.jpg\" alt=\"Top: in the stomach, low pH turns pepsinogen into pepsin. Bottom: in the small intestine, brush-border enterokinase makes the first trypsin, and trypsin then activates all five zymogens, including more trypsinogen.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,684px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:684px\">Top: in the stomach, low pH turns pepsinogen into pepsin. Bottom: in the small intestine, brush-border enterokinase makes the first trypsin, and trypsin then activates all five zymogens, including more trypsinogen. <small>· Costanzo, Physiology, 6th ed., Fig. 8.28</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p>The pancreatic proteases leave free amino acids, dipeptides, tripeptides and longer <b>oligopeptides</b>, which are too big to absorb. <b>Brush-border peptidases</b> (amino-oligopeptidases and dipeptidases) cut oligopeptides into amino acids, dipeptides and tripeptides. The last step is <b>intracellular</b>: most di- and tripeptides are absorbed whole and split into amino acids by cytoplasmic peptidases inside the enterocyte.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-503d38c59c.jpg\" alt=\"Oligopeptides are trimmed by brush-border peptidases; small peptides are split by intracellular peptidases; free amino acids (neutral, basic, acidic) enter on carriers; all leave as amino acids into the capillary.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,430px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">Oligopeptides are trimmed by brush-border peptidases; small peptides are split by intracellular peptidases; free amino acids (neutral, basic, acidic) enter on carriers; all leave as amino acids into the capillary. <small>· Carbohydrate &amp; protein digestion lecture, slide 54</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Worked example: one enzyme missing, all missing</h4>\n<p>Deficiency of <b>trypsin</b> looks like deficiency of every pancreatic protease, because trypsin activates all the zymogens. Missing chymotrypsin, elastase or one carboxypeptidase removes only that enzyme; and chymotrypsin and elastase never activate trypsin.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-663bcccc09.jpg\" alt=\"Pancreatic juice arrives with amylase and lipase already active but the proteases inactive. Enteropeptidase (enterokinase) on the duodenal lining activates trypsin, and trypsin activates the other proenzymes.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,418px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">Pancreatic juice arrives with amylase and lipase already active but the proteases inactive. Enteropeptidase (enterokinase) on the duodenal lining activates trypsin, and trypsin activates the other proenzymes. <small>· Carbohydrate &amp; protein digestion lecture, slide 60</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Worked example: which enzyme is an exopeptidase?</h4>\n<p><b>Carboxypeptidase B</b> (like A) clips single amino acids from the C-terminal end. Trypsin, chymotrypsin and elastase are endopeptidases. Enterokinase is the tempting wrong answer: it is the brush-border activator of trypsinogen, not a digestive exopeptidase.</p>","s":"Digesting proteins","sn":3,"pn":"3.2"},{"l":"PHYS-C8","h":"The pancreatic enzymes","p":"<p>Most fat digestion happens in the small intestine. The pancreas supplies <b>pancreatic lipase</b>, its helper <b>colipase</b>, <b>cholesterol ester hydrolase</b> and <b>phospholipase A₂</b>. Each enzyme strips fatty acids off a big lipid, so the pieces become small enough to enter the cell.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-ffc9b1f190.png\" alt=\"Each pancreatic enzyme cuts fatty acids off one kind of dietary lipid. The products on the right, plus free fatty acids (FFA), are what micelles carry.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,472px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">Each pancreatic enzyme cuts fatty acids off one kind of dietary lipid. The products on the right, plus free fatty acids (FFA), are what micelles carry. <small>· Flowchart from GI Physiology, Explained</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Pancreatic lipase</h4>\n<p><b>Pancreatic lipase</b> is secreted already <b>active</b>. It cuts the fatty acids at positions 1 and 3 of the glycerol backbone, so each triglyceride yields two free fatty acids and one <b>2-monoglyceride</b> (the middle fatty acid stays on). Lipase works best at pH 6–8. Below about pH 5 acid inactivates it, and below pH 3 it is destroyed (\"acid cooks the lipase\"). So lipase depends on pancreatic HCO₃⁻ (bicarbonate) to neutralize gastric acid. <b>Orlistat</b>, a weight-loss drug, blocks gastric and pancreatic lipase, so undigested fat passes into the stool.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-a928e1fa3d.jpg\" alt=\"Lipase takes the two outer fatty acids (red chains) off the glycerol backbone (blue). The middle chain stays on, so one monoglyceride and two free fatty acids result.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,418px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">Lipase takes the two outer fatty acids (red chains) off the glycerol backbone (blue). The middle chain stays on, so one monoglyceride and two free fatty acids result. <small>· Lipid digestion &amp; absorption lecture, slide 5</small></figcaption></figure>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-ff68af01b2.jpg\" alt=\"Pancreatic lipase adds water across the ester bonds at positions 1 and 3, releasing two &quot;free&quot; fatty acids. The fatty acid at position 2 stays on glycerol as a 2-monoglyceride.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,537px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">Pancreatic lipase adds water across the ester bonds at positions 1 and 3, releasing two \"free\" fatty acids. The fatty acid at position 2 stays on glycerol as a 2-monoglyceride. <small>· Lipid digestion &amp; absorption lecture, slide 7</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Why lipase needs colipase</h4>\n<p>Here is the catch. Lipase must sit on the surface of a fat droplet to reach its substrate. That surface is crowded with bile salts, and bile salts <b>push lipase off</b> the oil–water interface. So bile salts, which make digestion possible, also block the enzyme. <b>Colipase</b> solves this. It binds lipase and anchors it to the droplet, shoving the bile salts aside like bodyguards clearing a path. With colipase in place, lipase can touch the fat and do its job.</p>\n<p>Colipase is secreted inactive, as <b>procolipase</b> (101 amino acids). In the lumen, <b>trypsin</b> cuts off the first five residues, and the rest (residues 6–101) is active colipase. The small cut-off peptide is <b>enterostatin</b>, which <b>decreases hunger and fat intake</b>; it works whether given peripherally or into the brain. So the same cut that switches on fat digestion also sends a \"stop eating fat\" signal.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-cebf5d0bba.png\" alt=\"Trypsin splits procolipase into two useful pieces: colipase, which anchors lipase, and enterostatin, a satiety signal.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,380px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">Trypsin splits procolipase into two useful pieces: colipase, which anchors lipase, and enterostatin, a satiety signal. <small>· Flowchart from GI Physiology, Explained</small></figcaption></figure>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-69d0336279.jpg\" alt=\"The same cut on the lecture slide: trypsin cuts procolipase after residue 5. The short green piece (enterostatin, residues 1–5) signals to regulate fat intake; the long blue piece (colipase, residues 6–101) lets lipase digest triglyceride.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,380px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">The same cut on the lecture slide: trypsin cuts procolipase after residue 5. The short green piece (enterostatin, residues 1–5) signals to regulate fat intake; the long blue piece (colipase, residues 6–101) lets lipase digest triglyceride. <small>· Lipid digestion &amp; absorption lecture, slide 8</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Worked example: what lowers hunger?</h4>\n<p>The product that decreases the sense of hunger is <b>enterostatin</b>, cut from procolipase by trypsin. Amylase, lysolecithin, ileal bile-salt reabsorption and duodenal acid neutralization are real digestive events, but none is a satiety signal.</p>\n<h4>Cholesterol ester hydrolase</h4>\n<p><b>Cholesterol ester hydrolase</b> is secreted <b>active</b>. It pulls the fatty acid off cholesterol ester, giving free cholesterol plus a fatty acid, small enough to absorb. It can also split triglyceride ester bonds, releasing glycerol.</p>\n<h4>Phospholipase A₂</h4>\n<p>A phospholipid is glycerol with fatty acids at positions 1 and 2 and a phosphate head group at position 3. <b>Phospholipase A₂</b> (PLA₂) removes only the fatty acid at <b>position 2</b>, hence its name. What is left is a lysophospholipid; from lecithin (phosphatidylcholine), the main dietary phospholipid, it is <b>lysolecithin</b> (1-acylglycerophosphorylcholine). PLA₂ is secreted as an inactive proenzyme and is <b>activated by trypsin</b>. Other phospholipases cut elsewhere: A₁ at position 1, C on the glycerol side of the phosphate, D on the head-group side.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-91843ec0ad.jpg\" alt=\"Phospholipase A₂ adds water at the position-2 ester bond (red). The fatty acid there (R₂) leaves, and a lysophospholipid remains.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,380px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">Phospholipase A₂ adds water at the position-2 ester bond (red). The fatty acid there (R₂) leaves, and a lysophospholipid remains. <small>· Lipid digestion &amp; absorption lecture, slide 16</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p>PLA₂ also works inside cells. It frees arachidonic acid from membrane phospholipids; cyclo-oxygenase turns this into prostaglandins and lipoxygenase into leukotrienes. Mechanical strain stimulates this PLA₂, and lipocortin inhibits it.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-fef17a75d8.jpg\" alt=\"Dashed arrows mark where each phospholipase cuts. Phospholipase A₂ cuts at position 2 (bold arrow), leaving lysolecithin plus a free fatty acid.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,537px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">Dashed arrows mark where each phospholipase cuts. Phospholipase A₂ cuts at position 2 (bold arrow), leaving lysolecithin plus a free fatty acid. <small>· Lipid digestion &amp; absorption lecture, slide 15</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Worked example: is PLA₂ a zymogen?</h4>\n<p>Yes: PLA₂ is secreted inactive and <b>trypsin</b> activates it. The tempting answer is enterokinase, but enterokinase (a brush-border enzyme) activates only trypsinogen, and trypsin then activates the other zymogens (Chapter 6). PLA₂ does not simply wait for a basic pH.</p>","s":"Breaking fat down","sn":1,"pn":"1.2"},{"l":"PHYS-C8","h":"Re-esterification and chylomicrons","p":"<p>Inside the enterocyte, the pieces are reassembled on the <b>smooth endoplasmic reticulum</b> (ER): monoglyceride + 2 FFA → triglyceride; cholesterol + FFA → cholesterol ester; lysolecithin + FFA → phospholipid. This <b>re-esterification</b> is the unpacking in the new house. It also keeps free products scarce inside the cell, so diffusion inward continues.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-db5aef540a.png\" alt=\"Inside the enterocyte: the digestion products diffuse in, are rebuilt in the smooth ER, packed into chylomicrons with apoB-48, and leave into the lacteal. FFA = free fatty acid; ER = endoplasmic reticulum.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,380px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">Inside the enterocyte: the digestion products diffuse in, are rebuilt in the smooth ER, packed into chylomicrons with apoB-48, and leave into the lacteal. FFA = free fatty acid; ER = endoplasmic reticulum. <small>· Flowchart from GI Physiology, Explained</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p>The rebuilt lipids are packed into <b>chylomicrons</b>, the moving trucks: everything is inside, but nothing can be used until it is unloaded later. A chylomicron is about 1,000 Å across, twenty times a micelle. Its core holds triglycerides and cholesterol esters. Its covering is <b>80% phospholipid and 20% apoprotein</b>. Overall it is triglycerides 85–92%, phospholipids 6–12%, cholesterol 1–3% and protein 1–2%. The essential apoprotein is <b>apoB-48</b> (apoprotein B-48), made in the intestinal cell itself; apoproteins A, C-II, C-III and E also sit on the surface. <b>No apoB, no absorption.</b></p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-88acc31295.jpg\" alt=\"A chylomicron: a core of triglycerides and cholesteryl esters, a shell of phospholipids and cholesterol, and apoproteins (B-48, C-II, C-III) on the surface.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,430px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">A chylomicron: a core of triglycerides and cholesteryl esters, a shell of phospholipids and cholesterol, and apoproteins (B-48, C-II, C-III) on the surface. <small>· Lipid digestion &amp; absorption lecture, slide 27</small></figcaption></figure>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-78a9df4cd0.png\" alt=\"Composition of the lipoproteins (bars are approximate readings of the original graph). The chylomicron is mostly triglyceride with the least protein; HDL is about half protein. VLDL, LDL, HDL = very-low-, low- and high-density lipoprotein.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,520px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">Composition of the lipoproteins (bars are approximate readings of the original graph). The chylomicron is mostly triglyceride with the least protein; HDL is about half protein. VLDL, LDL, HDL = very-low-, low- and high-density lipoprotein. <small>· Graph redrawn from Lipids lecture, slide 28 (composition of chylomicron, VLDL, LDL, HDL; values read off the bar graph)</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p>The Golgi packs chylomicrons into secretory vesicles, which fuse with the basolateral membrane (<b>exocytosis</b>). Sugars and amino acids enter the villus blood capillaries, but chylomicrons are far too large for them. Instead they squeeze between the endothelial cells of the <b>lacteal</b>, the lymph capillary in the center of each villus. Lymph carries them to the cisterna chyli and up the <b>thoracic duct</b> (first described in humans by Thomas Bartholin), which empties into the great veins of the neck. So absorbed long-chain fat <b>bypasses the liver</b> and reaches the systemic blood first.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-45040c985f.jpg\" alt=\"Lymph (green) collects in the cisterna chyli in the abdomen and climbs the thoracic duct (white arrow) to empty into the great veins at the base of the neck (blue). This is the road every chylomicron takes to the blood.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,494px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">Lymph (green) collects in the cisterna chyli in the abdomen and climbs the thoracic duct (white arrow) to empty into the great veins at the base of the neck (blue). This is the road every chylomicron takes to the blood. <small>· Lipid digestion &amp; absorption lecture, slide 24</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p><b>Short- and medium-chain fatty acids</b> (fewer than about 12 carbons) behave differently. They are water-soluble enough to skip re-esterification and pass straight from the enterocyte into <b>portal blood</b>, as glycerol does. That is why medium-chain fat is given to patients who cannot absorb ordinary fat.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-1219c24997.jpg\" alt=\"The whole route in five numbered steps: (1) micelles hold the products in the lumen; (2) the lipids diffuse into the cell while bile salts stay behind; (3) re-esterification; (4) packing with apo B into chylomicrons; (5) exocytosis into lymph. Chol E = cholesterol ester; MG = monoglyceride; LysoPL = lysolecithin; PL = phospholipid.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,760px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:760px\">The whole route in five numbered steps: (1) micelles hold the products in the lumen; (2) the lipids diffuse into the cell while bile salts stay behind; (3) re-esterification; (4) packing with apo B into chylomicrons; (5) exocytosis into lymph. Chol E = cholesterol ester; MG = monoglyceride; LysoPL = lysolecithin; PL = phospholipid. <small>· Costanzo, Physiology, 6th ed., Fig. 8.32</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Worked example: where is fat re-esterified?</h4>\n<p>Re-esterification happens in the <b>intestinal epithelial cells</b>. Micelles only ferry digestion products, and lacteals and the thoracic duct only carry finished chylomicrons; villous capillaries never receive them.</p>\n<h4>Abetalipoproteinemia</h4>\n<p>In this rare autosomal recessive disease no <b>apoB</b>-containing lipoproteins are made: the protein that loads lipid onto apoB, MTP (microsomal triglyceride transfer protein), is missing. So enterocytes <b>cannot package chylomicrons</b> or export them into lymph. Fat is digested and taken up, but it piles up inside the intestinal cells, causing steatorrhea and poor absorption of fat-soluble vitamins. Apolipoproteins, which bind lipids to form lipoproteins, are made mainly in the liver and partly in the small intestine.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-1e291491e5.jpg\" alt=\"The chylomicron coat with its apoproteins in colour. ApoB (magenta) is the one the intestinal cell must make; without it no chylomicron forms, and fat stays trapped in the cell.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,418px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">The chylomicron coat with its apoproteins in colour. ApoB (magenta) is the one the intestinal cell must make; without it no chylomicron forms, and fat stays trapped in the cell. <small>· Lipid digestion &amp; absorption lecture, slide 26</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Worked example: abetalipoproteinemia</h4>\n<p>The defining defect: intestinal cells <b>unable to package chylomicrons</b>. Fat and vitamin absorption are not normal, apoproteins are missing, not overexpressed, and apoB coats the chylomicron surface rather than enclosing lipids in the lumen.</p>","s":"Absorbing fat","sn":2,"pn":"2.2"},{"l":"PHYS-C8","h":"Too few bile salts","p":"<h4>Too few bile salts</h4>\n<p><b>No bile = minimal fat absorbed</b>, because without bile salts there is no emulsification and no micelles. Bile-duct obstruction stops delivery. The pool also depends on recycling: more than 95% of bile salts return from the <b>terminal ileum</b> (Chapter 6). So <b>ileal resection</b> or ileal disease (for example, Crohn disease) shrinks the pool and causes steatorrhea, even though the liver raises bile-acid synthesis (worked example in Chapter 6). Losing the ileum also costs vitamin B₁₂ absorption (Chapter 9).</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-145767fe5c.jpg\" alt=\"The bile-salt loop: about 24 g/day is secreted into the duodenum, and more than 95% returns from the ileum in portal blood. Only the small fecal loss (0.4 g/day) is replaced by new synthesis, so losing the ileum drains the pool faster than the liver can refill it.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,570px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:570px\">The bile-salt loop: about 24 g/day is secreted into the duodenum, and more than 95% returns from the ileum in portal blood. Only the small fecal loss (0.4 g/day) is replaced by new synthesis, so losing the ileum drains the pool faster than the liver can refill it. <small>· Lipid digestion &amp; absorption lecture, slide 34</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p><b>Removing the gallbladder does not cause fat malabsorption.</b> The gallbladder only stores and concentrates bile. Afterward the liver secretes bile continuously, and it still recirculates.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-af1bd3ef5f.jpg\" alt=\"Bile runs from the liver down the common bile duct to the duodenum, with the gallbladder only a side reservoir. Bile salts come back from the terminal ileum through the hepatic portal vein (red line), so the loop works with or without a gallbladder, but not without the terminal ileum.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,418px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">Bile runs from the liver down the common bile duct to the duodenum, with the gallbladder only a side reservoir. Bile salts come back from the terminal ileum through the hepatic portal vein (red line), so the loop works with or without a gallbladder, but not without the terminal ileum. <small>· Lipid digestion &amp; absorption lecture, slide 33</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Worked example: no weight loss after gallbladder removal</h4>\n<p>Fat absorption continues because bile <b>continuously recirculates without loss</b>. Bile is essential for fat absorption, fat is not absorbed in the colon, vitamins A, D, E and K have no active-transport backup, and lingual lipase does not increase.</p>\n<h4>Bacterial overgrowth</h4>\n<p>Conjugation keeps bile salts ionized and trapped in the lumen. Overgrown bacteria in the small bowel <b>deconjugate</b> them back into bile acids. At intestinal pH, bile acids are mostly un-ionized and lipid-soluble, so they diffuse into cells <b>too early</b>, in the upper intestine. Micelles then fail before fat absorption is finished.</p>","s":"Steatorrhea: when a step fails","sn":3,"pn":"3.2"},{"l":"PHYS-C8","h":"Celiac disease, tropical sprue, and the causes compared","p":"<h4>Lost surface: celiac disease and tropical sprue</h4>\n<p>Fat enters cells by diffusion, and diffusion depends on surface area. Both diseases <b>destroy the villi</b> of the small bowel: no villi, no absorption.</p>\n<p><b>Celiac disease</b>: immune reaction to gluten (its gliadin protein) in wheat, rye, barley and oats (oats mostly through wheat contamination). Tissue transglutaminase chemically modifies gliadin peptides; antigen-presenting cells show them to CD4 (helper) T cells, which inflame the mucosa. Villi are stripped flat from duodenum to terminal ileum → fat malabsorption, iron-deficiency anemia, bloating, gas, fatigue, weight loss; ↑ bowel-wall lymphoma. Blood tests: <b>anti-endomysial</b> and <b>anti-tissue transglutaminase</b> antibodies; HLA-DQ2/DQ8 (human leukocyte antigen) typing can rule it out. <b>Biopsy is most accurate</b>: blunted villi, intraepithelial lymphocytes, crypt hyperplasia. Treatment: stop gluten; it resolves in a few weeks. Steroids help but are not a real solution.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-b77a382427.jpg\" alt=\"Small-bowel biopsies. Normal mucosa has tall, finger-like villi (left); in celiac disease the villi are flattened and the crypts below them are deep (right), so the absorptive surface is gone.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,684px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:684px\">Small-bowel biopsies. Normal mucosa has tall, finger-like villi (left); in celiac disease the villi are flattened and the crypts below them are deep (right), so the absorptive surface is gone. <small>· Lipid digestion &amp; absorption lecture, slide 39</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p><b>Tropical sprue</b>: chronic infection of the small intestine (bacterial, viral, amebic or parasitic) → fewer epithelial cells and villi → fat malabsorption, and especially folate and B₁₂ malabsorption. Found on <b>biopsy</b>; treated with <b>antibiotics</b>. Workup for any malabsorption: bone density, complete blood count, folate, B₁₂ and vitamin D levels.</p>\n<h4>Worked example: flat villi in a young man</h4>\n<p>A 23-year-old has a year of low-volume, oily, foul diarrhea; biopsy shows absent villi, many intraepithelial lymphocytes and hyperplastic crypts. This is celiac disease, so the useful therapy is a <b>gluten-free diet</b>. Antibiotics treat tropical sprue, steroids are no lasting fix, and vagotomy or duodenal resection has no role.</p>\n<h4>Worked example: diagnosing tropical sprue</h4>\n<p>The most specific test is <b>biopsy</b>. A fecal fat stain (Sudan black) proves steatorrhea of any cause. Hypocalcemia, low bone density and a prolonged prothrombin time reflect vitamin D and K loss, seen in any fat malabsorption.</p>\n<h4>Worked example: tropical sprue and low calcium</h4>\n<p>Low serum calcium in tropical sprue comes from impaired absorption of <b>lipids and fat-soluble vitamins</b>: less vitamin D means less calcium (Ca²⁺) absorption. The organisms do not consume calcium or make calcium-pump toxins, and the lymphoma risk belongs to celiac disease.</p>\n<h4>The causes side by side</h4>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead><tr><th>Cause</th><th>Step that fails</th><th>Clue</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>Pancreatic insufficiency; orlistat</td><td>no lipase</td><td>chronic pancreatitis, cystic fibrosis</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Zollinger–Ellison syndrome</td><td>lipase inactivated (pH &lt; 5)</td><td>ulcers, ↑ gastrin</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Bile obstruction; ileal resection</td><td>too few bile salts</td><td>↑ bile acid synthesis; B₁₂ loss</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Bacterial overgrowth</td><td>bile salts deconjugated</td><td>bile acids absorbed early</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Celiac disease</td><td>villi flattened</td><td>gluten; antibodies; biopsy</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Tropical sprue</td><td>villi lost</td><td>infection; antibiotics</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Abetalipoproteinemia</td><td>no chylomicrons</td><td>no apoB; fat inside enterocytes</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>","s":"Steatorrhea: when a step fails","sn":3,"pn":"3.3"},{"l":"PHYS-C8","h":"No fat absorption, no ADEK absorption","p":"<p>Vitamins <b>A, D, E and K</b> (ADEK) are absorbed exactly like fat: into micelles, by diffusion into the cell, into chylomicrons, and out through lymph. So <b>no fat absorption, no ADEK absorption</b>. Celiac disease, tropical sprue, bile insufficiency, ileal resection or Crohn disease, duodenal acid (Zollinger–Ellison) and lipase loss all lower ADEK absorption. Water-soluble vitamins, B₁₂ included, are absorbed differently (Chapter 9). Pancreatic insufficiency lowers B₁₂ absorption too, by another route: pancreatic proteases must free B₁₂ from R-protein before intrinsic factor can bind it (Chapter 9).</p>\n<p>Deficiency signs follow each vitamin's job. <b>A</b>: night blindness, dry eyes and skin. <b>D</b>: ↓ Ca²⁺ absorption → hypocalcemia, low bone density (rickets in children, osteomalacia in adults). <b>E</b>: hemolytic anemia, nerve damage. <b>K</b>: ↓ clotting factors II, VII, IX, X → prolonged prothrombin time (PT) and bleeding. Liver disease with poor bile flow also lowers vitamin D absorption (vitamin D activation: Chapter 9).</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-a7d5771b1b.jpg\" alt=\"Vitamin D from skin or diet is hydroxylated in the liver, then the kidney, to 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D₃, which makes the calcium carrier calbindin in intestinal cells. When dietary vitamin D is lost with the fat, calcium absorption falls.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,418px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">Vitamin D from skin or diet is hydroxylated in the liver, then the kidney, to 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D₃, which makes the calcium carrier calbindin in intestinal cells. When dietary vitamin D is lost with the fat, calcium absorption falls. <small>· Discussion session (digestion &amp; absorption), 2026, slide 9</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Worked example: which vitamin is not fat-soluble?</h4>\n<p><b>Vitamin B₁₂</b>. It is water-soluble and absorbed in the ileum with intrinsic factor, while A, D, E and K travel with fat.</p>\n<h4>Do not confuse</h4>\n<p><b>Micelle vs chylomicron</b>: micelles (≈ 50 Å, bile-salt monolayer) work in the lumen and never enter the cell; chylomicrons (≈ 1,000 Å, phospholipid and apoprotein coat) are built inside the cell and leave by lymph. <b>Emulsion droplets</b> (bile salts coating fat, before lipase) are not micelles. <b>Secreted active</b>: lipase, cholesterol ester hydrolase. <b>Activated by trypsin</b>: procolipase, PLA₂. <b>Gallbladder removal</b> spares fat absorption; <b>ileal resection</b> does not.</p>","s":"Fat-soluble vitamins A, D, E and K","sn":4,"pn":"4.1"},{"l":"PHYS-C9","h":"The colon and the crypts","p":"<h4>Colon: Na⁺ in, K⁺ out, set by aldosterone</h4>\n<p>The colon works like the <b>principal cells</b> of the kidney's late distal tubule and collecting duct. Stool enters it watery; the colon absorbs Na⁺ and water but no nutrients. Its apical membrane has Na⁺ channels and K⁺ channels. <b>Aldosterone</b> induces synthesis of the Na⁺ channels. More Na⁺ enters, more is pumped out by the Na⁺/K⁺ ATPase, more K⁺ is pumped in, and more K⁺ leaks out to the lumen. So aldosterone raises Na⁺ absorption and, secondarily, <b>K⁺ secretion</b>. The colon also secretes HCO₃⁻.</p>\n<p>Colonic K⁺ secretion is also <b>flow-rate dependent</b>. Normally, secreted K⁺ builds up in the lumen and shrinks the gradient for more K⁺ to leave. Fast flow, as in diarrhea, flushes it away, keeps luminal K⁺ low, and lets K⁺ keep pouring out. That is why diarrhea causes <b>hypokalemia</b> (low blood K⁺).</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-b816944a0c.jpg\" alt=\"Left, the colon cell: aldosterone adds apical Na⁺ channels, and K⁺ leaves through apical K⁺ channels, faster at high flow (hence hypokalemia). Right, the kidney&#x27;s principal cell, which aldosterone runs the same way.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,760px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:760px\">Left, the colon cell: aldosterone adds apical Na⁺ channels, and K⁺ leaves through apical K⁺ channels, faster at high flow (hence hypokalemia). Right, the kidney's principal cell, which aldosterone runs the same way. <small>· Discussion session (digestion &amp; absorption), 2026, slide 42</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Crypts: Cl⁻ secretion through CFTR</h4>\n<p>Crypt cells secrete. Their basolateral membrane has a <b>Na⁺–K⁺–2Cl⁻ cotransporter</b>, like the one in the thick ascending limb of the loop of Henle. It uses the Na⁺ gradient to drag Cl⁻ into the cell against its own gradient, another case of secondary active transport. Cl⁻ then exits to the lumen through apical <b>CFTR</b> Cl⁻ channels (cystic fibrosis transmembrane conductance regulator; Chapter 6). Na⁺ follows Cl⁻ between the cells, and water follows the NaCl. CFTR is normally closed. <b>VIP</b> (vasoactive intestinal peptide) and <b>ACh</b> (acetylcholine) bind basolateral receptors → Gs (stimulatory G protein) → adenylyl cyclase → <b>cAMP</b> (cyclic AMP) → CFTR opens. So VIP increases secretion.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-3726dabd40.jpg\" alt=\"Crypt cell. The basolateral Na⁺–K⁺–2Cl⁻ cotransporter loads Cl⁻; a hormone or neurotransmitter such as VIP acts on its receptor (R) → Gs → adenylyl cyclase (AC) → cAMP, which opens the apical Cl⁻ channel. Na⁺ and water follow between the cells. Circled steps 1–4 are cholera toxin locking this switch on (Diarrhea, below).\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,684px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:684px\">Crypt cell. The basolateral Na⁺–K⁺–2Cl⁻ cotransporter loads Cl⁻; a hormone or neurotransmitter such as VIP acts on its receptor (R) → Gs → adenylyl cyclase (AC) → cAMP, which opens the apical Cl⁻ channel. Na⁺ and water follow between the cells. Circled steps 1–4 are cholera toxin locking this switch on (Diarrhea, below). <small>· Costanzo, Physiology, 6th ed., Fig. 8.36</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Worked example: where does Cl⁻ enter cells by secondary active transport?</h4>\n<p>At the <b>basolateral membrane of crypt cells</b>, on the Na⁺–K⁺–2Cl⁻ cotransporter. The thick ascending limb has the same kind of carrier \"flipped\" to its luminal membrane, where furosemide blocks it. Jejunal and colonic apical membranes lack it; pancreatic acinar cells carry it basolaterally.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-3f338dc957.jpg\" alt=\"For comparison, a thick ascending limb cell: the same Na⁺–K⁺–2Cl⁻ carrier, but on the luminal side, where furosemide blocks it.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,418px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">For comparison, a thick ascending limb cell: the same Na⁺–K⁺–2Cl⁻ carrier, but on the luminal side, where furosemide blocks it. <small>· Discussion session (digestion &amp; absorption), 2026, slide 60</small></figcaption></figure>","s":"Water and salt along the gut","sn":1,"pn":"1.3"},{"l":"PHYS-C9","h":"Volume loss, acidosis and low K⁺","p":"<p>Diarrhea means the intestine fails to finish absorbing the fluid it receives.</p>\n<h4>What severe diarrhea does to the body</h4>\n<p><b>Volume</b>: stool carries extracellular-type fluid, so ECF (extracellular fluid) volume, blood volume and <b>blood pressure</b> fall. Baroreceptors and the renin–angiotensin–aldosterone system fight back but fail if the loss is too large or fast. <b>Acid–base</b>: saliva, pancreatic juice and intestinal juice are rich in HCO₃⁻, which is normally reabsorbed farther down the gut. In profuse diarrhea, fast transit carries it out in stool, and the pancreas and mucosa secrete still more. Losing base without losing acid causes <b>metabolic acidosis</b> (blood pH &lt; 7.35). As HCO₃⁻ falls, the kidney keeps Cl⁻ while saving salt and water, so blood Cl⁻ rises: <b>hyperchloremic metabolic acidosis</b>.</p>\n<p>That acidosis has a <b>normal anion gap</b>. In words, the anion gap is plasma Na⁺ minus the two main measured anions; in symbols, gap = Na⁺ − (Cl⁻ + HCO₃⁻). It estimates unmeasured anions, such as lactate. Diarrhea adds no new acid anion: each lost HCO₃⁻ is replaced by a Cl⁻, so Cl⁻ + HCO₃⁻, and the gap, stay normal. <b>K⁺</b>: flow-dependent colonic secretion, boosted by aldosterone released in volume loss, empties K⁺ into stool. The rule: <b>diarrhea = acidosis + low K⁺</b>. Vomiting, which loses gastric H⁺, causes alkalosis instead.</p>\n<h4>Worked example: laxative abuse</h4>\n<p>A 17-year-old who abuses laxatives has acidosis and high blood Cl⁻. Mechanism: <b>increased loss of HCO₃⁻ and K⁺</b> in stool. Not \"like cholera\": no toxin, no cAMP surge, smaller volume. H⁺–peptide cotransport (Chapter 7) is irrelevant; acid made to replace gastric fluid describes vomiting.</p>","s":"Diarrhea","sn":2,"pn":"2.1"},{"l":"PHYS-C9","h":"Cholera and secretory diarrhea","p":"<h4>Secretory diarrhea: cholera</h4>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-50738a9038.png\" alt=\"Cholera toxin hijacks the normal VIP/ACh switch and locks it on. Read down: one stuck G protein ends in volume loss, acidosis and hypokalemia.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,489px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">Cholera toxin hijacks the normal VIP/ACh switch and locks it on. Read down: one stuck G protein ends in volume loss, acidosis and hypokalemia. <small>· Flowchart from GI Physiology, Explained</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p>Secretory diarrhea is too much <b>secretion</b> by crypt cells, usually from bacterial toxins (<i>Vibrio cholerae</i>, some <i>Escherichia coli</i>). Cholera toxin enters the crypt cell across the apical membrane. Its A subunit moves to the basolateral membrane and <b>ADP-ribosylates</b> the αs subunit of Gs: it attaches an ADP-ribose (adenosine diphosphate–ribose) tag. This blocks the subunit's own GTPase, so its GTP (guanosine triphosphate, the \"on\" signal) is never split and Gs never switches off. <b>Adenylyl cyclase</b> stays on, cAMP stays high, and CFTR stays open. Cl⁻ pours out, Na⁺ and water follow, and secretion overwhelms the villi and colon: massive diarrhea.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-fd852562e3.jpg\" alt=\"Villi absorb; crypts (arrow), the pits between them, secrete. Cholera toxin acts on the crypt cells, and their secretion outruns what the villi and colon can absorb.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,418px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">Villi absorb; crypts (arrow), the pits between them, secrete. Cholera toxin acts on the crypt cells, and their secretion outruns what the villi and colon can absorb. <small>· Discussion session (digestion &amp; absorption), 2026, slide 21</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Worked examples: cholera</h4>\n<p>Watery diarrhea from <i>V. cholerae</i>: the toxin causes <b>adenylyl cyclase activation</b>, not inhibition, and it opens Cl⁻ channels rather than closing them. A toxin that irreversibly activates adenylyl cyclase in crypt cells causes <b>secretory diarrhea</b>. The tempting \"osmotic diuresis\" is a kidney term: extra urine driven by a filtered solute such as mannitol.</p>\n<p><b>Cystic fibrosis and cholera</b>: cystic fibrosis (CF) is loss of CFTR, the very channel cholera toxin forces open (Chapter 6). People who carry one CF allele have fewer working channels and lose less fluid in cholera, the classic explanation for why the CF allele is common.</p>","s":"Diarrhea","sn":2,"pn":"2.2"},{"l":"PHYS-C9","h":"B₁₂ deficiency: anemia, gastrectomy and ileal resection","p":"<p>Dividing erythroblasts (red cell precursors) need B₁₂ and folate to make DNA. Without them, purine and thymidine synthesis fails, many erythroblasts die in the marrow, and the survivors become oversized red cells: <b>megaloblastic anemia</b>. B₁₂ also maintains nerve myelin.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-a92e1a7f8a.jpg\" alt=\"Anemias sorted by red-cell size (MCV, mean cell volume). B₁₂ or folate deficiency gives large (macrocytic, megaloblastic) cells; iron deficiency gives small (microcytic) ones.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,684px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:684px\">Anemias sorted by red-cell size (MCV, mean cell volume). B₁₂ or folate deficiency gives large (macrocytic, megaloblastic) cells; iron deficiency gives small (microcytic) ones. <small>· Water-soluble vitamins lecture (biochemistry), slide 41</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p><b>Pernicious anemia</b> is autoimmune loss of IF: CD4 (helper) T cells attack the parietal cell's H⁺/K⁺ ATPase, or antibodies target IF itself. Atrophic gastritis also cuts IF. Removing the stomach or the terminal ileum gives the same anemia, often also called pernicious anemia; a vegan diet lacking B₁₂ causes it with normal IF.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-b3cb6cc7fe.jpg\" alt=\"Pernicious anemia. Parietal cells make intrinsic factor and acid (H⁺/K⁺ ATPase). CD4 T cells primed against the H⁺/K⁺ ATPase attack them, so no intrinsic factor binds B₁₂ and the distal ileum takes none up.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,570px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:570px\">Pernicious anemia. Parietal cells make intrinsic factor and acid (H⁺/K⁺ ATPase). CD4 T cells primed against the H⁺/K⁺ ATPase attack them, so no intrinsic factor binds B₁₂ and the distal ileum takes none up. <small>· Discussion session (digestion &amp; absorption), 2026, slide 15</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Worked example: what is B₁₂ essential for?</h4>\n<p>The <b>maturation of red blood cells</b>; deficiency gives megaloblastic anemia, with red cells larger than normal. White-cell formation and the absorption of B₆ or folic acid are not its defining job.</p>\n<h4>Gastrectomy and ileal resection</h4>\n<p>After <b>gastrectomy</b>: no parietal cells → no IF → no B₁₂ absorption → eventual B₁₂ deficiency. Give B₁₂ by <b>injection</b>; oral B₁₂ in ordinary doses is not absorbed without IF. Gastrectomy can also cause <b>dumping syndrome</b>: food rushes into the small intestine, causing cramping and diarrhea. After <b>ileal resection</b>, IF is present but the uptake site is gone, so B₁₂ must again be injected; bile salts are lost too, causing steatorrhea (worked example in Chapter 6).</p>\n<h4>Worked example: total gastrectomy for cancer</h4>\n<p>A 60-year-old has his whole stomach removed. Expect <b>pernicious anemia</b> (B₁₂ deficiency). Not steatorrhea: pancreatic lipase and bile still digest fat. Not acute pancreatitis. Not fecal carbohydrate loss: pancreatic amylase and the brush-border enzymes still work.</p>","s":"Vitamins, calcium and iron","sn":3,"pn":"3.2"},{"l":"PHYS-C9","h":"Calcium and iron","p":"<p><b>Calcium</b> absorption needs active vitamin D, <b>1,25-dihydroxycholecalciferol</b> (1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D₃). Sunlight converts 7-dehydrocholesterol in skin to cholecalciferol (vitamin D₃); diet supplies D₃ (fish, meat) and D₂ (supplements); all inactive. The <b>liver</b> hydroxylates it to 25-hydroxycholecalciferol, still inactive but the main circulating form. The <b>kidney</b> proximal tubule adds the second hydroxyl with <b>1α-hydroxylase</b>, making the active hormone. In the gut it induces <b>calbindin</b> (calbindin D-28K), a Ca²⁺-binding protein in intestinal cells, so more Ca²⁺ is absorbed. Deficiency, or failed activation (chronic renal failure), means poor Ca²⁺ absorption: <b>rickets</b> in children, <b>osteomalacia</b> in adults. Fat malabsorption also lowers vitamin D absorption (Chapter 8).</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-612ea5995c.jpg\" alt=\"Rickets: a child&#x27;s X-ray with soft, bowed leg bones, from too little vitamin D and so too little Ca²⁺ absorbed.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,319px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">Rickets: a child's X-ray with soft, bowed leg bones, from too little vitamin D and so too little Ca²⁺ absorbed. <small>· Fat-soluble vitamins lecture (biochemistry), slide 25</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Worked example: hepatitis A</h4>\n<p>A 10-year-old has jaundice from hepatitis A (a fecal–oral virus). Expect <b>hypocalcemia</b>. The inflamed liver hydroxylates less vitamin D, and <b>cholestasis</b> (reduced or stopped bile flow) leaves less bile to absorb this fat-soluble vitamin. Less active vitamin D → less calbindin → less Ca²⁺ absorbed; vitamin D deficiency is also common in chronic hepatitis B and C. Hyperkalemia (impaired hepatic K⁺ uptake), hyponatremia and failing glucose output belong to advanced cirrhosis; protein absorption does not need the liver.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-d084fa7dbe.jpg\" alt=\"Vitamin D needs two hydroxylations, first in the liver, then in the kidney, before it can induce calbindin in intestinal cells.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,456px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">Vitamin D needs two hydroxylations, first in the liver, then in the kidney, before it can induce calbindin in intestinal cells. <small>· Discussion session (digestion &amp; absorption), 2026, slide 9</small></figcaption></figure>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-27055f664f.jpg\" alt=\"Nonheme iron (left) is reduced to Fe²⁺ before entry; heme iron (right) enters whole. Both leave the cell as Fe²⁺ and ride transferrin as Fe³⁺.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,483px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">Nonheme iron (left) is reduced to Fe²⁺ before entry; heme iron (right) enters whole. Both leave the cell as Fe²⁺ and ride transferrin as Fe³⁺. <small>· Discussion session (digestion &amp; absorption), 2026, slide 56</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p><b>Iron</b> is eaten mostly as ferric <b>Fe³⁺</b> but absorbed as ferrous <b>Fe²⁺</b>, in the duodenum. On the brush border, Dcytb (duodenal cytochrome b) reduces Fe³⁺ to Fe²⁺, and DMT1 (divalent metal transporter 1) carries it in. <b>Heme iron</b> (hemoglobin and myoglobin in meat) is easier to absorb: whole heme enters (via HCP1, heme carrier protein 1), and heme oxygenase frees Fe²⁺ inside the cell. Inside, Fe²⁺ binds <b>apoferritin</b> (forming ferritin, the storage form) or exits through ferroportin. Hephaestin re-oxidizes it to Fe³⁺ for <b>transferrin</b>, a β-globulin that carries iron to liver stores, then to bone marrow for hemoglobin. Duodenal damage, as in celiac disease (Chapter 8), causes iron-deficiency anemia.</p>\n<p>Stomach acid helps keep dietary iron soluble and in the ferrous Fe²⁺ form, so removing the stomach also cuts iron absorption: after gastrectomy, iron-deficiency anemia can join B₁₂ deficiency.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-6927c6af8a.jpg\" alt=\"Whole-body iron. Only 1–2 mg a day is absorbed and lost; plasma transferrin (Tf) holds just 3–4 mg, most of it headed for the marrow&#x27;s new red cells, and the liver stores the excess as ferritin.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,494px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">Whole-body iron. Only 1–2 mg a day is absorbed and lost; plasma transferrin (Tf) holds just 3–4 mg, most of it headed for the marrow's new red cells, and the liver stores the excess as ferritin. <small>· Iron metabolism lecture (biochemistry), slide 6</small></figcaption></figure>","s":"Vitamins, calcium and iron","sn":3,"pn":"3.3"},{"l":"PHYS-C9","h":"Jaundice: which bilirubin rises","p":"<p><b>Jaundice</b> is yellow skin and sclera from excess bilirubin; ask which kind. Too much made (<b>hemolytic</b>, prehepatic jaundice) → <b>unconjugated</b> bilirubin rises, because conjugation cannot keep up. Example: hemolytic disease of the newborn, where maternal antibodies against fetal ABO or Rh antigens cross the placenta and destroy fetal red cells. <b>Physiologic newborn jaundice</b> (day 2 or 3) is also unconjugated: UDP-glucuronyl transferase is made slowly after birth.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-79ee8efea8.jpg\" alt=\"Scleral icterus: the whites of the eyes turn yellow, often the first visible sign of excess bilirubin.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,418px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">Scleral icterus: the whites of the eyes turn yellow, often the first visible sign of excess bilirubin. <small>· Heme &amp; bilirubin lecture (biochemistry), slide 38</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p>Unconjugated bilirubin stays bound to albumin, so the kidney cannot filter it: hemolytic jaundice puts no bilirubin in the urine (only urine urobilinogen rises). Chronic hemolysis, as in sickle cell disease, also pours extra bilirubin into bile, where it can precipitate with calcium as pigment gallstones.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-b929ce0932.jpg\" alt=\"Top (A): hemolysis makes more bilirubin (B) than the liver can conjugate, so unconjugated bilirubin rises. Bottom (B): in the newborn, too little UDP-glucuronosyl transferase does the same. CB, conjugated bilirubin; U, urobilinogen; S, stercobilin.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,286px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">Top (A): hemolysis makes more bilirubin (B) than the liver can conjugate, so unconjugated bilirubin rises. Bottom (B): in the newborn, too little UDP-glucuronosyl transferase does the same. CB, conjugated bilirubin; U, urobilinogen; S, stercobilin. <small>· Heme &amp; bilirubin lecture (biochemistry), slide 44</small></figcaption></figure>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-3833fdf05a.jpg\" alt=\"Newborn jaundice: yellow skin (right) beside a healthy baby (left).\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,342px)\"><figcaption style=\"max-width:560px\">Newborn jaundice: yellow skin (right) beside a healthy baby (left). <small>· Heme &amp; bilirubin lecture (biochemistry), slide 44</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p>Blocked outflow (<b>bile duct obstruction</b>: gallstones, tumors) or liver disease (hepatitis) → <b>conjugated</b> bilirubin cannot enter bile and backs up into blood. Being water-soluble, it darkens the urine; with little stercobilin, the stool turns pale (\"clay-colored\").</p>\n<h4>Worked example: jaundice with conjugated bilirubin</h4>\n<p>The cause is <b>common bile duct obstruction</b>: the liver conjugates normally but cannot excrete. Hemolytic and physiologic jaundice raise unconjugated bilirubin, so \"all of the above\" is wrong.</p>","s":"The liver","sn":4,"pn":"4.2"}]};